nt(((r, theta)))\n```\n```output\n(3.0, 1.5707963267948966)\n```\nThe polar coordinates for the point $(0,3)$ are \\(\\left(3, \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)\\).\n\nSo the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\left(3, \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)}\n\\]", "To convert the rectangular coordinates $(0, 3)$ to polar coordinates $(r, \\theta)$, we need to use the following formulas:\n\n1. The distance $r$ from the origin is given by:\n\\[ r = \\sqrt{x^2 + y^2} \\]\n\n2. The angle $\\theta$ is given by:\n\\[ \\theta = \\begin{cases} \n\\arctan\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right) & \\text{if } x > 0 \\\\\n\\arctan\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right) + \\pi & \\text{if } x < 0 \\text{ and } y \\ge 0 \\\\\n\\arctan\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right) - \\pi & \\text{if } x < 0 \\text{ and } y < 0 \\\\\n\\frac{\\pi}{2} & \\text{if } x = 0 \\text{ and } y > 0 \\\\\n-\\frac{\\pi}{2} & \\text{if } x = 0 \\text{ and } y < 0 \\\\\n\\text{undefined} & \\text{if } x = 0 \\text{ and } y = 0\n\\end{cases} \\]\n\nFor the point $(0, 3)$:\n- $x = 0$\n- $y = 3$\n\nUsing the formula for $r$:\n\\[ r = \\sqrt{0^2 + 3^2} = \\sqrt{9} = 3 \\]\n\nUsing the formula for $\\theta$:\nSince $x = 0$ and $y > 0$, we have:\n\\[ \\theta = \\frac{\\pi}{2} \\]\n\nSo the polar coordinates are $(3, \\frac{\\pi}{2})$.\n\nLet's verify this using Python code.\n```python\nimport math\n\n# Rectangular coordinates\nx = 0\ny = 3\n\n# Calculate r\nr = math.sqrt(x**2 + y**2)\n\n# Calculate theta\nif x == 0:\n    if y > 0:\n        theta = math.pi / 2\n    elif y < 0:\n        theta = -math.pi / 2\n    else:\n        theta = None  # This should not happen for our point (0, 3)\nelse:\n    theta = math.atan2(y, x)\n\nprint(((r, theta)))\n```\n```output\n(3.0, 1.5707963267948966)\n```\nThe polar coordinates for the point $(0, 3)$ are $(3, \\frac{\\pi}{2})$.\n\nSo the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{(3, \\frac{\\pi}{2})}\n\\]", "To convert the rectangular coordinates $(0, 3)$ to polar coordinates $(r, \\theta)$, we need to use the following formulas:\n\n1. $r = \\sqr