theta$ is $\\frac{\\pi}{2}$ radians (or 90 degrees).\n\nSo the polar coordinates of the point $(0,3)$ are $\\left(3, \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$.\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\nimport math\n\n# Rectangular coordinates\nx = 0\ny = 3\n\n# Calculate the radius r\nr = math.sqrt(x**2 + y**2)\n\n# Calculate the angle theta\ntheta = math.atan2(y, x)\n\n# Convert theta to the range [0, 2*pi)\nif theta < 0:\n    theta += 2 * math.pi\n\nprint(((r, theta)))\n```\n```output\n(3.0, 1.5707963267948966)\n```\nThe polar coordinates of the point $(0,3)$ are $\\left(3, \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$. \n\nSo the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\left(3, \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)}\n\\]", 'The point $(2 \\sqrt{3}, 6)$ in Cartesian coordinates can be expressed as $(r, \\theta)$ in polar coordinates, where $r$ is a positive real number and $0 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\frac{\\pi}{2}.$  Enter the ordered pair $(r, \\theta).$  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  