he angle $\\theta$ is given by:\n\\[ \\theta = \\begin{cases} \n\\arctan\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right) & \\text{if } x > 0 \\\\\n\\arctan\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right) + \\pi & \\text{if } x < 0 \\text{ and } y \\ge 0 \\\\\n\\arctan\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right) - \\pi & \\text{if } x < 0 \\text{ and } y < 0 \\\\\n\\frac{\\pi}{2} & \\text{if } x = 0 \\text{ and } y > 0 \\\\\n-\\frac{\\pi}{2} & \\text{if } x = 0 \\text{ and } y < 0 \\\\\n\\text{undefined} & \\text{if } x = 0 \\text{ and } y = 0 \n\\end{cases} \\]\n\nFor the point $(0, 3)$:\n\\[ r = \\sqrt{0^2 + 3^2} = \\sqrt{9} = 3 \\]\nSince $x = 0$ and $y > 0$, the angle $\\theta$ is:\n\\[ \\theta = \\frac{\\pi}{2} \\]\n\nSo the polar coordinates are $(3, \\frac{\\pi}{2})$. Let's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\nimport math\n\n# Rectangular coordinates\nx = 0\ny = 3\n\n# Calculate the radius r\nr = math.sqrt(x**2 + y**2)\n\n# Calculate the angle theta\nif x == 0 and y > 0:\n    theta = math.pi / 2\nelif x == 0 and y < 0:\n    theta = -math.pi / 2\nelif x > 0:\n    theta = math.atan(y / x)\nelif x < 0 and y >= 0:\n    theta = math.atan(y / x) + math.pi\nelif x < 0 and y < 0:\n    theta = math.atan(y / x) - math.pi\n\n# Ensure theta is in the range [0, 2*pi)\nif theta < 0:\n    theta += 2 * math.pi\n\nprint(((r, theta)))\n```\n```output\n(3.0, 1.5707963267948966)\n```\nThe polar coordinates for the point $(0, 3)$ are $(3, \\frac{\\pi}{2})$. Therefore, the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{(3, \\frac{\\pi}{2})}\n\\]", 'In polar coordinates, identify the curve defined by the equation $r = 2.$  (A) Line (B) Circle (C) Parabola (D) Ellipse (E) Hyperbola \\boxed{}. \\text{Please provide a detailed explanation for your answer. \\text{Please provide a detailed explanation for your answer. \\text{Please provide a detailed explanation for your answer. \\text{Please provide a detailed explanation for your answer. \\text{Please provide a detailed explanation for your answer. \\text{Please provide a detailed explanation for your