ent with the greatest average speed is Carla, with an average speed of approximately 1.22 units per time unit.\n\nSo, the answer is \\(\\boxed{\\text{Carla}}\\).', 'To determine which student has the greatest average speed, we need to calculate the speed for each student. The speed is given by the slope of the line connecting the origin to the point representing each student\'s progress in the graph. The slope of the line is the distance traveled divided by the time taken.\n\nFrom the graph:\n- Evelyn\'s point is (1.25, 4.5), so her speed is \\( \\frac{4.5}{1.25} \\).\n- Briana\'s point is (2.5, 2.2), so her speed is \\( \\frac{2.2}{2.5} \\).\n- Carla\'s point is (4.25, 5.2), so her speed is \\( \\frac{5.2}{4.25} \\).\n- Debra\'s point is (5.6, 2.8), so her speed is \\( \\frac{2.8}{5.6} \\).\n- Angela\'s point is (6.8, 1.4), so her speed is \\( \\frac{1.4}{6.8} \\).\n\nLet\'s calculate these speeds using Python.\n```python\n# Define the distances and times for each student\ndistances = [4.5, 2.2, 5.2, 2.8, 1.4]\ntimes = [1.25, 2.5, 4.25, 5.6, 6.8]\n\n# Calculate the speeds\nspeeds = [distance / time for distance, time in zip(distances, times)]\n\n# Find the maximum speed and the corresponding student\nmax_speed = max(speeds)\nmax_speed_index = speeds.index(max_speed)\nstudents = ["Evelyn", "Briana", "Carla", "Debra", "Angela"]\nfastest_student = students[max_speed_index]\n\nprint((max_speed, fastest_student))\n```\n```output\n(3.6, \'Evelyn\')\n```\nThe greatest average speed is \\(3.6\\) units per time unit, and the student with this speed is Evelyn.\n\nSo, the student with the greatest average speed is \\(\\boxed{\\text{Evelyn}}\\).', 'To determine which student has the greatest average speed, we need to calculate the speed for each student using the graph provided. The average speed is given by the formula:\n\n\\[ \\text{Average Speed} = \\frac{\\text{Total Distance}}{\\text{Total Time}} \\]\n\nFrom the graph, we can observe the following:\n\n- Evelyn: She covers