Setting \\(2m + 1 = 8\\), we get \\(2m = 7\\), which does not give an integer value for \\(m\\). The next smallest perfect cube is 27, which is \\(3^3\\). Setting \\(2m + 1 = 27\\), we get \\(2m = 26\\), giving \\(m = 13\\). Therefore, the two consecutive integers are 13 and 14, and their sum is \\(13 + 14 = 27\\), which is a perfect cube.\n\nLet's verify these solutions using Python code to ensure accuracy.\n```python\n# Function to find the smallest positive perfect cube that can be written as the sum of three consecutive integers\ndef smallest_cube_sum_three_consecutive():\n    n = 1\n    while True:\n        cube = n ** 3\n        if cube % 3 == 0:\n            return cube\n        n += 1\n\n# Function to find the smallest positive perfect cube that can be written as the sum of two consecutive integers\ndef smallest_cube_sum_two_consecutive():\n    n = 1\n    while True:\n        cube = n ** 3\n        if (cube - 1) % 2 == 0:\n            return cube\n        n += 1\n\n# Finding the results\nsmallest_cube_three_consecutive = smallest_cube_sum_three_consecutive()\nsmallest_cube_two_consecutive = smallest_cube_sum_two_consecutive()\n\nprint((smallest_cube_three_consecutive, smallest_cube_two_consecutive))\n```\n```output\n(27, 1)\n```\nThe smallest positive perfect cube that can be written as the sum of three consecutive integers is \\(27\\). The smallest positive perfect cube that can be written as the sum of two consecutive integers is \\(1\\).\n\nThus, the final answers are:\n\\[\n\\boxed{27} \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\boxed{1}\n\\]", "What is the smallest positive perfect square that can be written as the sum of two consecutive integers? \\boxed{} \\end{align*}\nThe sum of three consecutive integers can be written as:\n\\[n + (n+1) + (n+2) = 3n + 3 = 3(n+1)\\]\nFor this to be a perfect cube, \\(3(n+1)\\) must be a perfect cube. The smallest positive perfect cube is \\(1^3 = 1\\), but \\(3(n+1) = 1\\) is not possible because \\(n\\) would not be an integer. Th