 = -4, 3\n\n# Calculate the differences\ndx = x2 - x1\ndy = y2 - y1\n\n# Calculate the distance using the distance formula\ndistance = math.sqrt(dx**2 + dy**2)\nprint(distance)\n```\n```output\n10.816653826391969\n```\nThe distance between the points \\((2, -6)\\) and \\((-4, 3)\\) is approximately \\(10.82\\) units. To express this in simplest radical form, we need to check if this value can be simplified.\n\nFirst, let's recompute the distance calculation step-by-step manually:\n\n\\[\nd = \\sqrt{((-4) - 2)^2 + (3 - (-6))^2}\n\\]\n\\[\nd = \\sqrt{(-6)^2 + (3 + 6)^2}\n\\]\n\\[\nd = \\sqrt{36 + 81}\n\\]\n\\[\nd = \\sqrt{117}\n\\]\n\nWe can simplify \\(\\sqrt{117}\\) by factoring 117 into its prime factors:\n\n\\[\n117 = 3^2 \\times 13\n\\]\n\nThus,\n\n\\[\n\\sqrt{117} = \\sqrt{3^2 \\times 13} = 3\\sqrt{13}\n\\]\n\nSo the distance in simplest radical form is \\(3\\sqrt{13}\\).\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{3\\sqrt{13}}\n\\]", "The distance between two points $(x_1, y_1)$ and $(x_2, y_2)$ in a coordinate plane is given by the formula:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Distance} = \\sqrt{(x_2 - x_1)^2 + (y_2 - y_1)^2}\n\\]\n\nGiven the points $(2, -6)$ and $(-4, 3)$, we can substitute these values into the formula:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Distance} = \\sqrt{((-4) - 2)^2 + (3 - (-6))^2}\n\\]\n\nLet's calculate this step by step using Python.\n```python\nimport math\n\n# Coordinates of the points\nx1, y1 = 2, -6\nx2, y2 = -4, 3\n\n# Calculate the distance\ndistance = math.sqrt((x2 - x1)**2 + (y2 - y1)**2)\nprint(distance)\n```\n```output\n10.816653826391969\n```\nThe distance between the points $(2, -6)$ and $(-4, 3)$ is approximately $10.82$ units. To express the answer in simplest radical form, we need to simplify the expression under the square root.\n\nWe have:\n\\[\n\\text{Distance} = \\sqrt{((-4) - 2)^2 + (3 - (-6))^2} = \\sqrt{(-6)^2 + (9)^2} = \\sqrt{36 + 81} = \\sqrt{117} = \\sqrt{9 \\cdot 13} = 3\\sqrt{13}\n\\]\n\nSo, the exact distance in simplest radical form is:\n\\[\n\