on\nsolutions = sp.solve(equation, x)\n\n# Verify the solutions\nvalid_solutions = [sol for sol in solutions if equation.subs(x, sol) == 0]\nprint(valid_solutions)\n```", "To solve the equation \\( x = \\sqrt{11 - 2x} + 4 \\), we can follow these steps:\n\n1. Isolate the square root term on one side of the equation.\n2. Square both sides to eliminate the square root.\n3. Solve the resulting equation.\n4. Verify the solutions by substituting them back into the original equation to ensure they satisfy it.\n\nLet's start by isolating the square root term:\n\n\\[ x - 4 = \\sqrt{11 - 2x} \\]\n\nNext, we square both sides:\n\n\\[ (x - 4)^2 = 11 - 2x \\]\n\nExpanding the left side gives:\n\n\\[ x^2 - 8x + 16 = 11 - 2x \\]\n\nRearranging all terms to one side of the equation yields:\n\n\\[ x^2 - 6x + 5 = 0 \\]\n\nThis is a quadratic equation, which we can solve using the quadratic formula \\( x = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a} \\), where \\( a = 1 \\), \\( b = -6 \\), and \\( c = 5 \\). Alternatively, we can factor the equation:\n\n\\[ (x - 5)(x - 1) = 0 \\]\n\nSo the solutions are \\( x = 5 \\) and \\( x = 1 \\). We need to verify these solutions by substituting them back into the original equation.\n\nFor \\( x = 5 \\):\n\n\\[ 5 = \\sqrt{11 - 2(5)} + 4 \\]\n\\[ 5 = \\sqrt{11 - 10} + 4 \\]\n\\[ 5 = \\sqrt{1} + 4 \\]\n\\[ 5 = 1 + 4 \\]\n\\[ 5 = 5 \\]\n\nThis is true, so \\( x = 5 \\) is a valid solution.\n\nFor \\( x = 1 \\):\n\n\\[ 1 = \\sqrt{11 - 2(1)} + 4 \\]\n\\[ 1 = \\sqrt{11 - 2} + 4 \\]\n\\[ 1 = \\sqrt{9} + 4 \\]\n\\[ 1 = 3 + 4 \\]\n\\[ 1 = 7 \\]\n\nThis is false, so \\( x = 1 \\) is not a valid solution.\n\nTherefore, the only valid solution is \\( x = 5 \\).\n\nThe final answer is:\n\n\\[ \\boxed{5} \\]\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the variable and the equation\nx = sp.symbols('x')\nequation = x - sp.sqrt(11 - 2*x) - 4\n\n# Solve the equation\nsolutions = sp.solve(equation, x)\n\n# Verify the solutions\nv