ng (6, 8) and (7, 4) is:\n\\[\n\\left( \\frac{6 + 7}{2}, \\frac{8 + 4}{2} \\right) = \\left( \\frac{13}{2}, 6 \\right)\n\\]\n\nSince these midpoints are equal, we can set up the following equations:\n\\[\n\\frac{5 + x}{2} = \\frac{13}{2}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\frac{3 + y}{2} = 6\n\\]\n\nSolving these equations will give us the coordinates of the fourth vertex (x, y).\n\nLet's solve these equations step-by-step using Python.\n```python\nfrom sympy import symbols, Eq, solve\n\n# Define the variables\nx, y = symbols('x y')\n\n# Define the equations based on the midpoints\neq1 = Eq((5 + x) / 2, 13 / 2)\neq2 = Eq((3 + y) / 2, 6)\n\n# Solve the equations\nsolution = solve((eq1, eq2), (x, y))\nprint(solution)\n```\n```output\n{x: 8.00000000000000, y: 9.00000000000000}\n```\nThe coordinates of the fourth vertex are (8, 9). Therefore, the value of \\(x + y\\) is \\(8 + 9 = 17\\).\n\nSo, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{17}\n\\]", "To find the value of \\(x + y\\) for the fourth vertex of the parallelogram, we can use the properties of parallelograms. In a parallelogram, the diagonals bisect each other, meaning the midpoint of one diagonal is equal to the midpoint of the other diagonal.\n\nGiven the vertices \\((5, 3)\\), \\((6, 8)\\), \\((7, 4)\\), and \\((x, y)\\), we can use the midpoint formula to find the coordinates of the fourth vertex. The midpoint of the diagonal connecting \\((5, 3)\\) and \\((x, y)\\) should be the same as the midpoint of the diagonal connecting \\((6, 8)\\) and \\((7, 4)\\).\n\nThe midpoint of the diagonal connecting \\((6, 8)\\) and \\((7, 4)\\) is:\n\\[\n\\left( \\frac{6 + 7}{2}, \\frac{8 + 4}{2} \\right) = \\left( \\frac{13}{2}, 6 \\right)\n\\]\n\nThis midpoint should also be the midpoint of the diagonal connecting \\((5, 3)\\) and \\((x, y)\\):\n\\[\n\\left( \\frac{5 + x}{2}, \\frac{3 + y}{2} \\right) = \\left( \\frac{13}{2}, 6 \\right)\n\\]\n\nFrom this, we can set up the following equations:\n\\[\n\\frac{5 + x}{2} = \\frac{13}{2}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\frac{3 + y}{2} = 6\n\\]\n\nSolving these equations, we get:\n\\[\n5 + x = 13 \\implies x = 8\n\\]\n\\[\n3 + y = 12 \\implies y = 9\n\\]\n\nSo the coordinates of the fourth vertex are \\((8, 9)\\). Therefore, the value of \\(x + y\\) is \\(8 + 9 = 17\\).\n\nLet's verify this with Python code to ensure the result is accurate.\n```python\n# Given vertices of the parallelogram\r\nx1, y1 = 5, 3\r\nx2, y2 = 6, 8\r\nx3, y3 = 7, 4\r\n\r\n# Midpoint of the diagonal connecting (6, 8) and (7, 4)\r\nmidpoint_x = (x2 + x3) / 2\r\nmidpoint_y = (y2 + y3) / 2\r\n\r\n# Using the midpoint to find the fourth vertex (x, y)\r\nx = 2 * midpoint_x - x1\r\ny = 2 * midpoint_y - y1\r\n\r\n# Calculate x + y\r\nresult = x + y\r\nprint((x, y, result))\n```\n```output\n(8.0, 9.0, 17.0)\n```\nThe coordinates of the fourth vertex are \\((8, 9)\\), and the value of \\(x + y\\) is \\(\\boxed{17}\\).", "Given the coordinates of the parallelogram: (5, 3), (6, 8), (7, 4), and \\((x, y)\\) with \\(x > 7\\), we can use the properties of a parallelogram to find the coordinates of the fourth vertex.\n\nIn a parallelogram, the diagonals bisect each other. This means the midpoint of the diagonal connecting (5, 3) and (x, y) should be the same as the midpoint of the diagonal connecting (6, 8) and (7, 4).\n\nThe midpoint of the diagonal connecting (5, 3) and (x, y) is:\n\\[\n\\left( \\frac{5 + x}{2}, \\frac{3 + y}{2} \\right)\n\\]\nThe midpoint of the diagonal connecting (6, 8) and (7, 4) is:\n\\[\n\\left( \\frac{6 + 7}{2}, \\frac{8 + 4}{2} \\right) = \\left( \\frac{13}{2}, 6 \\right)\n\\]\nSince these midpoints are the same, we can set up the following equations:\n\\[\n\\frac{5 + x}{2} = \\frac{13}{2} \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\frac{3 + y}{2} = 6\n\\]\nSolving these equations, we get:\n\\[\n5 + x = 13 \\implies x = 8\n\\]\n\\[\n3 + y = 12 \\implies y = 9\n\\]\nSince \\(x > 7\\), the coordinates \\((8, 9)\\) satisfy this condition. Therefore, the value of \\(x + y\\) is:\n\\[\nx + y = 8 + 9 = 17\n\\]\n\nLet's confi