_after > 200:\n            return total_before\n        k += 1\n\nmin_coins = find_min_coins()\nprint(min_coins)\n```\n```output\n189\n```\nThe smallest number of coins you could have had before finding the bag of 53 coins is \\(\\boxed{189}\\).", "To solve this problem, let's denote the number of gold coins in each of the original seven bags as \\( x \\). Therefore, the total number of coins before finding the bag of 53 coins is \\( 7x \\). After finding the bag of 53 coins, the total number of coins becomes \\( 7x + 53 \\). We need to redistribute these coins into eight bags such that each bag has the same number of coins. Let's denote the number of coins in each of the eight bags after redistribution as \\( y \\). Therefore, the total number of coins after redistribution is \\( 8y \\), and we have the equation:\n\\[ 7x + 53 = 8y \\]\nWe are also given that the total number of coins after redistribution is more than 200, so:\n\\[ 8y > 200 \\]\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[ y > 25 \\]\nSince \\( y \\) must be an integer, the smallest possible value for \\( y \\) is 26. We can now substitute \\( y = 26 \\) into the equation \\( 7x + 53 = 8y \\) to find \\( x \\):\n\\[ 7x + 53 = 8 \\cdot 26 \\]\n\\[ 7x + 53 = 208 \\]\n\\[ 7x = 155 \\]\n\\[ x = \\frac{155}{7} \\]\nSince \\( x \\) must be an integer, \\( x = \\frac{155}{7} \\) is not a valid solution. We need to try the next smallest possible value for \\( y \\), which is 27:\n\\[ 7x + 53 = 8 \\cdot 27 \\]\n\\[ 7x + 53 = 216 \\]\n\\[ 7x = 163 \\]\n\\[ x = \\frac{163}{7} \\]\nAgain, \\( x \\) is not an integer. We continue this process until we find an integer solution for \\( x \\). Let's try \\( y = 28 \\):\n\\[ 7x + 53 = 8 \\cdot 28 \\]\n\\[ 7x + 53 = 224 \\]\n\\[ 7x = 171 \\]\n\\[ x = \\frac{171}{7} \\]\nAgain, \\( x \\) is not an integer. Let's try \\( y = 29 \\):\n\\[ 7x + 53 = 8 \\cdot 29 \\]\n\\[ 7x + 53 = 232 \\]\n\\[ 7x = 179 \\]\n\\[ x = \\frac{179}{7} \\]\nAgain, \\( x \\) is not an integer. Let's try \\( y = 30 