atrix} = (4 - \\lambda)(13 - \\lambda) - 36 = \\lambda^2 - 17\\lambda + 16. \\]\n\nThe roots of the characteristic polynomial are the eigenvalues:\n\\[ \\lambda^2 - 17\\lambda + 16 = 0. \\]\nUsing the quadratic formula \\(\\lambda = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}\\), we get:\n\\[ \\lambda = \\frac{17 \\pm \\sqrt{17^2 - 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 16}}{2 \\cdot 1} = \\frac{17 \\pm \\sqrt{289 - 64}}{2} = \\frac{17 \\pm \\sqrt{225}}{2} = \\frac{17 \\pm 15}{2}. \\]\nSo the eigenvalues are:\n\\[ \\lambda_1 = 16 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\lambda_2 = 1. \\]\n\nThe largest eigenvalue is 16. Therefore, the largest singular value of \\( A \\) is \\(\\sqrt{16} = 4\\). Thus, the smallest positive real number \\( C \\) for which \\(\\left\\| \\begin{pmatrix} 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -2 \\end{pmatrix} \\mathbf{v} \\right\\| \\le C \\|\\mathbf{v}\\|\\) for all two-dimensional vectors \\(\\mathbf{v}\\) is \\( C = 4 \\).\n\nThe final answer is:\n\\[ \\boxed{4} \\]\n\nLet's verify this with Python code to ensure accuracy.\n```python\nimport numpy as np\n\n# Define the matrix A\nA = np.array([[2, 3], [0, -2]])\n\n# Compute A^T A\nA_T_A = np.dot(A.T, A)\n\n# Find the eigenvalues of A^T A\neigenvalues, _ = np.linalg.eig(A_T_A)\n\n# The largest eigenvalue is the square of the largest singular value\nlargest_eigenvalue = np.max(eigenvalues)\n\n# The largest singular value is the square root of the largest eigenvalue\nlargest_singular_value = np.sqrt(largest_eigenvalue)\n\nprint(largest_singular_value)\n```\n\nIf the solution set of the inequality $x^{2}-2ax+1 \\lt 0$ is not an empty set, then the range of values for $a$ is ( )\n\nA: $\\left[-1,1\\right]$\n\nB: $(-1,1)$\n\nC: $(-\\infty ,-1]\\cup \\left[1,+\\infty \\right)$\n\nD: $(-\\infty ,-1)\\cup \\left(1,+\\infty \\right)$\nTo determine the range of values for \\(a\\) such that the inequality \\(x^2 - 2ax + 1 < 0\\) has a non-empty solution set, we need to analyze the quadratic equation \\(x^2 - 2", "To find the smallest positive real number \\( 