\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\tan x^\\circ = \\frac{\\tan 53^\\circ + \\tan 81^\\circ}{\\tan 53^\\circ \\tan 81^\\circ - 1}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Calculate the values of \\(\\tan 53^\\circ\\) and \\(\\tan 81^\\circ\\):**\n   We can use Python to get the precise values of these trigonometric functions.\n\n4. **Substitute the values into the equation and solve for \\( x \\):**\n   Use the arctangent function to find \\( x \\) from \\(\\tan x^\\circ\\).\n\nLet's implement this in Python to find the value of \\( x \\).\n\n```python\nimport math\n\n# Calculate tan(53 degrees) and tan(81 degrees)\ntan_53 = math.tan(math.radians(53))\ntan_81 = math.tan(math.radians(81))\n\n# Calculate the right-hand side of the equation\nnumerator = tan_53 + tan_81\ndenominator = tan_53 * tan_81 - 1\ntan_x = numerator / denominator\n\n# Calculate the angle x in degrees\nx = math.degrees(math.atan(tan_x))\n\n# Print the result\nprint(x)\n```\n\nRunning this code will give us the value of \\( x \\). Let's execute it to find the final answer.\n```python\nimport math\n\n# Calculate tan(53 degrees) and tan(81 degrees)\ntan_53 = math.tan(math.radians(53))\ntan_81 = math.tan(math.radians(81))\n\n# Calculate the right-hand side of the equation\nnumerator = tan_53 + tan_81\ndenominator = tan_53 * tan_81 - 1\ntan_x = numerator / denominator\n\n# Calculate the angle x in degrees\nx = math.degrees(math.atan(tan_x))\n\n# Print the result\nprint(x)\n```\n```output\n46.00000000000001\n```\nThe value of \\( x \\) that satisfies the equation is \\( 46^\\circ \\).\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{46}\n\\]", "We start with the given equation:\n\n\\[\n\\tan 53^\\circ \\tan 81^\\circ \\tan x^\\circ = \\tan 53^\\circ + \\tan 81^\\circ + \\tan x^\\circ.\n\\]\n\nWe can rearrange this equation to isolate \\(\\tan x^\\circ\\):\n\n\\[\n\\tan x^\\circ (\\tan 53^\\circ \\tan 81^\\circ - 1) = \\tan 53^\\circ + \\tan 81^\\circ.\n\\]\n\nThus,\n\n\\[\n\\tan x^\\circ = \\frac{\\tan 53^\\circ + \\tan 81^\\circ}{\\tan 53^\\cir