( r \\neq 0 \\)):\n\\[\n\\frac{4}{3} r = 4\n\\]\n\nNow, solve for \\( r \\) by multiplying both sides by \\( \\frac{3}{4} \\):\n\\[\nr = 4 \\times \\frac{3}{4} = 3\n\\]\n\nThus, the radius of the sphere is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{3}\n\\]\n\n### Problem 2: Smallest Prime Factor of 2016\n\nTo find the smallest prime factor of 2016, we start by performing prime factorization.\n\nFirst, we check if 2016 is divisible by the smallest prime number, which is 2:\n\\[\n2016 \\div 2 = 1008\n\\]\n\nNext, we continue dividing by 2:\n\\[\n1008 \\div 2 = 504\n\\]\n\\[\n504 \\div 2 = 252\n\\]\n\\[\n252 \\div 2 = 126\n\\]\n\\[\n126 \\div 2 = 63\n\\]\n\nNow, 63 is not divisible by 2, so we move to the next smallest prime number, which is 3:\n\\[\n63 \\div 3 = 21\n\\]\n\\[\n21 \\div 3 = 7\n\\]\n\nFinally, 7 is a prime number.\n\nThus, the prime factorization of 2016 is:\n\\[\n2016 = 2^5 \\times 3^2 \\times 7\n\\]\n\nThe smallest prime factor is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{2}\n\\]\n\n### Problem 3: Simplifying the Expression\n\nWe need to simplify the expression:\n\\[\n\\sqrt{12} + \\sqrt{27} - \\sqrt{3}\n\\]\n\nFirst, simplify each square root:\n\\[\n\\sqrt{12} = \\sqrt{4 \\times 3} = \\sqrt{4} \\times \\sqrt{3} = 2\\sqrt{3}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\sqrt{27} = \\sqrt{9 \\times 3} = \\sqrt{9} \\times \\sqrt{3} = 3\\sqrt{3}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\sqrt{3} = \\sqrt{3}\n\\]\n\nNow, substitute these back into the expression:\n\\[\n2\\sqrt{3} + 3\\sqrt{3} - \\sqrt{3}\n\\]\n\nCombine the like terms:\n\\[\n(2 + 3 - 1)\\sqrt{3} = 4\\sqrt{3}\n\\]\n\nThus, the simplified expression is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{4\\sqrt{3}}\n\\]\n\n### Problem 4: Finding the Radius of a Sphere with Equal Volume and Surface Area\n\nWe have already solved this problem in Problem 1. The radius of the sphere is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{3}\n\\]\n\n### Problem 5: Smallest Prime Factor of 2016\n\nWe have already solved this problem in Problem 2. The smallest prime factor is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{2}\n\\]\n\n### Problem 6: Smallest Prime Factor of 2016\n\nWe have already solved this problem in 