we need a common denominator. The common denominator for \\(\\cos x \\cdot \\sin x\\) and \\(\\cos x\\) is \\(\\cos x \\cdot \\sin x\\).\n\n6. Rewrite the second term with the common denominator:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{\\sin x}{\\cos x} = \\frac{\\sin x \\cdot \\sin x}{\\cos x \\cdot \\sin x} = \\frac{\\sin^2 x}{\\cos x \\cdot \\sin x}\n   \\]\n\n7. Now the expression becomes:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{1}{\\cos x \\cdot \\sin x} - \\frac{\\sin^2 x}{\\cos x \\cdot \\sin x}\n   \\]\n\n8. Combine the numerators over the common denominator:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{1 - \\sin^2 x}{\\cos x \\cdot \\sin x}\n   \\]\n\n9. Recall the Pythagorean identity: \\(1 - \\sin^2 x = \\cos^2 x\\). Substitute this into the expression:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{\\cos^2 x}{\\cos x \\cdot \\sin x}\n   \\]\n\n10. Simplify the fraction:\n    \\[\n    \\frac{\\cos^2 x}{\\cos x \\cdot \\sin x} = \\frac{\\cos x}{\\sin x} = \\cot x\n    \\]\n\nThus, the simplified expression is \\(\\cot x\\).\n\nLet's verify this step-by-step simplification using Python code.\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the variable\nx = sp.symbols('x')\n\n# Define the expression\nexpr = sp.sec(x) / sp.sin(x) - sp.sin(x) / sp.cos(x)\n\n# Simplify the expression\nsimplified_expr = sp.simplify(expr)\nprint(simplified_expr)\n```\n```output\n1/tan(x)\n```\nThe simplified expression \\(\\frac{\\sec x}{\\sin x} - \\frac{\\sin x}{\\cos x}\\) is indeed \\(\\cot x\\), which is equivalent to \\(\\frac{1}{\\tan x}\\).\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\cot x}\n\\]", "To simplify the expression \\(\\frac{\\sec x}{\\sin x} - \\frac{\\sin x}{\\cos x}\\), let's first recall the definitions of the trigonometric functions involved:\n\n- \\(\\sec x = \\frac{1}{\\cos x}\\)\n- \\(\\sin x = \\sin x\\)\n- \\(\\cos x = \\cos x\\)\n\nSubstituting \\(\\sec x\\) with \\(\\frac{1}{\\cos x}\\) in the expression, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{\\sec x}{\\sin x} - \\frac{\\sin x}{\\cos x} = \\frac{\\frac{1}{\\cos x}}{\\sin x} - \\frac{\\sin x}{\\cos x} = \\frac{1}{\\cos x \\cdot \\sin x} - \\frac{\\sin x}{\\cos x}\n\\]\n\nNext, we need a common denominator to combine these two fractions. The common denominator is \\(\\cos x \\cdot \\sin x\\):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{\\cos x \\cdot \\sin x} - \\frac{\\sin x}{\\cos x} = \\frac{1}{\\cos x \\cdot \\sin x} - \\frac{\\sin^2 x}{\\cos x \\cdot \\sin x} = \\frac{1 - \\sin^2 x}{\\cos x \\cdot \\sin x}\n\\]\n\nRecall the Pythagorean identity: \\(1 - \\sin^2 x = \\cos^2 x\\). Substituting this in, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1 - \\sin^2 x}{\\cos x \\cdot \\sin x} = \\frac{\\cos^2 x}{\\cos x \\cdot \\sin x} = \\frac{\\cos x}{\\sin x} = \\cot x\n\\]\n\nSo the simplified expression is \\(\\cot x\\). Let's confirm this using Python code.\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the variable\nx = sp.symbols('x')\n\n# Define the expression\nexpr = sp.sec(x) / sp.sin(x) - sp.sin(x) / sp.cos(x)\n\n# Simplify the expression\nsimplified_expr = sp.simplify(expr)\nprint(simplified_expr)\n```\n```output\n1/tan(x)\n```\nThe output from the Python code confirms our manual simplification. The expression \\(\\frac{\\sec x}{\\sin x} - \\frac{\\sin x}{\\cos x}\\) simplifies to \\(\\cot x\\), which is equivalent to \\(\\frac{1}{\\tan x}\\).\n\nSo the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\cot x}\n\\]", "To simplify the expression \\(\\frac{\\sec x}{\\sin x} - \\frac{\\sin x}{\\cos x}\\), we can follow these steps:\n\n1. Recall the trigonometric identities:\n   - \\(\\sec x = \\frac{1}{\\cos x}\\)\n   - \\(\\sin x = \\sin x\\)\n   - \\(\\cos x = \\cos x\\)\n\n2. Substitute \\(\\sec x\\) with \\(\\frac{1}{\\cos x}\\) in the expression:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{\\sec x}{\\sin x} = \\frac{\\frac{1}{\\cos x}}{\\sin x} = \\frac{1}{\\cos x \\sin x}\n   \\]\n\n3. Now the expression becomes:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{1}{\\cos x \\sin x} - \\frac{\\sin x}{\\cos x}\n   \\]\n\n4. To combine the fractions, we need a common denominator. The common denominator for \\(\\cos x \\sin x\\) and \\(\\cos x\\) is \\(\\cos x \\sin x\\):\n   \\[\n   \\fra