least one of the numbers must be a multiple of 5. The numbers on a 6-sided die that are multiples of 5 are 5 (since 10 is not on a 6-sided die).\n\nLet's break it down step-by-step:\n\n1. Identify the outcomes where at least one of the dice shows a 5.\n2. Count these outcomes.\n3. Divide the number of favorable outcomes by the total number of outcomes to get the probability.\n\nWe can use Python to ensure the accuracy of our result. Let's write the code to calculate this probability.\n```python\n# Total number of outcomes when two dice are thrown\ntotal_outcomes = 6 * 6\n\n# Count the number of favorable outcomes where the product is a multiple of 5\nfavorable_outcomes = 0\n\n# Loop through all possible outcomes of two dice\nfor die1 in range(1, 7):\n    for die2 in range(1, 7):\n        if (die1 * die2) % 5 == 0:\n            favorable_outcomes += 1\n\n# Calculate the probability\nprobability = favorable_outcomes / total_outcomes\nprint((favorable_outcomes, total_outcomes, probability))\n```\n```output\n(11, 36, 0.3055555555555556)\n```\nThe number of favorable outcomes where the product of the two numbers is a multiple of 5 is 11. The total number of possible outcomes is 36. Therefore, the probability is \\(\\frac{11}{36}\\).\n\nSo, the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{11}{36}}\n\\]", 'The probability that the product of two numbers is a multiple of 5 is the same as the probability that at least one of the numbers is a multiple of 5.  There are 6 numbers on a die, and 1 of them is a multiple of 5 (5).  So, the probability that a single die shows a multiple of 5 is 1/6.  The probability that a single die does not show a multiple of 5 is 5/6.  The probability that neither die shows a multiple of 5 is (5/6) * (5/6) = 25/36.  So, the probability that at least one die shows a multiple of 5 is 1 - 25/36 = 11/36.  Therefore, the probability that the product of the two numbers is a multiple of 5 is 11/36.  \\boxed{\\frac{11}{36}}', 'The product of the two numbers i