 the value of $a + b$. \\boxed{}. Shay\nTo solve the problem, we need to analyze the given conditions step by step.\n\n1. **Understanding the Domain of \\(\\log x^2\\):**\n   - The function \\(\\log x^2\\) is defined when \\(x^2 > 0\\). This means \\(x \\neq 0\\). So, the domain is all real numbers except 0, i.e., \\(x < 0\\) or \\(x > 0\\).\n   - Given that the domain is \\(x < a\\) or \\(x > b\\), we can infer that \\(a = 0\\) and \\(b = 0\\). Therefore, \\(a + b = 0\\).\n\n2. **Solving for \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) with the Given Conditions:**\n   - We have \\(f(x) = \\log_2(x)\\) and \\(g(x) = \\log_3(x)\\).\n   - Given \\(f(a) = g(b)\\) and \\(f(b) = g(a)\\), we can write:\n     \\[\n     \\log_2(a) = \\log_3(b) \\quad \\text{(1)}\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     \\log_2(b) = \\log_3(a) \\quad \\text{(2)}\n     \\]\n   - Let's solve these equations. From equation (1), we get:\n     \\[\n     \\frac{\\log(a)}{\\log(2)} = \\frac{\\log(b)}{\\log(3)}\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     \\log(a) \\cdot \\log(3) = \\log(b) \\cdot \\log(2) \\quad \\text{(3)}\n     \\]\n   - From equation (2), we get:\n     \\[\n     \\frac{\\log(b)}{\\log(2)} = \\frac{\\log(a)}{\\log(3)}\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     \\log(b) \\cdot \\log(3) = \\log(a) \\cdot \\log(2) \\quad \\text{(4)}\n     \\]\n   - Equations (3) and (4) are essentially the same, so we can use either one. Let's use equation (3):\n     \\[\n     \\log(a) \\cdot \\log(3) = \\log(b) \\cdot \\log(2)\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     \\log(a) = \\log(b) \\cdot \\frac{\\log(2)}{\\log(3)}\n     \\]\n   - Substitute \\(\\log(a)\\) in equation (2):\n     \\[\n     \\log(b) = \\log(a) \\cdot \\frac{\\log(3)}{\\log(2)}\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     \\log(b) = \\left(\\log(b) \\cdot \\frac{\\log(2)}{\\log(3)}\\right) \\cdot \\frac{\\log(3)}{\\log(2)}\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     \\log(b) = \\log(b)\n     \\]\n   - This is an identity, so it holds true. Now, let's find the values of \\(a\\) and \\(b\\). Since \\(\\log(a) = \\log(b) \\cdot \\frac{\\log(2)}{\\log(3)}\\),