 do this by substituting each solution back into the original equation.\n\n1. For \\(x = 1\\):\n\\[|5(1) - 1| = 1 + 3\\]\n\\[|5 - 1| = 4\\]\n\\[|4| = 4\\]\n\\[4 = 4\\]\nThis is true, so \\(x = 1\\) is a valid solution.\n\n2. For \\(x = -\\frac{1}{3}\\):\n\\[|5\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right) - 1| = -\\frac{1}{3} + 3\\]\n\\[|-\\frac{5}{3} - 1| = \\frac{8}{3}\\]\n\\[|-\\frac{5}{3} - \\frac{3}{3}| = \\frac{8}{3}\\]\n\\[|-\\frac{8}{3}| = \\frac{8}{3}\\]\n\\[\\frac{8}{3} = \\frac{8}{3}\\]\nThis is true, so \\(x = -\\frac{1}{3}\\) is also a valid solution.\n\nSince we are asked to find the largest value of \\(x\\) that satisfies the equation, and \\(1 > -\\frac{1}{3}\\), the largest value of \\(x\\) is \\(1\\).\n\nLet's confirm this using Python code.\n```python\n# Define the function to check the solutions\r\ndef check_solution(x):\r\n    return abs(5 * x - 1) == x + 3\r\n\r\n# Solutions found\r\nsolutions = [1, -1/3]\r\n\r\n# Check each solution\r\nvalid_solutions = [x for x in solutions if check_solution(x)]\r\n\r\n# Find the largest valid solution\r\nlargest_solution = max(valid_solutions)\r\nprint(largest_solution)\n```\n```output\n1\n```\nThe largest value of \\(x\\) that satisfies the equation \\(|5x - 1| = x + 3\\) is \\(\\boxed{1}\\).", "To solve the equation \\( |5x - 1| = x + 3 \\), we need to consider the two possible cases for the absolute value function:\n\n1. \\( 5x - 1 = x + 3 \\)\n2. \\( 5x - 1 = -(x + 3) \\)\n\nWe will solve each case separately and then determine which solution is the largest.\n\n### Case 1: \\( 5x - 1 = x + 3 \\)\n\n\\[\n5x - 1 = x + 3\n\\]\n\nSubtract \\( x \\) from both sides:\n\n\\[\n4x - 1 = 3\n\\]\n\nAdd 1 to both sides:\n\n\\[\n4x = 4\n\\]\n\nDivide by 4:\n\n\\[\nx = 1\n\\]\n\n### Case 2: \\( 5x - 1 = -(x + 3) \\)\n\n\\[\n5x - 1 = -x - 3\n\\]\n\nAdd \\( x \\) to both sides:\n\n\\[\n6x - 1 = -3\n\\]\n\nAdd 1 to both sides:\n\n\\[\n6x = -2\n\\]\n\nDivide by 6:\n\n\\[\nx = -\\frac{1}{3}\n\\]\n\nNow we have two solutions: \\( x = 1 \\) a