\):\n\\[ |5(1) - 1| = 1 + 3 \\]\n\\[ |5 - 1| = 4 \\]\n\\[ 4 = 4 \\]\nThis is true, so \\( x = 1 \\) is a solution.\n\n2. For \\( x = -\\frac{1}{3} \\):\n\\[ |5\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right) - 1| = -\\frac{1}{3} + 3 \\]\n\\[ \\left| -\\frac{5}{3} - 1 \\right| = \\frac{8}{3} \\]\n\\[ \\left| -\\frac{5}{3} - \\frac{3}{3} \\right| = \\frac{8}{3} \\]\n\\[ \\left| -\\frac{8}{3} \\right| = \\frac{8}{3} \\]\n\\[ \\frac{8}{3} = \\frac{8}{3} \\]\nThis is true, so \\( x = -\\frac{1}{3} \\) is also a solution.\n\nSince we are asked to find the largest value of \\( x \\) that satisfies the equation, the answer is \\( x = 1 \\).\n\nLet's confirm this using Python code.\n```python\n# Define the function to check the solutions\r\ndef check_solution(x):\r\n    return abs(5 * x - 1) == x + 3\r\n\r\n# Possible solutions from the cases\r\npossible_solutions = [1, -1/3]\r\n\r\n# Check which solutions satisfy the original equation\r\nvalid_solutions = [x for x in possible_solutions if check_solution(x)]\r\n\r\n# Find the largest valid solution\r\nlargest_solution = max(valid_solutions)\r\nprint(largest_solution)\n```\n```output\n1\n```\nThe largest value of \\( x \\) that satisfies the equation \\( |5x - 1| = x + 3 \\) is \\(\\boxed{1}\\).", "To solve the equation $|5x-1|=x+3$, we need to consider two cases due to the definition of absolute value:\n\n1. Case 1: $5x - 1 = x + 3$\n2. Case 2: $5x - 1 = -(x + 3)$\n\nLet's solve each case separately.\n\n### Case 1: $5x - 1 = x + 3$\n\\[\n5x - 1 = x + 3\n\\]\nSubtract $x$ from both sides:\n\\[\n4x - 1 = 3\n\\]\nAdd 1 to both sides:\n\\[\n4x = 4\n\\]\nDivide by 4:\n\\[\nx = 1\n\\]\n\n### Case 2: $5x - 1 = -(x + 3)$\n\\[\n5x - 1 = -x - 3\n\\]\nAdd $x$ to both sides:\n\\[\n6x - 1 = -3\n\\]\nAdd 1 to both sides:\n\\[\n6x = -2\n\\]\nDivide by 6:\n\\[\nx = -\\frac{1}{3}\n\\]\n\nNow, we need to verify which of these solutions satisfy the original equation. We will substitute $x = 1$ and $x = -\\frac{1}{3}$ back into the original equation $|5x-1|=x+3$.\