\\circ\n\\]\n\n\\[\n3x + 3y = 150^\\circ\n\\]\n\n\\[\nx + y = 50^\\circ\n\\]\n\nNow, we need to find \\(\\angle AFD\\). Notice that \\(\\angle AFD\\) is an external angle to the trisected angles at \\(A\\) and \\(D\\). The measure of \\(\\angle AFD\\) is equal to the sum of the angles at \\(B\\) and \\(C\\) minus the sum of the trisected angles at \\(A\\) and \\(D\\):\n\n\\[\n\\angle AFD = 180^\\circ - (x + y)\n\\]\n\nSince \\(x + y = 50^\\circ\\), we get:\n\n\\[\n\\angle AFD = 180^\\circ - 50^\\circ = 130^\\circ\n\\]\n\nThus, the degree measure of \\(\\angle AFD\\) is \\(\\boxed{130}\\).\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code to ensure the calculations are accurate.\n```python\n# Given angles at B and C\nangle_B = 110\nangle_C = 100\n\n# Sum of the interior angles of a quadrilateral is 360 degrees\nsum_of_angles = 360\n\n# Let angle BAD = 3x and angle CDA = 3y\n# So, 3x + 3y + angle_B + angle_C = 360\n# 3x + 3y + 110 + 100 = 360\n# 3x + 3y = 150\n# x + y = 50\n\n# The measure of angle AFD is 180 - (x + y)\nangle_AFD = 180 - 50\nprint(angle_AFD)\n```\n\nIf the cost of 1 piece of gum in Euros is €0.02, what is the cost of 2500 pieces of gum, in Euros?\nTo find the cost of 2500 pieces of gum, we can use the given cost of one piece of gum, which is €0.02. The total cost can be calculated by multiplying the cost of one piece of gum by the total number of pieces.\n\nThe calculation is straightforward:\n\\[ \\text{Total cost} = 2500 \\times 0.02 \\]\n\nLet's write the Python code to perform this calculation.\n```python\n# Given data\ncost_per_piece = 0.02  # in Euros\nnumber_of_pieces = 2500\n\n# Calculate the total cost\ntotal_cost = cost_per_piece * number_of_pieces\nprint(total_cost)\n```\n```output\n50.0\n```\nThe total cost of 2500 pieces of gum is \\(\\boxed{50.0}\\) Euros.\n\nFind the remainder when $x^{44} + x^{33} + x^{22} + x^{11} + 1$ is divided by $x^4 + x^3 + x^2 + x + 1.$\nTo solve the problem of finding the remainder when \\(P(x) = x^{44}", "To solve this p