ible values for \\(y\\) are \\(y = 10 + \\sqrt{41}\\) and \\(y = 10 - \\sqrt{41}\\).\n\nNext, we substitute these values of \\(y\\) back into one of the original equations to find the corresponding \\(x\\) values. We'll use the first equation \\(x^2 + y^2 + 6x - 24y + 72 = 0\\).\n\nFor \\(y = 10 + \\sqrt{41}\\):\n\n\\[\nx^2 + (10 + \\sqrt{41})^2 + 6x - 24(10 + \\sqrt{41}) + 72 = 0\n\\]\n\nSimplifying, we get:\n\n\\[\nx^2 + 100 + 20\\sqrt{41} + 41 + 6x - 240 - 24\\sqrt{41} + 72 = 0\n\\]\n\n\\[\nx^2 + 6x - 27 + (-4\\sqrt{41}) = 0\n\\]\n\n\\[\nx^2 + 6x - 27 - 4\\sqrt{41} = 0\n\\]\n\nWe can solve this quadratic equation for \\(x\\) using the quadratic formula \\(x = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}\\), where \\(a = 1\\), \\(b = 6\\), and \\(c = -27 - 4\\sqrt{41}\\):\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{-6 \\pm \\sqrt{36 + 4(27 + 4\\sqrt{41})}}{2} = \\frac{-6 \\pm \\sqrt{36 + 108 + 16\\sqrt{41}}}{2} = \\frac{-6 \\pm \\sqrt{144 + 16\\sqrt{41}}}{2}\n\\]\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{-6 \\pm \\sqrt{16(9 + \\sqrt{41})}}{2} = \\frac{-6 \\pm 4\\sqrt{9 + \\sqrt{41}}}{2} = -3 \\pm 2\\sqrt{9 + \\sqrt{41}}\n\\]\n\nSo, the possible values for \\(x\\) are \\(x = -3 + 2\\sqrt{9 + \\sqrt{41}}\\) and \\(x = -3 - 2\\sqrt{9 + \\sqrt{41}}\\).\n\nSimilarly, for \\(y = 10 - \\sqrt{41}\\):\n\n\\[\nx^2 + (10 - \\sqrt{41})^2 + 6x - 24(10 - \\sqrt{41}) + 72 = 0\n\\]\n\nSimplifying, we get:\n\n\\", "To solve this problem, we need to find the points of intersection of the two given curves and then compute the sum of the distances from these points to the point \\((-3, 2)\\).\n\nThe equations of the curves are:\n1. \\(x^2 + y^2 + 6x - 24y + 72 = 0\\)\n2. \\(x^2 - y^2 + 6x + 16y - 46 = 0\\)\n\nFirst, we will solve these equations simultaneously to find the points of intersection. We can subtract the second equation from the first to eliminate the \\(x^2\\) and \\(6x\\) terms:\n\n\\[\n(x^2 + y^2 + 6x - 24y + 72) - (x^2 - y^2 + 6x + 16y - 46) = 0\n\\]\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[\n2y^2 - 40y + 118 = 0\n\\]\n\nDividing throug