g(f(x))) \\), which is odd. Since the composition of odd functions is odd, \\( f(f(g(f(g(f(x)))))) \\) is odd.\n\nTherefore, the function \\( f(f(g(f(g(f(x)))))) \\) is odd.\n\nLet's confirm this reasoning with Python code using sympy to symbolically verify the properties of the functions.\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the symbols\nx = sp.symbols('x')\n\n# Define the properties of odd and even functions\ndef is_odd(f):\n    return sp.simplify(f.subs(x, -x) + f) == 0\n\ndef is_even(f):\n    return sp.simplify(f.subs(x, -x) - f) == 0\n\n# Define the functions f and g symbolically\nf = sp.Function('f')\ng = sp.Function('g')\n\n# Assume f is odd and g is even\nf_odd = is_odd(f(x))\ng_even = is_even(g(x))\n\n# Define the composition\ncomposition = f(f(g(f(g(f(x))))))\n\n# Check if the composition is odd\ncomposition_odd = is_odd(composition)\n\nprint(composition_odd)\n```\n```output\ntimeout_decorator.timeout_decorator.TimeoutError: 'Timed Out'\n```\nIt seems that the symbolic verification using sympy might be too complex for direct computation within a reasonable time frame. However, based on our reasoning, we can conclude that the function \\( f(f(g(f(g(f(x)))))) \\) is indeed odd. Let's summarize the reasoning:\n\n1. \\( f \\) is an odd function, so \\( f(-x) = -f(x) \\).\n2. \\( g \\) is an even function, so \\( g(-x) = g(x) \\).\n\nNow, let's analyze the composition step by step:\n- \\( f(g(f(x))) \\): Since \\( f \\) is odd and \\( g \\) is even, \\( f(g(f(x))) \\) is odd because the composition of an odd function with an even function is odd.\n- \\( f(f(g(f(x)))) \\): Since \\( f \\) is odd, the composition of \\( f \\) with itself is also odd.\n- \\( f(f(g(f(g(f(x)))))) \\): This is the composition of \\( f \\) with itself and \\( f(g(f(x))) \\), which is odd. Since the composition of odd functions is odd, \\( f(f(g(f(g(f(x)))))) \\) is odd.\n\nTherefore, the function \\( f(f(g(f(g(f(x)))))) \\) is odd.\n\nThe final answer is \\(\\boxed{\\text{odd}