s:\n\\[ \\text{Area} = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot AB \\cdot CD \\]\n\\[ 60 = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 17 \\cdot CD \\]\n\\[ CD = \\frac{120}{17} \\]\n4. Now we can find the length of $AD$ using the Pythagorean theorem in triangle $ACD$:\n\\[ AD = \\sqrt{AC^2 - CD^2} = \\sqrt{8^2 - \\left(\\frac{120}{17}\\right)^2} = \\sqrt{64 - \\frac{14400}{289}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{18496 - 14400}{289}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{4096}{289}} = \\frac{64}{17} \\]\n5. Finally, the area of triangle $ACD$ is:\n\\[ \\text{Area} = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot AD \\cdot CD = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{64}{17} \\cdot \\frac{120}{17} = \\frac{3840}{289} \\]\n\n### Problem 2: Length of $AD$\nGiven:\n- $AB = 13$\n- $AC = 15$\n- $BC = 14$\n- $D$ is the midpoint of $BC$\n\nWe can use the Angle Bisector Theorem to find the length of $AD$. The Angle Bisector Theorem states that:\n\\[ \\frac{BD}{DC} = \\frac{AB}{AC} = \\frac{13}{15} \\]\nSince $D$ is the midpoint of $BC$, $BD = DC = 7$. So we can use the Angle Bisector Length formula:\n\\[ AD = \\sqrt{AB \\cdot AC \\left(1 - \\frac{BC^2}{(AB + AC)^2}\\right)}", 'In triangle $ABC$, $AB = 13$, $AC = 15$, and $BC = 14$.  Let $AD$ be the angle bisector of $\\angle BAC$, and let $AE$ be the median from $A$ to $BC$.  Let $M$ and $N$ be the feet of the perpendiculars from $D$ and $E$ to $BC$, respectively.  Find $MN$.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}. 