an square) is given by:\n\n\\[\nQ = \\sqrt{\\frac{\\sum_{i=1}^n a_i^2}{n}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{144}{n}} = \\frac{12}{\\sqrt{n}}.\n\\]\n\nThe cubic mean is given by:\n\n\\[\nC = \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{\\sum_{i=1}^n a_i^3}{n}} = \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{216}{n}} = \\frac{6}{\\sqrt[3]{n}}.\n\\]\n\nWe can use the inequality between the arithmetic mean and the quadratic mean:\n\n\\[\n\\mu \\leq Q \\implies \\frac{96}{n} \\leq \\frac{12}{\\sqrt{n}} \\implies 96 \\sqrt{n} \\leq 12n \\implies 8 \\sqrt{n} \\leq n \\implies n \\geq 64.\n\\]\n\nWe can also use the inequality between the arithmetic mean and the cubic mean:\n\n\\[\n\\mu \\leq C \\implies \\frac{96}{n} \\leq \\frac{6}{\\sqrt[3]{n}} \\implies 96 \\sqrt[3]{n} \\leq 6n \\implies 16 \\sqrt[3]{n} \\leq n \\implies n \\geq 64.\n\\]\n\nThus, the only possible value for \\(n\\) is 64. We can verify this by checking if the sequence can be composed of 64 identical terms. If all \\(a_i\\) are equal, then \\(a_i = \\frac{96}{64} = \\frac{3}{2}\\). Then:\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^{64} a_i = 64 \\cdot \\frac{3}{2} = 96,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^{64} a_i^2 = 64 \\cdot \\left( \\frac{3}{2} \\right)^2 = 64 \\cdot \\frac{9}{4} = 144,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^{64} a_i^3 = 64 \\cdot \\left( \\frac{3}{2} \\right)^3 = 64 \\cdot \\frac{27}{8} = 216.\n\\]\n\nThus, the only possible value for \\(n\\) is 64. The sum of all possible values of \\(n\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{64}.\n\\]\n\nLet's verify this with Python code to ensure the accuracy of the result.\n```python\n# Let's verify the solution using Python\n\n# Given values\nsum_a = 96\nsum_a2 = 144\nsum_a3 = 216\n\n# Let's check if n = 64 satisfies the conditions\nn = 64\na = sum_a / n  # Since all a_i are equal, a_i = 96 / ", "To solve this problem, we need to find the value of \\(n\\) given the constraints:\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^n a_i = 96, \\quad \\sum_{i=1}^n a_i^2 = 144, \\quad \\sum_{i=1}^n a_i^3 = 216.\n\\]\nWe can use the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality and other algebraic identities to derive the possible values of \