r after dividing them equally into three bags. This is essentially finding the remainder when the total number of pencils is divided by 3.\n\nSince the number of pencils left over can be any number from 0 to 2 (as the remainder when dividing by 3 can only be 0, 1, or 2), the greatest number of pencils left over would be 2.\n\nLet's confirm this with a simple Python code snippet. We'll simulate the process by iterating through a range of possible total pencil counts and checking the remainder when divided by 3.\n\n```python\n# Let's find the greatest number of pencils left over after dividing them equally into three bags\n# The remainder when dividing by 3 can be 0, 1, or 2\n# So the greatest possible remainder is 2\n\n# Let's confirm this by iterating through a range of possible total pencil counts\ndef greatest_pencils_left_over():\n    max_remainder = 0\n    for total_pencils in range(1, 100):  # We can use any range, but let's use 1 to 99 for demonstration\n        remainder = total_pencils % 3\n        if remainder > max_remainder:\n            max_remainder = remainder\n    return max_remainder\n\n# Get the result\ngreatest_remainder = greatest_pencils_left_over()\nprint(greatest_remainder)\n```\n```output\n2\n```\nThe greatest number of pencils that could be left over after dividing them equally into three bags is \\(\\boxed{2}\\).", 'Zach has three bags and a bunch of pencils to be placed into the bags. He is told to place the greatest number of pencils possible into each of the three bags while also keeping the number of pencils in each bag the same. What is the greatest number of pencils he could have left over? \\boxed{3}', 'Zach has three bags and a bunch of pencils to be placed into the bags. He is told to place the greatest number of pencils possible into each of the three bags while also keeping the number of pencils in each bag the same. What is the greatest number of pencils he could have left over? \\boxed{3} \\boxed{3}\nZach has three bags and a bu