_2 - y_3)^2 = 25.\n\\]\n\nThese conditions represent the squared distances between the points \\((x_1, y_1)\\), \\((x_2, y_2)\\), and \\((x_3, y_3)\\). We can interpret these points as vertices of a triangle with side lengths 3, 4, and 5. The area of a triangle with sides \\(a\\), \\(b\\), and \\(c\\) can be found using Heron's formula. First, we calculate the semi-perimeter \\(s\\):\n\n\\[\ns = \\frac{3 + 4 + 5}{2} = 6.\n\\]\n\nThen, the area \\(A\\) of the triangle is:\n\n\\[\nA = \\sqrt{s(s-a)(s-b)(s-c)} = \\sqrt{6(6-3)(6-4)(6-5)} = \\sqrt{6 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 1} = \\sqrt{36} = 6.\n\\]\n\nThe area of the triangle can also be expressed in terms of the determinant \\(D\\):\n\n\\[\nA = \\frac{1}{2} \\left| D \\right|.\n\\]\n\nSo,\n\n\\[\n6 = \\frac{1}{2} \\left| D \\right| \\implies \\left| D \\right| = 12.\n\\]\n\nTherefore,\n\n\\[\nD^2 = 12^2 = 144.\n\\]\n\nLet's verify this with Python code to ensure accuracy.\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\r\n\r\n# Define the variables\r\nx1, y1, x2, y2, x3, y3 = sp.symbols('x1 y1 x2 y2 x3 y3')\r\n\r\n# Define the determinant\r\ndet = sp.Matrix([[x1, y1, 1], [x2, y2, 1], [x3, y3, 1]]).det()\r\n\r\n# Define the equations for the squared distances\r\neq1 = (x1 - x2)**2 + (y1 - y2)**2 - 9\r\neq2 = (x1 - x3)**2 + (y1 - y3)**2 - 16\r\neq3 = (x2 - x3)**2 + (y2 - y3)**2 - 25\r\n\r\n# Solve the system of equations\r\nsolution = sp.solve([eq1, eq2, eq3], (x1, y1, x2, y2, x3, y3), dict=True)\r\n\r\n# Substitute the solution into the determinant and calculate the square of the determinant\r\ndet_value = det.subs(solution[0])\r\ndet_squared = det_value**2\r\n\r\nprint(det_squared)\n```\n```output\ntimeout_decorator.timeout_decorator.TimeoutError: 'Timed Out'\n```\nIt seems that solving the system of equations directly is computationally intensive and may not be feasible within a reasonable time frame. Instead, we can use the geometric interpretation we discussed earlier. The area of the triangle formed by the points \\((x_1, y_1)\\), 