1}{2} \\times AB \\times AC = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 3 \\times 4 = 6.\n\\]\n\nThe area of the triangle can also be expressed in terms of the determinant of the matrix. The area of a triangle with vertices \\((x_1, y_1)\\), \\((x_2, y_2)\\), and \\((x_3, y_3)\\) is given by:\n\\[\n\\text{Area} = \\frac{1}{2} \\left| x_1(y_2 - y_3) + x_2(y_3 - y_1) + x_3(y_1 - y_2) \\right|.\n\\]\n\nSince we know the area is 6, we have:\n\\[\n6 = \\frac{1}{2} \\left| \\text{det} \\right| \\implies \\left| \\text{det} \\right| = 12.\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the value of the determinant squared is:\n\\[\n\\text{det}^2 = 12^2 = 144.\n\\]\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code to ensure the calculations are accurate.\n```python\n# Given conditions\r\nAB_squared = 9\r\nAC_squared = 16\r\nBC_squared = 25\r\n\r\n# Calculate the area of the triangle using the right triangle formula\r\narea = 0.5 * 3 * 4\r\n\r\n# The determinant of the matrix is twice the area of the triangle\r\ndet = 2 * area\r\n\r\n# The value of the determinant squared\r\ndet_squared = det ** 2\r\nprint(det_squared)\n```\n```output\n144.0\n```\nThe value of \\(\\begin{vmatrix} x_1 & y_1 & 1 \\\\ x_2 & y_2 & 1 \\\\ x_3 & y_3 & 1 \\end{vmatrix}^2\\) is \\(\\boxed{144}\\).", "To solve the problem, we need to find the determinant of the matrix \\(\\begin{vmatrix} x_1 & y_1 & 1 \\\\ x_2 & y_2 & 1 \\\\ x_3 & y_3 & 1 \\end{vmatrix}\\) and then square it. The determinant of the matrix is given by:\n\\[\n\\text{det} = \\begin{vmatrix} x_1 & y_1 & 1 \\\\ x_2 & y_2 & 1 \\\\ x_3 & y_3 & 1 \\end{vmatrix} = x_1(y_2 - y_3) + x_2(y_3 - y_1) + x_3(y_1 - y_2).\n\\]\n\nWe can use the given conditions to find the relationship between \\(x_1, x_2, x_3, y_1, y_2,\\) and \\(y_3\\). These conditions represent the squared distances between the points \\((x_1, y_1)\\), \\((x_2, y_2)\\), and \\((x_3, y_3)\\). Let's denote these points as \\(A(x_1, y_1)\\), \\(B(x_2, y_2)\\), and \\(C(x_3, y_3)\\). The conditions can be rewritten as:\n\\[\nAB^2 = 9, \\quad AC^2 = 16, \\quad BC^2 = 25.\n\\]\n\nThis means that the points \\(A\\), \\(B\\), and \\(C\\) form a right triangle with \\(AB = 3\\), \\(AC = 4\\), and \\(BC = 5\\). The area of the triangle can be calculated using the determinant formula for the area of a triangle with vertices \\((x_1, y_1)\\), \\((x_2, y_2)\\), and \\((x_3, y_3)\\):\n\\[\n\\text{Area} = \\frac{1}{2} \\left| x_1(y_2 - y_3) + x_2(y_3 - y_1) + x_3(y_1 - y_2) \\right|.\n\\]\n\nSince the area of the triangle can also be calculated using the base and height, we have:\n\\[\n\\text{Area} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times AB \\times AC = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 3 \\times 4 = 6.\n\\]\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\\[\n\\left| x_1(y_2 - y_3) + x_2(y_3 - y_1) + x_3(y_1 - y_2) \\right| = 12.\n\\]\n\nThus, the determinant squared is:\n\\[\n\\left( x_1(y_2 - y_3) + x_2(y_3 - y_1) + x_3(y_1 - y_2) \\right)^2 = 12^2 = 144.\n\\]\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\n# Given conditions\nAB = 3\nAC = 4\nBC = 5\n\n# Calculate the area of the triangle using the base and height\narea = 0.5 * AB * AC\n\n# The determinant of the matrix is twice the area of the triangle\ndet = 2 * area\n\n# The squared determinant\ndet_squared = det ** 2\nprint(det_squared)\n```\n```output\n144.0\n```\nThe squared determinant of the matrix \\(\\begin{vmatrix} x_1 & y_1 & 1 \\\\ x_2 & y_2 & 1 \\\\ x_3 & y_3 & 1 \\end{vmatrix}\\) is \\(\\boxed{144}\\).", "To solve this problem, we need to find the determinant of the matrix \\(\\begin{vmatrix} x_1 & y_1 & 1 \\\\ x_2 & y_2 & 1 \\\\ x_3 & y_3 & 1 \\end{vmatrix}\\) and then square it. Let's denote this determinant by \\(D\\). The determinant \\(D\\) is given by:\n\\[ D = \\begin{vmatrix} x_1 & y_1 & 1 \\\\ x_2 & y_2 & 1 \\\\ x_3 & y_3 & 1 \\end{vmatrix} = x_1(y_2 - y_3) - y_1(x_2 - x_3) + (x_2 y_3 - x_3 y_2). \\]\n\nTo find \\(D^2\\), we will use the given conditions:\n\\[\n(x_1 - x_2)^2 + (y_1 - y_2)^2 = 9,\n\\]\n\\[\n(x_1 - x_3)^2 + (y_1 - y_3)^2 = 16,\n\\]\n\\[\n(x_2 - x_3)^2 + (y_2 - y_3)^2 = 25.\n\\]\n\nThese conditions suggest that the points \\((x_1, y_1)\\), \\((x_2, y_2)\\), and \\((x_3, y_3)\\) form a right triangle with side lengths 3, 4, and 5. The determinant \\(D\\) is related to the area of the triangle formed by these points. The area \\(A\\) of the triangle can be found using the formula for the area of a right triangle with legs 3 and 4:\n\\[ A = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 3 \\times 4 = 6. \\]\n\nThe determinant \\(D\\) is twice the area of the triangle (since the determinant gives the signed area of the parallelogram formed by the vectors \\((x_1, y_1)\\), \\((x_2, y_2)\\), and \\((x_3, y_3)\\)):\n\\[ D = 2 \\times 6 = 12. \\]\n\nTherefore, \\(D^2 = 12^2 = 144\\). Let's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the variables\nx1, y1, x2, y2, x3, y3 = sp.symbols('x1 y1 x2 y2 x3 y3')\n\n# Define the determinant\nD = sp.Matrix([[x1, y1, 1], [x2, y2, 1], [x3, y3, 1]]).det()\n\n# Define the equations\neq1 = (x1 - x2)**2 + (y1 - y2)**2 - 9\neq2 = (x1 - x3)**2 + (y1 - y3)**2 - 16\neq3 = (x2 - x3)**2 + (y2 - y3)**2 - 25\n\n# Solve the equations\nsolutions = sp.solve([eq1, eq2, eq3], (x1, y1, x2, y2, x3, y3), dict=True)\n\n# Calculate the determinant for each solution\nD_squared_values = [D.subs(sol)**2 for sol in solutions]\n\n# Since all solutions should give the same value for D^2, we can just take the first one\nD_squared = D_squared_values[0]\nprint(D_squared)\n```\n```output\ntimeout_decorator.timeout_decorator.TimeoutError: 'Timed Out'\n```\nIt seems that solving the system of equations symbolically is computationally intensive and may not be feasible within a reasonable time frame. Instead, let's reason through the problem more carefully.\n\nGiven the conditions:\n\\[\n(x_1 - x_2)^2 + (y_1 - y_2)^2 = 9,\n\\]\n\\[\n(x_1 - x_3)^2 + (y_1 - y_3)^2 = 16,\n\\]\n\\[\n(x_2 - x_3)^2 + (y_2 - y_3)^2 = 25,\n\\]\nwe can see that these represent the squared distances between the points \\((x_1, y_1)\\), \\((x_2, y_2)\\), and \\((x_