 318\n 320\n 322\n 324\n 326\n 328\n 330\n 332\n 334\n 336\n 338\n 340\n 342\n 344\n 346", "The points $A(-1, -2)$, $B(3, -2)$, $C(3, 1)$, and $D(-1, 1)$ are the vertices of rectangle $ABCD$.  What is the area of this rectangle?  Please put your answer in the form $a \\times b$ and put your answer in the form $a \\times b$, where $a$ and $b$ are positive integers, in the box below.  (This means that $a$ and $b$ are the length and width of the rectangle, respectively.  For example, if the area is 12, you would put $3 \\times 4$ in the box, because 3 and 4 are the length and width of the rectangle, respectively.  Please note that the order of $a$ and $b$ does not matter.  So, if you put $4 \\times 3$ in the box, that is also correct.)\n$$\\boxed{\\phantom{000}}$$\nPlease provide a step-by-step reasoning process for each problem.\nCertainly! Let's solve each problem step by step.\n\n### Problem 1: Find the area of triangle \\( EFG \\)\n\nGiven:\n- The area of triangle \\( ABC \\) is 120.\n- \\( \\frac{AE}{EB} = \\frac{2}{3} \\)\n- \\( \\frac{AF}{FC} = \\frac{4}{3} \\)\n\nFirst, we use the given ratios to find the areas of triangles \\( AEF \\) and \\( EBC \\).\n\n1. **Area of \\( \\triangle AEF \\)**:\n   - Since \\( \\frac{AE}{EB} = \\frac{2}{3} \\), \\( E \\) divides \\( AB \\) in the ratio 2:3.\n   - Similarly, \\( \\frac{AF}{FC} = \\frac{4}{3} \\), so \\( F \\) divides \\( AC \\) in the ratio 4:3.\n\n2. **Area of \\( \\triangle AEF \\)**:\n   - The area of \\( \\triangle AEF \\) can be found using the formula for the area of a triangle with given ratios of sides:\n     \\[\n     \\text{Area of } \\triangle AEF = \\left( \\frac{AE}{AB} \\right) \\left( \\frac{AF}{AC} \\right) \\times \\text{Area of } \\triangle ABC\n     \\]\n   - Here, \\( \\frac{AE}{AB} = \\frac{2}{5} \\) and \\( \\frac{AF}{AC} = \\frac{4}{7} \\).\n   - Therefore,\n     \\[\n     \\text{Area of } \\triangle AEF = \\left( \\frac{2}{5} \\right) \\left( \\frac{4}{7} \\right) \\times 120 = \\frac{8}{35} \\times 120 = \\frac{960}{35} = \\frac{192}{7}\n     \\]\n\n3. **Area of \\( \\triangle EBC \\)**:\n   - The area of \\( \\triangle EBC \\) can be found by subtracting the area of \\( \\triangle AEF \\) from the area of \\( \\triangle ABC \\):\n     \\[\n     \\text{Area of } \\triangle EBC = 120 - \\frac{192}{7} = \\frac{840}{7} - \\frac{192}{7} = \\frac{648}{7}\n     \\]\n\n4. **Area of \\( \\triangle EFG \\)**:\n   - Since \\( G \\) is the centroid of \\( \\triangle ABC \\), it divides each median into a ratio of 2:1.\n   - The area of \\( \\triangle EFG \\) is one-seventh of the area of \\( \\triangle EBC \\) because \\( G \\) divides \\( \\triangle EBC \\) into three smaller triangles of equal area.\n     \\[\n     \\text{Area of } \\triangle EFG = \\frac{1}{3} \\times \\frac{648}{7} = \\frac{216}{7}\n     \\]\n\nThus, the area of triangle \\( EFG \\) is \\(\\boxed{16}\\).\n\n### Problem 2: Find the area of triangle \\( ENG \\)\n\nGiven:\n- The medians \\( AD \\), \\( BE \\), and \\( CF \\) of triangle \\( ABC \\) intersect at the centroid \\( G \\).\n- The line through \\( G \\) parallel to \\( BC \\) intersects \\( AB \\) and \\( AC \\) at \\( M \\) and \\( N \\), respectively.\n- The area of triangle \\( ABC \\) is 144", ' 112\n 144\n 16\n 54\n 128\n 108\n 48\n 72\n 36\n 96\n 24\n 192\n 18\n 12\n 32\n 64\n 84\n 132\n 156\n 168\n 174\n 180\n 198\n 210\n 222\n 234\n 246\n 258\n 270\n 282\n 294\n 306\n 318\n 330\n 342\n 354\n 366\n 378\n 390\n 402\n 414\n 426\n 438\n 450\n 462\n 474\n 486\n 498\n 510\n 522\n 534\n 546\n 558\n 570\n 582\n 594\n 606\n 618\n 630\n 642\n 654\n 666\n 678\n 690\n 702\n 714\n 726\n 738\n 750\n 762\n 774\n 786\n 798\n 810\n 822\n 834\n 846\n 858\n 870\n 882\n 894\n 906\n 918\n 930\n 942\n 954\n 966\n 978\n 990\n 1002\n 1014\n 1026\n 1038\n 1050\n 1062\n 1074\n 1086\n 1098\n 1110\n 1122\n 1134\n 1146\n 1158\n 1170\n 1182\n 1194\n 1206\n 1218\n 1230\n 1242\n 1254\n 1266\n 1278\n 1290\n 1302\n 1314\n 1326\n 1338\n 1350\n 1362\n 1374\n 