e raised to get $2\\sqrt{2}$. Since $2\\sqrt{2} = 2^1 \\cdot 2^{\\frac{1}{2}} = 2^{\\frac{3}{2}}$, we have $\\log_2(2\\sqrt{2}) = \\frac{3}{2}$.\n\n5. **Evaluate $\\log_2\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$:**\n   - The logarithm $\\log_2\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ is the power to which 2 must be raised to get $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$. Since $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} = 2^{\\frac{1}{2}} \\cdot 2^{-1} = 2^{-\\frac{1}{2}}$, we have $\\log_2\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} = -\\frac{1}{2}$.\n\nLet's summarize the results in a table:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{|c|c|}\n\\hline\n\\text{Expression} & \\text{Value} \\\\\n\\hline\n\\log_264 & 6 \\\\\n\\hline\n\\log_2\\left(\\frac{1}{8}\\right) & -3 \\\\\n\\hline\n\\log_2\\sqrt{2} & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\n\\hline\n\\log_2(2\\sqrt{2}) & \\frac{3}{2} \\\\\n\\hline\n\\log_2\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\\n\\hline\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nLet's verify these results using Python code.\n```python\nimport math\n\n# Calculate the logarithms\nlog2_64 = math.log2(64)\nlog2_1_8 = math.log2(1/8)\nlog2_sqrt2 = math.log2(2**0.5)\nlog2_2sqrt2 = math.log2(2 * 2**0.5)\nlog2_sqrt2_over_2 = math.log2(2**0.5 / 2)\n\n# Print the results\nprint(((log2_64, log2_1_8, log2_sqrt2, log2_2sqrt2, log2_sqrt2_over_2)))\n```\n```output\n(6.0, -3.0, 0.5000000000000001, 1.5000000000000002, -0.4999999999999999)\n```\nThe calculated values using Python are as follows:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{|c|c|}\n\\hline\n\\text{Expression} & \\text{Value} \\\\\n\\hline\n\\log_264 & 6.0 \\\\\n\\hline\n\\log_2\\left(\\frac{1}{8}\\right) & -3.0 \\\\\n\\hline\n\\log_2\\sqrt{2} & 0.5000000000000001 \\\\\n\\hline\n\\log_2(2\\sqrt{2}) & 1.500000000000", 'Evaluate $\\log_3\\left(\\frac{1}{27}\\right)$. 2. Evaluate \\(\\log_2 64\\):\n\nTo find \\(\\log_2 64\\), we need to determine the exponent to which 2 must be raised to get 64. We know that:\n\\[ 64 = 2^6 \\]\nTherefore,\n\\[ \\log_2 64 = 6 \\]\nSo, the answer is:\n\\[ \\boxed{6} \\]\n\n3. Evaluate \\(\\log_3 \\left(\\frac{1}{27}\\right)\\):\n\nTo find \\(\\log_3 \\left(\\frac{1}{27}\\right)\\), we need to determine the exponent to which 3 must be raised to get \\(\\frac{1}{27}\\). We know that:\n\\[ \\frac{1}{27} = 3^{-3} \\]\nTherefore,\n\\[ \\log_3 \\left(\\frac{1}{27}\\right) = -3 \\]\nSo, the answer is:\n\\[ \\boxed{-3} \\]', "To evaluate \\(\\log_2 64\\), we need to determine the power to which 2 must be raised to obtain 64. In other words, we need to find \\(x\\) such that \\(2^x = 64\\).\n\nWe know that:\n\\[ 2^6 = 64 \\]\n\nTherefore, \\(x = 6\\). So, \\(\\log_2 64 = 6\\).\n\nLet's confirm this using Python code.\n```python\nimport math\n\n# Calculate log base 2 of 64\nlog_base_2_of_64 = math.log2(64)\nprint(log_base_2_of_64)\n```\n```output\n6.0\n```\nThe value of \\(\\log_2 64\\) is indeed \\(6\\).\n\nSo, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{6}\n\\]", 'Evaluate $\\log_3\\frac{1}{27}$. 3\nEvaluate $\\log_4\\sqrt[4]{64}$. 3\nEvaluate $\\log_5125$. 5\nEvaluate $\\log_6\\frac{1}{36}$. 6\nEvaluate $\\log_7\\sqrt{7}$. 7\nEvaluate $\\log_81$. 8\nEvaluate $\\log_9\\frac{1}{81}$. 9\nEvaluate $\\log_{10}1000$. 10\nEvaluate $\\log_{11}\\sqrt[3]{121}$. 11\nEvaluate $\\log_{12}144$. 12\nEvaluate $\\log_{13}\\frac{1}{169}$. 13\nEvaluate $\\log_{14}\\sqrt[6]{196}$. 14\nEvaluate $\\log_{15}225$. 15\nEvaluate $\\log_{16}\\frac{1}{256}$. 16\nEvaluate $\\log_{17}\\sqrt[4]{289}$. 17\nEvaluate $\\log_{18}1800$. 18\nEvaluate $\\log_{19}\\frac{1}{361}$. 19\nEvaluate $\\log_{20}\\sqrt[5]{400}$. 20\nEvaluate $\\log_{21}441$. 21\nEvaluate $\\log_{22}\\frac{1}{484}$. 22\nEvaluate $\\log_{23}\\sqrt[3]{529}$. 23\nEvaluate $\\log_{24}1152$. 24\nEvaluate $\\log_{25}\\frac{1}{625}$. 25\nEvaluate $\\log_{26}\\sqrt[6]{676}$. 26\nEvaluate $\\log_{27}1701$. 27\nEvaluate $\\log_{28}\\frac{1}{784}$. 28\nEvaluate $\\log_{29}\\sqrt[3]{841}$. 29\nEvaluate $\\log_{30}2700$. 30\nEvaluate $\\log_{31}\\frac{1}{961}$. 31\nEvaluate $\\log_{32}\\sqrt[5]{1024}$. 32\nEvaluate $\\log_{33}35937$. 33\nEvaluate $\\log_{34}\\frac{1}{1156}$. 34\nEvaluate $\\log_{35}\\sqrt[3]{12