ally, we need to ensure that \\(\\gcd(|A|,|B|,|C|,|D|) = 1\\). The greatest common divisor (gcd) of 20, 28, 44, and 16 is 4. Dividing the equation by 4, we get:\n\\[\n5x - 7y + 11z + 4 = 0\n\\]\n\nSo, the equation of the plane is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{5x - 7y + 11z + 4 = 0}\n\\]\n\nLet's verify this using Python code.\n```python\nimport numpy as np\r\nfrom math import gcd\r\nfrom functools import reduce\r\n\r\n# Points\r\nP1 = np.array([0, -1, -1])\r\nP2 = np.array([-4, 4, 4])\r\nP3 = np.array([4, 5, 1])\r\n\r\n# Vectors\r\nv1 = P2 - P1\r\nv2 = P3 - P1\r\n\r\n# Cross product to find the normal vector\r\nn = np.cross(v1, v2)\r\n\r\n# Equation of the plane: A(x - x1) + B(y - y1) + C(z - z1) = 0\r\nA, B, C = n\r\nD = -np.dot(n, P1)\r\n\r\n# Normalize the equation to ensure gcd(|A|, |B|, |C|, |D|) = 1\r\ncoefficients = [A, B, C, D]\r\ngcd_all = reduce(gcd, map(abs, coefficients))\r\nnormalized_coefficients = [int(x // gcd_all) for", "To find the equation of the plane containing the points \\(P(0, -1, -1)\\), \\(Q(-4, 4, 4)\\), and \\(R(4, 5, 1)\\), we can use the following steps:\n\n1. **Find two vectors in the plane**: We can use the points to form two vectors that lie in the plane. For example, we can use the vectors \\(\\vec{PQ} = Q - P = (-4 - 0, 4 - (-1), 4 - (-1)) = (-4, 5, 5)\\) and \\(\\vec{PR} = R - P = (4 - 0, 5 - (-1), 1 - (-1)) = (4, 6, 2)\\).\n\n2. **Find the normal vector to the plane**: The normal vector \\(\\vec{n}\\) to the plane can be found by taking the cross product of \\(\\vec{PQ}\\) and \\(\\vec{PR}\\). The cross product of two vectors \\((a_1, a_2, a_3)\\) and \\((b_1, b_2, b_3)\\) is given by:\n   \\[\n   \\vec{n} = \\vec{PQ} \\times \\vec{PR} = \\begin{vmatrix}\n   \\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\\n   -4 & 5 & 5 \\\\\n   4 & 6 & 2\n   \\end{vmatrix}\n   = \\mathbf{i}(5 \\cdot 2 - 5 \\cdot 6) - \\mathbf{j}(-4 \\cdot 2 - 5 \\cdot 4) + \\mathbf{k}(-4 \\cdot 6 - 5 \\cdot 4)\n   = \\mathbf{i}(-30) - \\mathbf{j}(-28) + \\mathbf{k}(-44)\n   = (-3