 of both 100 and 150\nnum_two_digit_factors = len(two_digit_factors)\nprint(num_two_digit_factors)\n```\n```output\n3\n```\nThe number of positive two-digit integers that are factors of both 100 and 150 is \\(\\boxed{3}\\).", "To determine how many positive two-digit integers are factors of both 100 and 150, we need to find the common factors of 100 and 150 that are two-digit numbers. Here are the steps to solve this problem:\n\n1. Find the greatest common divisor (GCD) of 100 and 150.\n2. Identify all the factors of the GCD.\n3. Count how many of these factors are two-digit numbers.\n\nLet's start by finding the GCD of 100 and 150. The prime factorizations of 100 and 150 are:\n- \\(100 = 2^2 \\times 5^2\\)\n- \\(150 = 2 \\times 3 \\times 5^2\\)\n\nThe GCD is the product of the lowest power of each prime factor appearing in the factorizations of both numbers:\n- \\( \\text{GCD}(100, 150) = 2 \\times 5^2 = 50 \\)\n\nNext, we need to find all the factors of 50 and count how many of them are two-digit numbers. The factors of 50 are:\n- \\(1, 2, 5, 10, 25, 50\\)\n\nAmong these, the two-digit factors are 10, 25, and 50. So, there are 3 two-digit factors.\n\nLet's write the Python code to confirm this.\n```python\nimport math\n\n# Function to find the factors of a number\ndef find_factors(n):\n    factors = []\n    for i in range(1, n + 1):\n        if n % i == 0:\n            factors.append(i)\n    return factors\n\n# Find the GCD of 100 and 150\ngcd = math.gcd(100, 150)\n\n# Find all factors of the GCD\nfactors_of_gcd = find_factors(gcd)\n\n# Filter out the two-digit factors\ntwo_digit_factors = [factor for factor in factors_of_gcd if 10 <= factor < 100]\n\n# Count the number of two-digit factors\nnum_two_digit_factors = len(two_digit_factors)\nprint(num_two_digit_factors)\n```\n```output\n3\n```\nThe number of positive two-digit integers that are factors of both 100 and 150 is \\(\\boxed{3}\\).", "To determine how many positive two-digit integers are factors of both 10