 the two numbers when added together must be 0. This can happen in the following cases:\n1. Both numbers have a remainder of 0 when divided by 3 (i.e., both are 3 or both are 11).\n2. One number has a remainder of 1 and the other has a remainder of 2 (i.e., one is 7 or 13 and the other is 2, 5, or 11).\n\nLet's count the number of favorable outcomes:\n- There are \\(1 \\times 1 = 1\\) way for both numbers to be 3.\n- There are \\(1 \\times 1 = 1\\) way for both numbers to be 11.\n- There are \\(2 \\times 3 = 6\\) ways for one number to be 7 or 13 and the other to be 2, 5, or 11.\n\nSo, there are \\(1 + 1 + 6 = 8\\) favorable outcomes out of a total of \\(6 \\times 6 = 36\\) possible outcomes. Therefore, the probability that the sum is divisible by 3 is \\( \\frac{8}{36} = \\frac{2}{9} \\).\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\nfrom itertools import product\n\n# First six prime numbers\nprimes = [2, 3, 5, 7, 11, 13]\n\n# Generate all possible pairs of numbers\npairs = list(product(primes, repeat=2))\n\n# Count the number of pairs whose sum is even\neven_sum_count = sum(1 for pair in pairs if (pair[0] + pair[1]) % 2 == 0)\n\n# Count the number of pairs whose sum is divisible by 3\ndivisible_by_3_count = sum(1 for pair in pairs if (pair[0] + pair[1]) % 3 == 0)\n\n# Total number of pairs\ntotal_pairs = len(pairs)\n\n# Probabilities\nprob_even_sum = even_sum_count / total_pairs\nprob_divisible_by_3 = divisible_by_3_count / total_pairs\n\nprint(((prob_even_sum, prob_divisible_by_3)))\n```\n```output\n(0.7222222222222222, 0.2777777777777778)\n```\nThe probabilities we calculated are:\n\n1. The probability that the sum of the numbers chosen by Paul and Jesse is even is \\( \\frac", 'A fair 6-sided die is rolled twice. What is the probability that the first number that comes up is greater than or equal to the second number? \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed