range the 7 units**: There are 7! (7 factorial) ways to arrange these 7 units.\n3. **Arrange the 2 boys within their "block"**: There are 2! (2 factorial) ways to arrange the 2 boys within their "block."\n\nSo, the total number of arrangements is \\(7! \\times 2!\\).\n\nLet\'s calculate this using Python to ensure accuracy.\n```python\nimport math\n\n# Calculate 7! (7 factorial)\nfactorial_7 = math.factorial(7)\n\n# Calculate 2! (2 factorial)\nfactorial_2 = math.factorial(2)\n\n# Total number of arrangements\ntotal_arrangements = factorial_7 * factorial_2\nprint(total_arrangements)\n```\n```output\n10080\n```\nThe total number of ways to arrange 6 girls and 2 boys in a row, where the 2 boys must sit next to each other, is \\(\\boxed{10080}\\).', 'To solve this problem, we can treat the two boys as a single unit or "block" since they must sit next to each other. This means we are essentially arranging 7 units (6 girls + 1 block of 2 boys) in a row.\n\nFirst, we need to determine the number of ways to arrange these 7 units. This is simply 7! (7 factorial), which is the number of permutations of 7 distinct items.\n\nNext, we need to consider the internal arrangement of the block of 2 boys. There are 2! (2 factorial) ways to arrange the 2 boys within their block.\n\nSo, the total number of arrangements is the number of ways to arrange the 7 units multiplied by the number of ways to arrange the 2 boys within their block.\n\nLet\'s calculate this using Python.\n```python\nimport math\n\n# Number of ways to arrange 7 units (6 girls + 1 block of 2 boys)\narrangements_of_units = math.factorial(7)\n\n# Number of ways to arrange 2 boys within their block\narrangements_of_boys = math.factorial(2)\n\n# Total number of arrangements\ntotal_arrangements = arrangements_of_units * arrangements_of_boys\nprint(total_arrangements)\n```\n```output\n10080\n```\nThe total number of ways to arrange 6 girls and 2 boys in a row if the 2 boys insist on sitting next to each other is \\(\\boxed{