iguration:\n\n\\[\na + b = 1 + i, \\quad a + c = 1 - 1 = 0, \\quad a + d = 1 - i,\n\\]\n\\[\nb + c = i - 1, \\quad b + d = i - i = 0, \\quad c + d = -1 - i.\n\\]\n\nSince \\( a + c = 0 \\) and \\( b + d = 0 \\), the product \\( |(a + b)(a + c)(a + d)(b + c)(b + d)(c + d)| \\) simplifies to zero. However, this is not the only configuration. We need to consider other possible configurations that might yield a non-zero maximum value.\n\nAnother possible configuration is when \\( a, b, c, d \\) are the vertices of a rectangle in the complex plane. In this case, we can choose \\( a = 1, b = -1, c = i, d = -i \\).\n\nLet's calculate \\( |(a + b)(a + c)(a + d)(b + c)(b + d)(c + d)| \\) for this configuration:\n\n\\[\na + b = 1 - 1 = 0, \\quad a + c = 1 + i, \\quad a + d = 1 - i,\n\\]\n\\[\nb + c = -1 + i, \\quad b + d = -1 - i, \\quad c + d = i - i = 0.\n\\]\n\nAgain, since \\( a + b = 0 \\) and \\( c + d = 0 \\), the product \\( |(a + b)(a + c)(a + d)(b + c)(b + d)(c + d)| \\) simplifies to zero. This suggests that we need to consider a more general approach.\n\nA more general approach is to use the fact that \\( a, b, c, d \\) are roots of a polynomial. Since \\( a + b + c + d = 0 \\), the polynomial \\( P(z) = (z - a)(z - b)(z - c)(z - d) \\) can be written as \\( P(z) = z^4 + pz^2 + q \\), where \\( p \\) and \\( q \\) are real numbers. The roots of this polynomial are \\( a, b, c, d \\).\n\nThe product \\( |(a + b)(a + c)(a + d)(b + c)(b + d)(c + d)| \\) can be related to the discriminant of the polynomial \\( P(z) \\). The discriminant of a polynomial \\( P(z) = z^4 + pz^2 + q \\) is given by \\( \\Delta = 256q^3 - 192p^2q^2 + 128p^3q - 27p^4 \\).\n\nTo find the maximum value, we need to consider the possible values of \\( p \\) and \\( q \\). Since \\( a, b, c, d \\) are on the unit circle, we can use the fact that the sum of the roots taken two at a time is zero. This implies that \\( p = 0 \\). The polynomial then simplifies to \\( P(z) = z^4 + q \\).\n\nThe discr