$\\boxed{125}$.\nThe answer is $\\boxed{125}$.', "We will solve the problem step-by-step using Python and SymPy.\n\n1. Let's define the variables:\n   - Let \\( C \\) be the set of students taking calculus.\n   - Let \\( P \\) be the set of students taking physics.\n   - Let \\( H \\) be the set of students taking chemistry.\n   - Let \\( n(C) \\) be the number of students taking calculus.\n   - Let \\( n(P) \\) be the number of students taking physics.\n   - Let \\( n(H) \\) be the number of students taking chemistry.\n   - Let \\( n(C \\cap P) \\) be the number of students taking both calculus and physics.\n   - Let \\( n(C \\cap H) \\) be the number of students taking both calculus and chemistry.\n   - Let \\( n(P \\cap H) \\) be the number of students taking both physics and chemistry.\n   - Let \\( n(C \\cap P \\cap H) \\) be the number of students taking all three subjects.\n   - Let \\( n(\\text{none}) \\) be the number of students taking none of the subjects.\n\n2. From the problem, we know:\n   - \\( n(C \\cap P \\cap H) = 15 \\)\n   - \\( n(\\text{none}) = 15 \\)\n   - \\( n(C) = 180 \\)\n   - \\( n(C \\cap H) = 75 \\)\n   - \\( n(P \\cap H) = 75 \\)\n   - \\( n(C \\cap P) = 30 \\)\n   - The total number of students is 360.\n   - Twice as many students take chemistry as take physics, so \\( n(H) = 2 \\cdot n(P) \\).\n\n3. Using the principle of inclusion-exclusion for three sets, we have:\n   \\[\n   n(C \\cup P \\cup H) = n(C) + n(P) + n(H) - n(C \\cap P) - n(C \\cap H) - n(P \\cap H) + n(C \\cap P \\cap H)\n   \\]\n   Since 15 students take none of the subjects, the number of students taking at least one of the subjects is \\( 360 - 15 = 345 \\). So,\n   \\[\n   345 = 180 + n(P) + 2n(P) - 30 - 75 - 75 + 15\n   \\]\n   Simplifying the right-hand side, we get:\n   \\[\n   345 = 180 + 3n(P) - 165\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   345 = 15 + 3n(P)\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   330 = 3n(P)\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   n(P) = 110\n   \\]\n\nLet's implement this in Python to verify our solutio