  \\[\n   28 - 3 = 25\n   \\]\n\nThus, the value of \\(f(4)\\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{25}\n\\]\n\n### Final Answer\n\nCombining all the parts, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{-x + 7, 5, (-1, 6), 25}\n\\]', "To find the midpoint of a line segment between two points \\((x_1, y_1)\\) and \\((x_2, y_2)\\), we use the midpoint formula:\n\n\\[\n\\left( \\frac{x_1 + x_2}{2}, \\frac{y_1 + y_2}{2} \\right)\n\\]\n\nGiven the points \\((-5, 5)\\) and \\((3, 7)\\), we can substitute these values into the formula:\n\n\\[\n\\left( \\frac{-5 + 3}{2}, \\frac{5 + 7}{2} \\right)\n\\]\n\nLet's calculate this step by step using Python to ensure accuracy.\n```python\n# Define the coordinates of the points\nx1, y1 = -5, 5\nx2, y2 = 3, 7\n\n# Calculate the midpoint\nmidpoint_x = (x1 + x2) / 2\nmidpoint_y = (y1 + y2) / 2\n\nprint(((midpoint_x, midpoint_y)))\n```\n```output\n(-1.0, 6.0)\n```\nThe midpoint of the line segment between the points \\((-5, 5)\\) and \\((3, 7)\\) is \\((-1.0, 6.0)\\).\n\nSo, the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{(-1.0, 6.0)}\n\\]", 'The distance between the points $(1,2)$ and $(3,a)$ is $2\\sqrt{2}$. Find $a$.  \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\