s that Yann and Camille can order, given that they refuse to order the same dish, is \\(\\boxed{90}\\).", 'Let\'s solve each problem step by step using Python code.\n\n### Problem 1: Number of ways to arrange the letters in the word "ALGEBRA"\n\nThe word "ALGEBRA" has 7 letters where \'A\' appears twice. The number of distinct permutations of the word "ALGEBRA" is given by the formula for permutations of a multiset:\n\\[\n\\frac{7!}{2!}\n\\]\nwhere 7! is the factorial of 7 (the total number of letters), and 2! accounts for the repetition of the letter \'A\'.\n\n### Problem 2: Number of different combinations of meals for Yann and Camille\n\nThere are 10 items on the menu. Yann can choose any one of the 10 items, and Camille can choose any one of the remaining 9 items (since they refuse to order the same dish). The number of different combinations of meals is:\n\\[\n10 \\times 9\n\\]\n\nLet\'s implement this in Python to get the final answers.\n```python\nimport math\n\n# Problem 1: Number of ways to arrange the letters in the word "ALGEBRA"\n# The word "ALGEBRA" has 7 letters with \'A\' appearing twice\ntotal_permutations = math.factorial(7) // math.factorial(2)\n\n# Problem 2: Number of different combinations of meals for Yann and Camille\nmenu_items = 10\ncombinations_of_meals = menu_items * (menu_items - 1)\n\nprint(((total_permutations, combinations_of_meals)))\n```\n```output\n(2520, 90)\n```\nThe number of ways to arrange the letters in the word "ALGEBRA" is \\(\\boxed{2520}\\).\n\nThe number of different combinations of meals that Yann and Camille can order, given that they refuse to order the same dish, is \\(\\boxed{90}\\).', "There are 10 items on the menu. Yann and Camille each choose one dish, and they cannot choose the same dish. The order in which they choose matters (Yann choosing chicken and Camille choosing fish is different from Yann choosing fish and Camille choosing chicken).\n\nTo solve this, we can use the following steps:\n1. Yann can choose a