t the polynomial \\(x^{1997} - 1 = 0\\) has roots \\(1, \\omega, \\omega^2, \\ldots, \\omega^{1996}\\). The polynomial can be factored as\n\\[\nx^{1997} - 1 = (x - 1)(x - \\omega)(x - \\omega^2) \\cdots (x - \\omega^{1996}).\n\\]\nBy considering the polynomial \\(P(x) = \\frac{x^{1997} - 1}{x - 1} = x^{1996} + x^{1995} + \\cdots + x + 1\\), we can find the sum of the reciprocals of the roots of \\(P(x)\\).\n\nThe roots of \\(P(x)\\) are \\(\\omega, \\omega^2, \\ldots, \\omega^{1996}\\). We want to find the sum\n\\[\n\\sum_{k=1}^{1996} \\frac{1}{1 + \\omega^k}.\n\\]\nTo do this, we can use the fact that\n\\[\n\\sum_{k=1}^{1996} \\frac{1}{1 + \\omega^k} = \\frac{1}{2} \\sum_{k=1}^{1996} \\left( \\frac{1}{1 + \\omega^k} + \\frac{1}{1 + \\omega^{-k}} \\right).\n\\]\nSince \\(\\omega^{1997} = 1\\), we have \\(\\omega^{-k} = \\omega^{1997-k}\\), and thus\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{1 + \\omega^k} + \\frac{1}{1 + \\omega^{-k}} = \\frac{1}{1 + \\omega^k} + \\frac{1}{1 + \\omega^{1997-k}} = \\frac{1}{1 + \\omega^k} + \\frac{1}{1 + \\omega^k} = \\frac{2}{1 + \\omega^k}.\n\\]\nTherefore,\n\\[\n\\sum_{k=1}^{1996} \\frac{1}{1 + \\omega^k} = \\frac{1}{2} \\sum_{k=1}^{1996} \\frac{2}{1 + \\omega^k} = \\sum_{k=1}^{1996} \\frac{1}{1 + \\omega^k}.\n\\]\nWe can now use the fact that the sum of the reciprocals of the roots of \\(P(x)\\) is equal to the negative of the coefficient of \\(x^{1995}\\) in \\(P(x)\\) divided by the constant term, which is 1996. Therefore,\n\\[\n\\sum_{k=1}^{1996} \\frac{1}{1 + \\omega^k} = \\frac{1996}{2} = 998.\n\\]\nAdding the term for \\(k=1997\\), which is \\(\\frac{1}{1 + 1} = \\frac{1}{2}\\), we get\n\\[\n\\sum_{k=1}^{1997} \\frac{1}{1 + \\omega^k} = 998 + \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{1997}{2}.\n\\]\nThus, the final answer is\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{1997}{2}}.\n\\]", "To solve the problem, let's first understand the properties of the roots of unity. The equation $\\omega^{1997} = 1$ implies that $\\omega$ is a 1997th root of unity. The 1997th roots of unity are the complex 