lifying, we get:\n\\[\n7 = 14 \\sin \\theta \\implies \\sin \\theta = \\frac{1}{2}.\n\\]\n\nThe angle \\(\\theta\\) for which \\(\\sin \\theta = \\frac{1}{2}\\) is \\(\\theta = 30^\\circ\\) or \\(\\theta = 150^\\circ\\). Since we are asked for the smallest possible angle, the answer is \\(\\theta = 30^\\circ\\).\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code to ensure the calculations are accurate.\n```python\nimport numpy as np\n\n# Given magnitudes of vectors a and b\nnorm_a = 2\nnorm_b = 7\n\n# Given cross product vector\ncross_product = np.array([3, 2, 6])\n\n# Calculate the magnitude of the cross product\nmagnitude_cross_product = np.linalg.norm(cross_product)\n\n# Calculate sin(theta)\nsin_theta = magnitude_cross_product / (norm_a * norm_b)\n\n# Calculate the angle theta in degrees\ntheta = np.degrees(np.arcsin(sin_theta))\nprint(theta)\n```\n```output\n30.000000000000004\n```\nThe smallest possible angle between the vectors \\(\\mathbf{a}\\) and \\(\\mathbf{b}\\) is \\(\\boxed{30}\\) degrees.", 'The smallest possible angle between two vectors \\(\\mathbf{a}\\) and \\(\\mathbf{b}\\) can be found using the properties of the cross product and the dot product.\n\nGiven:\n\\[\n\\|\\mathbf{a}\\| = 2, \\quad \\|\\mathbf{b}\\| = 7, \\quad \\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b} = \\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 6 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nThe magnitude of the cross product \\(\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b}\\) is given by:\n\\[\n\\|\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b}\\| = \\|\\mathbf{a}\\| \\|\\mathbf{b}\\| \\sin(\\theta)\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\theta\\) is the angle between \\(\\mathbf{a}\\) and \\(\\mathbf{b}\\).\n\nFirst, we calculate the magnitude of \\(\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b}\\):\n\\[\n\\|\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b}\\| = \\sqrt{3^2 + 2^2 + 6^2} = \\sqrt{9 + 4 + 36} = \\sqrt{49} = 7\n\\]\n\nUsing the given magnitudes of \\(\\mathbf{a}\\) and \\(\\mathbf{b}\\), we can write:\n\\[\n7 = 2 \\cdot 7 \\cdot \\sin(\\theta)\n\\]\n\nSimplifying this equation:\n\\[\n7 = 14 \\sin(\\theta)\n\\