 -49(x^2 + 10x + 25) = -49x^2 - 490x - 1225, \\]\n\\[ 623(x + 5) = 623x + 3115. \\]\n\nCombining these, we get:\n\\[ g(x) = x^3 + 15x^2 + 75x + 125 - 49x^2 - 490x - 1225 + 623x + 3115 - 2015. \\]\n\\[ g(x) = x^3 + (15 - 49)x^2 + (75 - 490 + 623)x + (125 - 1225 + 3115 - 2015). \\]\n\\[ g(x) = x^3 - 34x^2 + 208x + 1000. \\]\n\nThe sum of the roots of a polynomial \\(ax^n + bx^{n-1} + \\cdots + k\\) is given by \\(-\\frac{b}{a}\\), where \\(a\\) is the coefficient of \\(x^n\\) and \\(b\\) is the coefficient of \\(x^{n-1}\\). For the polynomial \\(g(x) = x^3 - 34x^2 + 208x + 1000\\), the sum of the roots is:\n\\[ -\\frac{-34}{1} = 34. \\]\n\nSo, the sum of the roots of \\(g(x)\\) is \\(\\boxed{34}\\).\n\nLet's verify this with Python code to ensure the result is accurate.\n```python\nfrom sympy import symbols, expand\n\n# Define the variable\nx = symbols('x')\n\n# Define the function f(x)\nf = x**3 - 49*x**2 + 623*x - 2015\n\n# Define the function g(x) = f(x + 5)\ng = f.subs(x, x + 5)\n\n# Expand g(x)\ng_expanded = expand(g)\n\n# Extract the coefficients\ncoefficients = g_expanded.as_coefficients_dict()\n\n# The sum of the roots of a polynomial a*x^3 + b*x^2 + c*x + d is -b/a\nsum_of_roots = -coefficients[x**2] / coefficients[x**3]\nprint(sum_of_roots)\n```", 'Let $f(x) = x^3 - 49x^2 + 623x - 2015.$  The roots of $f$ are $r, s,$ and $t.$  Let $g(x) = x^3 + 5x^2 + 2x + 3.$  Find $g(r) + g(s) + g(t).$  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\bo