30 and 40 but not a multiple of 16.\n\nLet's confirm this with Python and SymPy:\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Problem 1\n# Convert repeating decimals to fractions\nfrac_7 = sp.Rational(7, 9)\nfrac_4 = sp.Rational(4, 9)\nfrac_2 = sp.Rational(2, 9)\n\n# Compute the expression\nresult_1 = frac_7 - frac_4 + frac_2\n\n# Problem 2\n# Find the LCM of 30 and 40\nlcm_30_40 = sp.lcm(30, 40)\n\n# Check if the LCM is a multiple of 16\nis_multiple_of_16 = lcm_30_40 % 16 == 0\n\n# If it is not a multiple of 16, it is the answer\nif not is_multiple_of_16:\n    result_2 = lcm_30_40\nelse:\n    # Find the next multiple of 30 and 40 that is not a multiple of 16\n    multiple = lcm_30_40\n    while multiple % 16 == 0:\n        multiple += sp.lcm(30, 40)\n    result_2 = multiple\n\nprint(result_1)\nprint(result_2)\n```\n```output\n5/9\n120\n```\nThe output confirms our calculations. Here are the final answers:\n\n1. The value of \\(0.\\overline{7} - 0.\\overline{4} + 0.\\overline{2}\\) is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{5}{9}}\\).\n2. The smallest positive integer that is a multiple of both 30 and 40 but not a multiple of 16 is \\(\\boxed{120}\\).", 'What is the smallest positive multiple of $23$ whose digits are all the same? \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{