}{ab} \\).\n\n   The area of the parallelogram is the product of the vertical distance and the horizontal distance:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Area} = |d - c| \\cdot \\frac{c|b - a|}{ab} = 18\n   \\]\n\n2. **Determine the area of the second parallelogram:**\n   Similarly, the vertical distance between \\(y = ax + c\\) and \\(y = ax - d\\) is \\(|c + d|\\), and the vertical distance between \\(y = bx + c\\) and \\(y = bx - d\\) is also \\(|c + d|\\). The horizontal distance between the lines \\(y = ax + c\\) and \\(y = bx + c\\) (or \\(y = ax - d\\) and \\(y = bx - d\\)) is the same as before, \\(\\frac{c|b - a|}{ab}\\).\n\n   The area of the parallelogram is:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Area} = |c + d| \\cdot \\frac{c|b - a|}{ab} = 72\n   \\]\n\n3. **Set up the equations:**\n   From the first parallelogram, we have:\n   \\[\n   |d - c| \\cdot \\frac{c|b - a|}{ab} = 18\n   \\]\n   From the second parallelogram, we have:\n   \\[\n   |c + d| \\cdot \\frac{c|b - a|}{ab} = 72\n   \\]\n\n4. **Solve the equations:**\n   Let's assume \\(d > c\\) for simplicity, so \\(|d - c| = d - c\\) and \\(|c + d| = c + d\\). We can divide the second equation by the first equation to eliminate the common term \\(\\frac{c|b - a|}{ab}\\):\n   \\[\n   \\frac{c + d}{d - c} = 4\n   \\]\n   Solving for \\(d\\) in terms of \\(c\\):\n   \\[\n   c + d = 4(d - c) \\implies c + d = 4d - 4c \\implies 5c = 3d \\implies d = \\frac{5c}{3}\n   \\]\n   Since \\(d\\) must be an integer, \\(c\\) must be a multiple of 3. Let's try the smallest possible value for \\(c\\), which is 3. Then \\(d = 5\\).\n\n   Substituting \\(c = 3\\) and \\(d = 5\\) into the first equation:\n   \\[\n   (5 - 3) \\cdot \\frac{3|b - a|}{ab} = 18 \\implies 2 \\cdot \\frac{3|b - a|}{ab} = 18 \\implies \\frac{6|b - a|}{ab} = 18 \\implies |b - a| = 3ab\n   \\]\n   Since \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are positive integers, let's try the smallest possible values for \\(a\\) and \\(b\\). If \\(a = 1\\) and \\(b = 4\\), then", "To solve this problem, we need to understand the relationship between the coefficients of the lines and the area of the parallelogram they form. The area of a parallelogram formed by the lines \\(y = ax + c\\), \\(y = ax + d\\), \\(y = bx + c\\), and \\(y = bx + d\\) can be determined by the distance between the parallel lines and the angle between them.\n\nThe distance between the lines \\(y = ax + c\\) and \\(y = ax + d\\) is given by \\(|c - d|\\). Similarly, the distance between the lines \\(y = bx + c\\) and \\(y = bx + d\\) is also \\(|c - d|\\). The area of the parallelogram is the product of these distances and the sine of the angle between the lines. The angle between the lines \\(y = ax + c\\) and \\(y = bx + c\\) is given by \\(\\theta = \\arctan(a) - \\arctan(b)\\). The sine of this angle is \\(\\left|\\frac{a - b}{1 + ab}\\right|\\).\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram is given by:\n\\[ \\text{Area} = |c - d| \\cdot |c - d| \\cdot \\left|\\frac{a - b}{1 + ab}\\right| = |c - d|^2 \\cdot \\left|\\frac{a - b}{1 + ab}\\right| \\]\n\nGiven that the area of the first parallelogram is 18 and the area of the second parallelogram is 72, we can set up the following equations:\n\\[ |c - d|^2 \\cdot \\left|\\frac{a - b}{1 + ab}\\right| = 18 \\]\n\\[ |c - d|^2 \\cdot \\left|\\frac{a - b}{1 + ab}\\right| = 72 \\]\n\nSince the expressions involving \\(a\\), \\(b\\), \\(c\\), and \\(d\\) are the same for both equations, we can deduce that the only difference is in the constant term \\(|c - d|^2\\). Let's denote \\(|c - d|^2\\) by \\(k\\). Then we have:\n\\[ k \\cdot \\left|\\frac{a - b}{1 + ab}\\right| = 18 \\]\n\\[ k \\cdot \\left|\\frac{a - b}{1 + ab}\\right| = 72 \\]\n\nThis implies that \\(k = 18 \\cdot \\left|\\frac{1 + ab}{a - b}\\right| = 72 \\cdot \\left|\\frac{1 + ab}{a - b}\\right|\\). Therefore, we can conclude that:\n\\[ 18 \\cdot \\left|\\frac{1 + ab}{a - b}\\right| = 72 \\cdot \\left|\\frac{1 + ab}{a - b}\\right| \\]\n\\[ \\left|\\frac{1 + ab}{a - b}\\right| = \\frac{72}{18} = 4 \\]