nd eigenvectors of \\(\\mathbf{A}\\). The eigenvalues \\(\\lambda\\) are the roots of the characteristic polynomial \\(\\det(\\mathbf{A} - \\lambda \\mathbf{I}) = 0\\), where \\(\\mathbf{I}\\) is the identity matrix. The characteristic polynomial is:\n\\[\n\\det \\begin{pmatrix} 3 - \\lambda & 7 \\\\ -1 & -2 - \\lambda \\end{pmatrix} = (3 - \\lambda)(-2 - \\lambda) + 7 = \\lambda^2 - \\lambda + 1.\n\\]\nThe eigenvalues are the roots of \\(\\lambda^2 - \\lambda + 1 = 0\\), which are:\n\\[\n\\lambda = \\frac{1 \\pm \\sqrt{-3}}{2} = \\frac{1 \\pm i\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n\\]\nThese eigenvalues are complex, so the matrix \\(\\mathbf{A}\\) cannot be diagonalized over the reals. However, we can use the fact that \\(\\mathbf{A}\\) satisfies its own characteristic polynomial, i.e., \\(\\mathbf{A}^2 - \\mathbf{A} + \\mathbf{I} = \\mathbf{0}\\). This means that \\(\\mathbf{A}^2 = \\mathbf{A} - \\mathbf{I}\\).\n\nWe can use this relation to find higher powers of \\(\\mathbf{A}\\):\n\\[\n\\mathbf{A}^3 = \\mathbf{A} \\cdot \\mathbf{A}^2 = \\mathbf{A} (\\mathbf{A} - \\mathbf{I}) = \\mathbf{A}^2 - \\mathbf{A} = (\\mathbf{A} - \\mathbf{I}) - \\mathbf{A} = -\\mathbf{I}.\n\\]\nThus, we have \\(\\mathbf{A}^3 = -\\mathbf{I}\\), and this implies that \\(\\mathbf{A}^6 = \\mathbf{I}\\). Therefore, any power of \\(\\mathbf{A}\\) can be reduced modulo 6. Specifically:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{A}^{27} = (\\mathbf{A}^6)^4 \\cdot \\mathbf{A}^3 = \\mathbf{I}^4 \\cdot (-\\mathbf{I}) = -\\mathbf{I},\n\\]\n\\[\n\\mathbf{A}^{31} = (\\mathbf{A}^6)^5 \\cdot \\mathbf{A} = \\mathbf{I}^5 \\cdot \\mathbf{A} = \\mathbf{A},\n\\]\n\\[\n\\mathbf{A}^{40} = (\\mathbf{A}^6)^6 \\cdot \\mathbf{A}^4 = \\mathbf{I}^6 \\cdot \\mathbf{A}^4 = \\mathbf{A}^4.\n\\]\nTo find \\(\\mathbf{A}^4\\), we use the relation \\(\\mathbf{A}^2 = \\mathbf{A} - \\mathbf{I}\\):\n\\[\n\\mathbf{A}^4 = (\\mathbf{A}^2)^2 = (\\mathbf{A} - \\mathbf{I})^2 = \\mathbf{A}^2 - 2\\mathbf{A} + \\mathbf{I} = (\\mathbf{A} - \\mathbf{I}) - 2\\mathbf{A} + \\mathbf{I} = -\