# Define the sum of the angles\nsum_of_angles = 180\n\n# Print the result\nprint(sum_of_angles)\n```\n```output\n180\n```\nThe sum of the measures of \\(\\angle J\\) and \\(\\angle H\\) is \\(\\boxed{180}\\) degrees.", "To solve this problem, we need to use some geometric properties of circles and tangents. Here's the step-by-step reasoning:\n\n1. **Identify the Geometry**: In the given problem, \\(HO\\) and \\(HN\\) are tangent to the circle at points \\(O\\) and \\(N\\), respectively. The point \\(J\\) is the center of the circle. The tangents \\(HO\\) and \\(HN\\) form right angles with the radii \\(JO\\) and \\(JN\\), respectively.\n\n2. **Sum of Angles in Quadrilateral**: The quadrilateral \\(HONJ\\) has four angles. Since \\(HO\\) and \\(HN\\) are tangents, \\(\\angle HOJ = 90^\\circ\\) and \\(\\angle HNJ = 90^\\circ\\). The angle \\(\\angle J\\) is the central angle subtended by the arc \\(ON\\), and \\(\\angle H\\) is the angle formed by the tangents \\(HO\\) and \\(HN\\).\n\n3. **Calculate the Sum of Angles**: The sum of the angles in any quadrilateral is \\(360^\\circ\\). So, we have:\n   \\[\n   \\angle HOJ + \\angle HNJ + \\angle J + \\angle H = 360^\\circ\n   \\]\n   Substituting the known angles:\n   \\[\n   90^\\circ + 90^\\circ + \\angle J + \\angle H = 360^\\circ\n   \\]\n   Simplifying, we get:\n   \\[\n   180^\\circ + \\angle J + \\angle H = 360^\\circ\n   \\]\n   Therefore:\n   \\[\n   \\angle J + \\angle H = 180^\\circ\n   \\]\n\nSo, the number of degrees in the sum of \\(m\\angle J\\) and \\(m\\angle H\\) is \\(180\\).\n\nLet's confirm this with Python and sympy:\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the angles\nangle_J, angle_H = sp.symbols('angle_J angle_H')\n\n# Equation based on the sum of angles in the quadrilateral\nequation = sp.Eq(90 + 90 + angle_J + angle_H, 360)\n\n# Solve for the sum of angle_J and angle_H\nsolution = sp.solve(equation, angle_J + angle_H)[0]\nprint(solution)\n```\n```output\n180\n```\nThe number of degrees in 