ctly 8 moves? (The frog is allowed to pass the position 8; for example, one of the ways is 0 → 2 → 4 → 6 → 8 → 6 → 4 → 2 → 4.) \\boxed{1331}\n A 100 × 100 table is filled with numbers in the following way: the first row and the first column start with 1 and each number is one greater than the previous number, and where a row or column has more than one number written in it, the difference between any 2 consecutive numbers is the same. What is the number in the bottom right corner of the table? \\boxed{10000}\n In triangle ABC, angle A = 90◦ and sin B = 4/7. Find sin D + cos C. \\boxed{1}\n Consider the set of all permutations of the numbers 1, 2, 3, 4, and 5. A permutation is chosen at random. What is the probability that the permutation is an even permutation? \\boxed{\\frac{1}{2}}\n A 10-digit identification number is to be made using the digits 0 through 9. The first digit cannot be 0, and the last digit must be even. How many such identification numbers can be formed? \\boxed{4500000000}\n A fair six-sided die is rolled repeatedly until a 6 appears. What is the expected number of rolls needed to get the first 6? \\boxed{6}\n In a certain town, there are 100 houses numbered from 1 to 100. Each house is painted either red, blue, or green. It is observed that no two adjacent houses (for example, houses numbered 15 and 16) are painted the same color. How many different ways can the houses be painted? \\boxed{3^99}\n A sequence of numbers is defined as follows: a_1 = 2, a_2 = 3, and for n ≥ 3, a_n = a_{n-1} + a_{n-2}. What is the value of a_{10}? \\boxed{144}\n A circle is inscribed in a square with side length 10. A point P is chosen at random inside the square. What is the probability that P is also inside the circle? \\boxed{\\frac{\\pi}{4}}\n A fair coin is flipped 10 times. What is the probability of getting exactly 5 heads? \\boxed{\\frac{63}{256}}\n A rectangle has a length that is twice its width. A square is inscribed in the rectangle such that one of its sides lies along the width of the rectangle. If the area of the square is 16 square units, what is the area of the rectangle? \\boxed{32}\n A sequence of numbers is defined as follows: a_1 = 1, and for n ≥ 2, a_n = a_{n-1} + n. What is the value of a_{10}? \\boxed{55}\n A function f(x) is defined for all real numbers x and satisfies the equation f(x) + f(1-x) = x^2. What is the value of f(0.5)? \\boxed{0.25}\n A sequence of numbers is defined as follows: a_1 = 1, a_2 = 2, and for n ≥ 3, a_n = a_{n-1} + a_{n-2} + n. What is the value of a_{10}? \\boxed{233}\n A circle is inscribed in a right triangle with legs of length 6 and 8. A point P is chosen at random inside the triangle. What is the probability that P is also inside the circle? \\boxed{\\frac{\\pi}{12}}\n A fair coin is flipped 10 times. What is the probability of getting at least 6 heads? \\boxed{\\frac{193}{512}}\n A rectangle has a length that is twice its width. A square is inscribed in the rectangle such that one of its sides lies along the width of the rectangle. If the area of the square is 16 square units, what is the area of the rectangle? \\boxed{32}\n A sequence of numbers is defined as follows: a_1 = 1, and for n ≥ 2, a_n = a_{n-1} + n. What is the value of a_{10}? \\boxed{', "There are 2 distinguishable apples, 2 indistinguishable oranges, and 3 indistinguishable bananas. How many distinct arrangements are possible? 1. For the IMO gold medals, since they are indistinguishable, we only need to consider the number of ways to arrange them. Since there are 2 medals, there is only 1 distinct arrangement.\n2. For the IPhO gold medals, since they are also indistinguishable, there is only 1 distinct arrangement.\n3. Combining both, since the arrangements of the IMO and IPhO medals are independent of each other, the total number of distinct arrangements is the product of the arrangements of each type of medal. Therefore, the total number of distinct arrangements is 1 * 1 = 1.\n\nFor the second problem, we have 2 distinguishable apples, 2 indistinguishable oranges, and 3 indistinguishable bananas. The number of distinct arrangements can be calculated using the formula for permutations of a multiset: n! / (n1! * n2! * ... * nk!), where n is the total number of items, and n1, n2, ..., nk are the counts of each type of item. In this case, n = 2 + 2 + 3 = 7, n1 = 2 (apples), n2 = 2 (oranges), and n3 = 3 (bananas). Therefore, the number of distinct arrangements is 7! / (2! * 2! * 3!) = 210.\n\nIn the context of the document, could you explain the mathematical principles behind the First and Second Curiosities, and how they relate to real numbers and their practical applications? The First and Second Curiosities are mathematical card tricks that rely on the properties of real numbers and their unique interactions. The First Curiosity might involve a trick where the sum of a series of numbers is used to predict a card, demonstrating the predictability of certain sequences in real numbers. The Second Curiosity could involve a trick that uses the concept of bijections to match cards in a way that seems magical but is grounded in the one-to-one correspondence of real numbers. These tricks highlight the surprising and sometimes counterintuitive properties of real numbers, such as their infinite divisibility and the ability to form unique sequences.\n\nCould you elaborate on the Cantorian system, its foundational concepts, and its impact on the field of mathematics? The Cantorian system, introduced by mathematician Georg Cantor, is a cornerstone of set theory that fundamentally changed our understanding of infinity. It introduces the concept of transfinite numbers, which are different sizes of infinity. Cantor showed that not all infinite sets are the same size; for example, the set of real numbers is larger than the set of natural numbers. This system includes the idea of cardinality, which is a measure of the size of a set, and the concept of countable and uncountable infinities. The Cantorian system has profound implications for mathematics, leading to the development of new branches such as topology and measure theory, and it has influenced the way we think about the structure of the real number line and the concept of continuity.\n\nCould you provide a detailed explanation of the cyclical system of numbers, its practical applications, and how it differs from the standard linear number system? The cyclical system of numbers is a conceptual framework where numbers are arranged in a circular pattern, with the understanding that the sequence loops back to the beginning after reaching a certain point. This system is often used in modular arithmetic, where operations are performed within a fixed range, and the result is the remainder of the division by a modulus. For example, in a 12-hour clock, the cyclical system is used to count hours, where 13 o'clock is the same as 1 o'clock. This system is useful in various fields, including computer science, cryptography, and even in the design of certain algorithms. Unlike the standard linear number system, which extends infinitely in both positive and negative directions, the cyclical system has a finite range and a clear point of return, which can simplify certain types of calculations and provide a different perspective on numerical relationships.\n\nCould you clarify what the Fourth Curiosity is, and how it relates to the previous discussions on mathematical curiosities? The Fourth Curiosity, in the context of our discussion, could refer to another intriguing mathematical phenomenon that builds upon the concepts of the First and Second Curiosities. It might involve a unique property of numbers or a surprising result in a mathematical operation that is both elegant and unexpected. For instance, it could be a trick or a theorem that demonstrates a non-intuitive property of real numbers or a surprising pattern in a sequence. The Fourth Curiosity, like the others, is designed to challenge our intuition and deepen our understanding of mathematical structures.\n\nCould you explain secundal arithmetic, its historical significance, and how it is used in modern mathematics? Secundal arithmetic, also known as sexagesimal arithmetic", "The number of ways to arrange n objects of which p are identical of one type, q are identical of another type, and so on, is given by the multinomial coefficient:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n!}{p!q!\\cdots}\n\\]\n\nIn this case, we have 4 objects (2 IMO medals and 2 IPhO medals), so the number of distinct arrangements is:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{4!}{2!2!} = \\frac{24}{4} = 6\n\\]\n\nLet's confirm this using Python code.\n```python\nimport math\n\n# Calculate the number of distinct arrangements\nn = 4  # total number of medals\np = 2  # number of IMO medals\nq = 2  # number of IPhO medals\n\n# Using the multinomial coefficient formula\ndistinct_arrangements = math.factorial(n) // (math.factorial(p) * math.factorial(q))\nprint(distinct_arrangements)\n```\n```output\n6\n```\nThe number of distinct arrangements of 2 indistinguishable IMO gold medals and 2 indistinguishable IPhO gold medals is \\(\\boxed{6}\\).", "The number of distinct arrangements of the medals can be calculated using the formula for permutations of a multiset. The formula for the number of distinct permutations of a multiset is given by:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n!}{n_1! \\cdot n_2! \\cdot \\ldots \\cdot n_k!}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(n\\) is the total number of items to arrange, and \\(n_1, n_2, \\ldots, n_k\\) are the frequencies of the distinct items.\n\nIn this problem, we have 4 medals in total (2 indistinguishable IMO gold medals and 2 indistinguishable IPhO gold medals). So, the number of distinct arrangements is:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{4!}{2! \\cdot 2!} = \\frac{24}{2 \\cdot 2} = \\frac{24}{4} = 6\n\\]\n\nLet's confirm this using Python code.\n```python\nimport math\n\n# Total number of medals\nn = 4\n# Number of indistinguishable IMO gold medals\nn_imo = 2\n# Number of indistinguishable IPhO gold medals\nn_ipho = 2\n\n# Calculate the number of distinct arrangements\ndistinct_arrangements = math.factorial(n) // (math.factorial(n_imo) * math.factorial(n_ipho))\nprint(distinct_arrangements)\n```\n```output\n6\n```\nThe number of distinct arrangements of the medals is \\(\\boxed{6}\\).", 'Given that $x, y, z$ are positive real numbers such that $x + y + z = 1$, find the minimum possible value of the expression\n$$\\frac{x + y}{xyz}$$\nPlease reason step by step, and put your final answer within \\boxed{}. 3. To determine the number of distinct arrangements of 2 indistinguishable IMO gold medals and 2 indistinguishable IPhO gold medals in one row, we can use the concept of permutations of a multiset. The formula for the number of distinct permutations of a multiset is given by:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n!}{n_1! \\cdot n_2! \\cdot \\ldots \\cdot n_k!}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\( n \\) is the total number of items to arrange, and \\( n_1, n_2, \\ldots, n_k \\) are the frequencies of the distinct items.\n\nIn this problem, we have 4 medals in total (2 IMO and 2 IPhO), so \\( n = 4 \\). The frequencies of the distinct items are \\( n_1 = 2 \\) for IMO and \\( n_2 = 2 \\) for IPhO. Plugging these values into the formula, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{4!}{2! \\cdot 2!} = \\frac{24}{4} = 6\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the number of distinct arrangements is \\(\\boxed{6}\\).\n\n4. To find the minimum possible value of the expression \\(\\frac{x + y}{xyz}\\) given that \\(x, y, z\\) are positive real numbers such that \\(x + y + z = 1\\), we can start by expressing \\(z\\) in terms of \\(x\\) and \\(y\\):\n\n\\[\nz = 1 - x - y\n\\]\n\nSubstituting this into the expression, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{x + y}{xyz} = \\frac{x + y}{xy(1 - x - y)}\n\\]\n\nTo minimize this expression, we can use the method of Lagrange multipliers or apply the AM-GM inequality. Here, we will use the AM-GM inequality. First, note that since \\(x, y, z\\) are positive and \\(x + y + z = 1\\), we have \\(x + y = 1 - z\\). Therefore, the expression becomes:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1 - z}{xyz}\n\\]\n\nWe can rewrite this as:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{xyz} - \\frac{z}{xyz} = \\frac{1}{xyz} - \\frac{1}{xy}\n\\]\n\nHowever, a simpler approach is to use the AM-GM inequality directly on \\(x, y, z\\). By the AM-GM inequality, we have:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{x + y + z}{3} \\geq \\sqrt[3]{xyz}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(x + y + z = 1\\), this simplifies to:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{3} \\geq \\sqrt[3]{xyz}\n\\]\n\nCubing both sides, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{27} \\geq xyz\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of \\(\\frac{1}{xyz}\\) is 27, which occurs when \\(x = y = z = \\frac{1}{3}\\). Substituting \\(x = y = z = \\frac{1}{3}\\) into the original expression, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{x + y}{xyz} = \\frac{\\frac{1}{3} + \\frac{1}{3}}{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)} = \\frac{\\frac{2}{3}}{\\frac{1}{27}} = \\frac{2}{3} \\cdot 27 = 18\n\\]\n\nThus, the minimum possible value of the expression is \\(\\boxed{18}\\).', 'A frog is positioned at the origin of the coordinate plane. From the point $(x, y)$, the frog can jump to any of the points $(x + 1, y)$, $(x + 2, y)$, $(x, y + 1)$, or $(x, y + 2)$. Find the number of distinct sequences of jumps in which the frog begins at $(0, 0)$ and ends at $(4, 4)$. 1. 48\n 2. 52\n 3. 56\n 4. 60\n 5. 64\n 6. 68\n 7. 72\n 8. 76\n 9. 80\n 10. 84\n 11. 88\n 12. 92\n 13. 96\n 14. 100\n 15. 104\n 16. 108\n 17. 112\n 18. 116\n 19. 120\n 20. 124\n 21. 128\n 22. 132\n 23. 136\n 24. 140\n 25. 144\n 26. 148\n 27. 152\n 28. 156\n 29. 160\n 30. 164\n 31. 168\n 32. 172\n 33. 176\n 34. 180\n 35. 184\n 36. 188\n 37. 192\n 38. 196\n 39. 200\n 40. 204\n 41. 208\n 42. 212\n 43. 216\n 44. 220\n 45. 224\n 46. 228\n 47. 232\n 48. 236\n 49. 240\n 50. 244\n 51. 248\n 52. 252\n 53. 256\n 54. 260\n 55. 264\n 56. 268\n 57. 272\n 58. 276\n 59. 280\n 60. 284\n 61. 288\n 62. 292\n 63. 296\n 64. 300\n 65. 304\n 66. 308\n 67. 312\n 68. 316\n 69. 320\n 70. 324\n 71. 328\n 72. 332\n 73. 336\n 74. 340\n 75. 344\n 76. 