rac{1}{3}\\).\n\nSince there is no \\(x\\) that satisfies the given equation, the solution set is empty.\n\nLet's confirm this using Python and SymPy:\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the variable\nx = sp.symbols('x')\n\n# Define the equation\nequation = sp.sin(sp.atan(x) + sp.acot(1/x)) - 1/3\n\n# Solve the equation\nsolutions = sp.solve(equation, x)\nprint(solutions)\n```\n```output\n[]\n```\nThe output confirms that there are no solutions to the equation \\(\\sin \\left( \\tan^{-1} (x) + \\cot^{-1} \\left( \\frac{1}{x} \\right) \\right) = \\frac{1}{3}\\).\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{No solutions}}\n\\]", "To solve the equation \\(\\sin \\left( \\tan^{-1} (x) + \\cot^{-1} \\left( \\frac{1}{x} \\right) \\right) = \\frac{1}{3}\\), we need to first simplify the argument of the sine function.\n\nRecall the following trigonometric identities:\n1. \\(\\cot^{-1}(y) = \\frac{\\pi}{2} - \\tan^{-1}(y)\\).\n2. \\(\\tan^{-1}(x) + \\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right) = \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\) for \\(x > 0\\), and \\(\\tan^{-1}(x) + \\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right) = -\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\) for \\(x < 0\\).\n\nSo, \\(\\tan^{-1}(x) + \\cot^{-1}\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right) = \\tan^{-1}(x) + \\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2} - \\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)\\right) = \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\) for all \\(x \\neq 0\\).\n\nTherefore, the equation simplifies to:\n\\[\n\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right) = \\frac{1}{3}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right) = 1\\), the equation \\(1 = \\frac{1}{3}\\) is not true. This means there are no real solutions to the original equation.\n\nLet's confirm this using Python code.\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\r\n\r\n# Define the variable\r\nx = sp.symbols('x')\r\n\r\n# Define the equation\r\nequation = sp.sin(sp.atan(x) + sp.acot(1/x)) - 1/3\r\n\r\n# Solve the equation\r\nsolutions = sp.solveset(equation, x, domain=sp.S.Reals)\r\nprint(solutions)\n```\n```output\nConditionSet(x, Eq(sin(atan(x) + acot(1/x)) - 0.33