 will calculate the possible values of \\(c\\) and sum them up.\n\nLet's write the Python code to solve this problem.\n```python\nfrom itertools import combinations_with_replacement\n\n# Function to find possible values of c given a\ndef find_possible_c_values(a):\n    possible_c_values = set()\n    # Iterate over all possible combinations of roots (r1, r2, r3)\n    for r1 in range(1, a):\n        for r2 in range(1, a - r1 + 1):\n            r3 = a - r1 - r2\n            if r3 > 0:\n                # Check the second Vieta's formula\n                if (r1 * r2 + r2 * r3 + r3 * r1) == (a**2 - 81) / 2:\n                    # Calculate c\n                    c = 2 * r1 * r2 * r3\n                    possible_c_values.add(c)\n    return possible_c_values\n\n# Iterate over possible values of a\npossible_c_values = set()\nfor a in range(6, 100):  # a must be at least 6 because the smallest sum of three positive integers is 1+2+3=6\n    c_values = find_possible_c_values(a)\n    if len(c_values) == 2:\n        possible_c_values.update(c_values)\n        break\n\n# Sum of the two possible values of c\nsum_of_c_values = sum(possible_c_values)\nprint((sum_of_c_values, possible_c_values))\n```\n```output\n(0, set())\n```\nIt seems that the initial range for \\(a\\) might not have been sufficient or there might be an issue with the logic. Let's refine the approach and extend the range for \\(a\\). We will also print intermediate results to debug the process.\n\nLet's extend the range for \\(a\\) and add some debug statements to understand the possible values of \\(a\\) and the corresponding roots.\n```python\nfrom itertools import combinations_with_replacement\n\n# Function to find possible values of c given a\ndef find_possible_c_values(a):\n    possible_c_values = set()\n    # Iterate over all possible combinations of roots (r1, r2, r3)\n    for r1 in range(1, a):\n        for r2 in range(1, a - r1 + 1):\n            r3 = a - r1 - r2\n            if r3 > 0:\n                #