 = np.array([-8, 7, 5])\n\n# Calculate the cross product of v and d\ncross_product = np.cross(v, d)\n\n# Calculate the magnitude of the cross product\nmagnitude_cross_product = np.linalg.norm(cross_product)\n\n# Calculate the magnitude of the direction vector d\nmagnitude_d = np.linalg.norm(d)\n\n# Calculate the distance between the lines\ndistance = magnitude_cross_product / magnitude_d\nprint(distance)\n```\n```output\n11.0\n```\nThe distance between the two parallel lines is \\(\\boxed{11}\\).", "To find the distance between two parallel lines in three-dimensional space, we can use the following formula:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Distance} = \\frac{\\| \\mathbf{d}_1 \\times \\mathbf{d}_2 \\|}{\\| \\mathbf{d}_1 \\|},\n\\]\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{d}_1$ and $\\mathbf{d}_2$ are the direction vectors of the two lines, and $\\mathbf{d}_1 \\times \\mathbf{d}_2$ is the cross product of these direction vectors.\n\nFirst, we identify the direction vectors of the lines:\n- The first line has direction vector $\\mathbf{d}_1 = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -9 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix}$.\n- The second line has direction vector $\\mathbf{d}_2 = \\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ -18 \\\\ -4 \\end{pmatrix}$.\n\nWe can see that $\\mathbf{d}_2 = 2 \\mathbf{d}_1$, so the lines are indeed parallel. The cross product $\\mathbf{d}_1 \\times \\mathbf{d}_2$ will be zero because the vectors are linearly dependent. However, we can use the formula for the distance between two parallel lines by finding the perpendicular distance from a point on one line to the other line.\n\nThe distance $D$ between the two parallel lines can be found using the formula:\n\\[\nD = \\frac{\\| (\\mathbf{r}_2 - \\mathbf{r}_1) \\times \\mathbf{d}_1 \\|}{\\| \\mathbf{d}_1 \\|},\n\\]\nwhere $\\mathbf{r}_1$ is a point on the first line and $\\mathbf{r}_2$ is a point on the second line.\n\nLet's choose the points $\\mathbf{r}_1 = \\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ -10 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix}$ (when $t=0$) and $\\mathbf{r}_2 = \\begin{pmatrix} -5 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 6 \\end{pmatrix}$ (when $u=0$).\n\nFirst, we compute the vector $\\mathbf{r}_2 - \\mathbf{r}_1$:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{r}_2 - \\mathbf{r}_1 = \\begin{pmatrix} -5 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 6 \\end{pmatrix} - \\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ -10 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -8 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 5 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nNext, we compute the cross product $(\\mathbf{r}_2 - \\mathbf{r}_1) \\times \\mathbf{d}_1$:\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} -8 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 5 \\end{pmatrix} \\times \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -9 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 7 \\cdot (-2) - 5 \\cdot (-9) \\\\ 5 \\cdot 2 - (-8) \\cdot (-2) \\\\ -8 \\cdot (-9) - 7 \\cdot 2 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -14 + 45 \\\\ 10 - 16 \\\\ 72 - 14 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 31 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 58 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nThe magnitude of this cross product is:\n\\[\n\\| \\begin{pmatrix} 31 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 58 \\end{pmatrix} \\| = \\sqrt{31^2 + (-6)^2 + 58^2} = \\sqrt{961 + 36 + 3364} = \\sqrt{4361}.\n\\]\n\nThe magnitude of the direction vector $\\mathbf{d}_1$ is:\n\\[\n\\| \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -9 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix} \\| = \\sqrt{2^2 + (-9)^2 + (-2)^2} = \\sqrt{4 + 81 + 4} = \\sqrt{89}.\n\\]\n\nFinally, the distance $D$ between the two parallel lines is:\n\\[\nD = \\frac{\\sqrt{4361}}{\\sqrt{89}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{4361}{89}} = \\sqrt{49} = 7.\n\\]\n\nSo the distance between the two lines is $\\boxed{7}$.", 'To find the distance between two parallel lines, we can use the following approach:\n\n1. Find the normal vector to the lines. Since the lines are parallel, the normal vector will be perpendicular to the direction vector of the lines.\n2. Find a point on each line.\n3. Use the normal vector and the points to find the distance between the lines.\n\nThe lines are given by:\n\\[ \\mathbf{r}_1(t) = \\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ -10 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix} + t \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -9 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix} \\]\n\\[ \\mathbf{r}_2(u) = \\begin{pmatrix} -5 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 6 \\end{pmatrix} + u \\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ -18 \\\\ -4 \\end{pmatrix} \\]\n\n