int on the second line is \\(\\mathbf{r_2} = \\begin{pmatrix} -5 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 6 \\end{pmatrix}\\).\n\n4. **Find the vector between the points**:\n   The vector \\(\\mathbf{r_{12}}\\) from \\(\\mathbf{r_1}\\) to \\(\\mathbf{r_2}\\) is:\n   \\[\n   \\mathbf{r_{12}} = \\mathbf{r_2} - \\mathbf{r_1}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Find the perpendicular distance**:\n   The perpendicular distance \\(D\\) between the two parallel lines can be found using the formula:\n   \\[\n   D = \\frac{|\\mathbf{r_{12}} \\times \\mathbf{d_1}|}{|\\mathbf{d_1}|}\n   \\]\n   where \\(\\times\\) denotes the cross product.\n\nLet's calculate this step by step using Python.\n```python\nimport numpy as np\n\n# Define the points on the lines\nr1 = np.array([3, -10, 1])\nr2 = np.array([-5, -3, 6])\n\n# Define the direction vectors of the lines\nd1 = np.array([2, -9, -2])\nd2 = np.array([4, -18, -4])\n\n# Verify that the lines are parallel (d2 should be a scalar multiple of d1)\n# In this case, d2 = 2 * d1, so they are indeed parallel\n\n# Calculate the vector r12 from r1 to r2\nr12 = r2 - r1\n\n# Calculate the cross product of r12 and d1\ncross_product = np.cross(r12, d1)\n\n# Calculate the magnitude of the cross product\ncross_product_magnitude = np.linalg.norm(cross_product)\n\n# Calculate the magnitude of the direction vector d1\nd1_magnitude = np.linalg.norm(d1)\n\n# Calculate the perpendicular distance D\nD = cross_product_magnitude / d1_magnitude\nprint(D)\n```\n```output\n7.0\n```\nThe perpendicular distance between the two parallel lines is \\(\\boxed{7}\\).\n\nIn the Cartesian coordinate system $xOy$, the parametric equation of line $l$ is $\\begin{cases} x=1+ \\sqrt {2}t \\\\ y= \\sqrt {2}t\\end{cases}$ (where $t$ is the parameter), and in the polar coordinate system with $O$ as the pole and the positive half-axis of $x$ as the polar axis, the polar equation of curve $C$ is $3\\rho^{2}\\cos ^{2}\\theta+4\\rho^{2}\\sin ^{2}\\theta=12$, and line $l$ intersects curve $C$ at points $P$ and $Q$.  \n(I) Fi