$. 1. Solving the Inequalities:\n\n   First, we solve each inequality individually:\n\n   - For $0\\ge 54p-144$:\n     \\[\n     0 \\ge 54p - 144\n     \\]\n     Adding 144 to both sides gives:\n     \\[\n     144 \\ge 54p\n     \\]\n     Dividing both sides by 54 (and noting that this does not change the direction of the inequality since 54 is positive):\n     \\[\n     \\frac{144}{54} \\ge p\n     \\]\n     Simplifying the fraction:\n     \\[\n     \\frac{8}{3} \\ge p\n     \\]\n\n   - For $0>12-20p$:\n     \\[\n     0 > 12 - 20p\n     \\]\n     Subtracting 12 from both sides gives:\n     \\[\n     -12 > -20p\n     \\]\n     Dividing both sides by -20 (and reversing the inequality since we are dividing by a negative number):\n     \\[\n     \\frac{12}{20} < p\n     \\]\n     Simplifying the fraction:\n     \\[\n     \\frac{3}{5} < p\n     \\]\n\n2. Combining the Solutions:\n\n   The solutions to the inequalities are:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{8}{3} \\ge p \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\frac{3}{5} < p\n   \\]\n   To find the values of $p$ that satisfy both inequalities, we consider the intersection of these intervals.\n\n   The inequality $\\frac{8}{3} \\ge p$ implies that $p$ must be less than or equal to $\\frac{8}{3}$.\n\n   The inequality $\\frac{3}{5} < p$ implies that $p$ must be greater than $\\frac{3}{5}$.\n\n   Therefore, the combined solution is:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{3}{5} < p \\le \\frac{8}{3}\n   \\]\n\n3. Interval Notation:\n\n   In interval notation, the solution is:\n   \\[\n   \\left( \\frac{3}{5}, \\frac{8}{3} \\right]\n   \\]\n\n4. Range of the Expression $2p + 7$:\n\n   To find the range of $2p + 7$ for $p$ in the interval $\\left( \\frac{3}{5}, \\frac{8}{3} \\right]$, we evaluate the expression at the bounds of the interval.\n\n   - At $p = \\frac{3}{5}$:\n     \\[\n     2\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right) + 7 = \\frac{6}{5} + 7 = \\frac{6}{5} + \\frac{35}{5} = \\frac{41}{5}\n     \\]\n\n   - At $p = \\frac{8}{3}$:\n     \\[\n     2\\left(\\frac{8}{3}\\right) + 7 