r equivalently \\(p > \\frac{3}{5}\\).\n\n3. To find the values of \\(p\\) that satisfy both inequalities, we need to find the intersection of the intervals \\(p \\le \\frac{8}{3}\\) and \\(p > \\frac{3}{5}\\). The intersection of these intervals is \\(\\frac{3}{5} < p \\le \\frac{8}{3}\\).\n\n4. To solve the inequality \\(8t^2 \\le 3 - 10t\\), we can follow these steps:\n   - Rearrange the inequality: \\(8t^2 + 10t - 3 \\le 0\\).\n   - Factor the quadratic expression: \\(8t^2 + 10t - 3 = (4t - 1)(2t + 3)\\).\n   - Find the roots of the equation \\(8t^2 + 10t - 3 = 0\\), which are \\(t = \\frac{1}{4}\\) and \\(t = -\\frac{3}{2}\\).\n   - The inequality \\(8t^2 + 10t - 3 \\le 0\\) is satisfied between the roots \\(-\\frac{3}{2} \\le t \\le \\frac{1}{4}\\).\n\nLet\'s write the Python code to confirm these results.\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the variable\np = sp.symbols(\'p\')\n\n# Define the inequalities\nineq1 = sp.Ge(0, 54*p - 144)\nineq2 = sp.Gt(0, 12 - 20*p)\n\n# Solve the inequalities\nsol1 = sp.solve(ineq1, p)\nsol2 = sp.solve(ineq2, p)\n\n# Find the intersection of the solutions\nsolution_p = sp.And(sol1, sol2)\n\n# Define the variable for the second inequality\nt = sp.symbols(\'t\')\n\n# Define the inequality\nineq3 = sp.Le(8*t**2, 3 - 10*t)\n\n# Solve the inequality\nsolution_t = sp.solve(ineq3, t)\n\nprint(f"Solution for p: {solution_p}")\nprint(f"Solution for t: {solution_t}")\n```\n```output\nSolution for p: (-oo < p) & (3/5 < p) & (p <= 8/3) & (p < oo)\nSolution for t: (-3/2 <= t) & (t <= 1/4)\n```\nBased on the output from the Python code, we can confirm the solutions:\n\n1. The solution for \\(p\\) is the interval \\(\\frac{3}{5} < p \\le \\frac{8}{3}\\).\n2. The solution for \\(t\\) is the interval \\(-\\frac{3}{2} \\le t \\le \\frac{1}{4}\\).\n\nSo, the final answers are:\n\n1. The values of \\(p\\) that satisfy both inequalities \\(0 \\ge 54p - 144\\) and \\(0 > 12 - 20p\\) are \\(\\boxed{\\left(\\frac{3}{5}, \\frac{8}{3}\\right]}\\).