es is no greater than the common value. Which of the following could be the graph of this set of points$http://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php?title=2017_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_16\nConsider the set of points $(x,y)$ in the plane such that two of the three quantities $3$, $x+2$, and $y-4$ are equal and the third of the three quantities is no greater than the common value. Which of the following could be the graph of this set of points$http://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php?title=2017_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_16\nConsider the set of points $(x,y)$ in the plane such that two of the three quantities $3$, $x+2$, and $y-4$ are equal and the third of the three quantities is no greater than the common value', "To solve this problem, we need to determine the number of integer coordinates that lie strictly inside the rectangle with vertices at $(5,4)$, $(5,-4)$, $(-5,4)$, and $(-5,-4)$. This means we need to exclude the boundary points.\n\nThe rectangle extends from $x = -5$ to $x = 5$ and from $y = -4$ to $y = 4$. The integer coordinates strictly inside the rectangle will range from $x = -4$ to $x = 4$ and from $y = -3$ to $y = 3$.\n\nWe can calculate the number of integer coordinates strictly inside the rectangle by counting the number of possible $x$ and $y$ values in these ranges and then multiplying them together.\n\nThe possible $x$ values range from $-4$ to $4$, which gives us $4 - (-4) + 1 = 9$ possible values. The possible $y$ values range from $-3$ to $3$, which gives us $3 - (-3) + 1 = 7$ possible values.\n\nThe total number of integer coordinates strictly inside the rectangle is then $9 \\times 7 = 63$.\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\n# Define the range for x and y coordinates strictly inside the rectangle\nx_range = range(-4, 5)  # from -4 to 4\ny_range = range(-3, 4)  # from -3 to 3\n\n# Calculate the number of integer coordinates strictly inside the rectangle\nnum_x = len(x_range)\nnum_y = len(y_range)\ntotal_points = num_x * num_y\n\nprint(total_points)\n```\n```output\n63\n```\nThe number of integer coordinates that lie strictly inside the rectangular region is \\(\\boxed{63}\\).", 'The sum of the first 2013 terms of a geometric sequence is 200. The sum of the first 4026 terms of the same sequence is 380. Find the sum of the first 6039 terms of the sequence. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{