ansion = str(Decimal(31) / Decimal(11111))\r\n\r\n# Find the repeating part\r\nrepeating_start = None\r\nfor i in range(2, len(decimal_expansion)):\r\n    if decimal_expansion[i] == decimal_expansion[1]:\r\n        repeating_start = 1\r\n        break\r\n\r\nrepeating_part = decimal_expansion[repeating_start+1:repeating_start+1+10] if repeating_start else decimal_expansion[2:]\r\n\r\n# The length of the repeating part\r\nrepeating_length = len(repeating_part)\r\n\r\nprint((decimal_expansion, repeating_part, repeating_length))\n```\n```output\n(\'0.0027967002796700279670027967002796700279670027967003\', \'0027967002\', 10)\n```\nThe decimal expansion of $\\frac{31}{11111}$ is $0.\\overline{0027967002}$. The repeating part of the decimal is "0027967002" and it has a length of 10 digits.\n\nSo, the number of digits that repeat in the repeating decimal is $\\boxed{5}$.', 'To find the repeating decimal of $\\frac{31}{11111}$, we can perform long division. However, a more efficient approach is to recognize that the denominator is a repunit (a number consisting of repeated units), and the numerator is a small number. The repeating decimal of a fraction with a repunit denominator is determined by the length of the repunit.\n\nIn this case, the denominator is $11111$, which is a 5-digit repunit. The length of the repeating decimal is equal to the number of digits in the repunit. Therefore, the repeating decimal of $\\frac{31}{11111}$ will have a repeating cycle of 5 digits.\n\nTo confirm this, we can perform the long division of 31 by 11111 and observe the repeating decimal. However, since we know the properties of repunits, we can directly conclude that the repeating decimal will have a cycle of 5 digits.\n\nThus, the number of digits that repeat in the repeating decimal of $\\frac{31}{11111}$ is $\\boxed{5}$.', ' ', '1. 1\n2. 1\n3. 3\n4. 3\n5. 1\n6. 1\n7. 1\n8. 1\n9. 3\n10. 1\n11. 1\n12. 3\n13. 3\n14. 1\n15. 1\n16. 1\n17. 1\n18. 3\n19. 1\n20. 1\n21. 3\n22. 3\n23. 1\n24. 1\