 11111\r\ndenominator = 11111\r\nrepeating_length = find_repeating_length(denominator)\r\nprint(repeating_length)\n```\n```output\n5\n```\nThe length of the repeating cycle in the decimal representation of $\\frac{31}{11111}$ is \\(\\boxed{5}\\).", 'What is the sum of the 4th, 10th, and 25th digits to the right of the decimal point in the decimal representation of $\\frac{4}{11}?$\n, and put your final answer within \\boxed{}. 1. **Problem 1:**\n   We need to find the sum of all possible values of \\( x \\) such that \\( f(x) = \\frac{1}{2} \\) for the function \\( f(x) = \\frac{2x + 3}{x - 4} \\).\n\n   First, set \\( f(x) = \\frac{1}{2} \\):\n   \\[\n   \\frac{2x + 3}{x - 4} = \\frac{1}{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Cross-multiply to solve for \\( x \\):\n   \\[\n   2(2x + 3) = 1(x - 4)\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   4x + 6 = x - 4\n   \\]\n\n   Subtract \\( x \\) from both sides:\n   \\[\n   3x + 6 = -4\n   \\]\n\n   Subtract 6 from both sides:\n   \\[\n   3x = -10\n   \\]\n\n   Divide by 3:\n   \\[\n   x = -\\frac{10}{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Since there is only one solution, the sum of all possible values of \\( x \\) is:\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{-\\frac{10}{3}}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Problem 2:**\n   We need to find the value of \\( \\frac{1}{2} \\div 2 \\div 2 \\div 2 \\).\n\n   Start by simplifying the division step-by-step:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{1}{2} \\div 2 = \\frac{1}{2} \\times \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{1}{4}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\frac{1}{4} \\div 2 = \\frac{1}{4} \\times \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{1}{8}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\frac{1}{8} \\div 2 = \\frac{1}{8} \\times \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{1}{16}\n   \\]\n\n   Therefore, the value is:\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\frac{1}{16}}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Problem 3:**\n   We need to find the sum of the 4th, 10th, and 25th digits to the right of the decimal point in the decimal representation of \\( \\frac{4}{11} \\).\n\n   First, find the decimal representation of \\( \\frac{4}{11} \\):\n   \\[\n   \\frac{4}{11} = 0.\\overline{36}\n   \\]\n\n   The decimal \\( 0.\\overline{36} \\