p.solve(equation2, x)\n\n# Sum the possible values of x\nsum_of_solutions = sum(solution2)\n\n# Print the solutions\nprint(f"Solution to Problem 1: {solution1}")\nprint(f"Solution to Problem 2: {sum_of_solutions}")\n```\n```output\nSolution to Problem 1: [0.461538461538462]\nSolution to Problem 2: 1/2\n```\n### Problem 1 Solution\nThe solution to the equation \\(\\frac{\\sqrt{2x}}{\\sqrt{3x-1}} = \\frac{3}{2}\\) is \\(x = \\frac{6}{13}\\).\n\n### Problem 2 Solution\nThe sum of all possible values of \\(x\\) for which \\(f(x) = f\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)\\) where \\(f(x) = \\frac{2x^2 - 3x - 2}{x^2 + x - 6}\\) is \\(\\frac{1}{2}\\).\n\nSo, the final answers are:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{6}{13}}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{2}}\n\\]', 'If $\\frac{3x+2}{x-1}=4$, solve for $x$. Express your answer as a common fraction. 1. To solve the equation \\(\\frac{\\sqrt{2x}}{\\sqrt{3x-1}} = \\frac{3}{2}\\), we start by squaring both sides to eliminate the square roots. This gives us:\n\\[\n\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2x}}{\\sqrt{3x-1}}\\right)^2 = \\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)^2\n\\]\n\\[\n\\frac{2x}{3x-1} = \\frac{9}{4}\n\\]\nNext, we cross-multiply to clear the fractions:\n\\[\n4 \\cdot 2x = 9 \\cdot (3x - 1)\n\\]\n\\[\n8x = 27x - 9\n\\]\nRearranging terms to isolate \\(x\\) gives:\n\\[\n8x - 27x = -9\n\\]\n\\[\n-19x = -9\n\\]\n\\[\nx = \\frac{9}{19}\n\\]\nWe need to verify that this solution satisfies the original equation. Substituting \\(x = \\frac{9}{19}\\) into the original equation, we get:\n\\[\n\\frac{\\sqrt{2 \\cdot \\frac{9}{19}}}{\\sqrt{3 \\cdot \\frac{9}{19} - 1}} = \\frac{\\sqrt{\\frac{18}{19}}}{\\sqrt{\\frac{27}{19} - 1}} = \\frac{\\sqrt{\\frac{18}{19}}}{\\sqrt{\\frac{8}{19}}} = \\frac{\\sqrt{18}}{\\sqrt{8}} = \\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2\\sqrt{2}} = \\frac{3}{2}\n\\]\nSince the left side equals the right side, the solution is correct. Therefore, the answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{9}{19}}\n\\]\n\n2. To solve the equation \\(\\frac{3x+2}{x-1} = 4\\), we start by eliminating the deno