\(a\\), \\(b\\), and \\(2009\\) that form a geometric sequence with an integer ratio \\(r\\). This means:\n     \\[\n     b = ar \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 2009 = ar^2\n     \\]\n   - From \\(2009 = ar^2\\), we can express \\(a\\) as:\n     \\[\n     a = \\frac{2009}{r^2}\n     \\]\n   - Since \\(a\\) and \\(r\\) are integers, \\(r^2\\) must be a divisor of 2009. The prime factorization of 2009 is:\n     \\[\n     2009 = 7^2 \\times 41\n     \\]\n   - The possible values for \\(r^2\\) are \\(1, 7^2 = 49, 41, 7^2 \\times 41 = 2009\\). Since \\(a < b < 2009\\), \\(r\\) must be greater than 1. So, the possible values for \\(r\\) are \\(7\\) and \\(\\sqrt{41}\\) (but \\(\\sqrt{41}\\) is not an integer, so it's not valid). Therefore, \\(r = 7\\).\n\n   - Substituting \\(r = 7\\) into the equation for \\(a\\):\n     \\[\n     a = \\frac{2009}{7^2} = \\frac{2009}{49} = 41\n     \\]\n   - So, \\(a = 41\\).\n\n2. **Creasing the Paper:**\n   - The paper is 10 inches by 50 inches, and \\(n\\) equally spaced creases are made parallel to the shorter side (10 inches). This means the paper is divided into \\(n+1\\) equal parts along the 10-inch side.\n   - The distance between two consecutive creases is:\n     \\[\n     \\frac{10}{n+1}\n     \\]\n\nLet's summarize the results and print them using Python code to ensure accuracy.\n```python\n# Geometric sequence calculation\n# Given 2009 = ar^2 and a < b < 2009, we need to find the integer ratio r\n# Prime factorization of 2009 is 7^2 * 41\n# Possible values for r^2 are 1, 49, 41, 2009\n# Since a < b < 2009, r must be greater than 1, so r = 7\nr = 7\na = 2009 // (r**2)\nprint(a)\n\n# Creasing the paper calculation\n# The paper is 10 inches by 50 inches, and n equally spaced creases are made parallel to the shorter side (10 inches)\n# The distance between two consecutive creases is 10 / (n + 1)\n# Since we don't know the value of n, we can't calculate the exact distance, but we can express it in terms of n\nn = 5  # Example value for n (you can change this to any value)\ndistance_between_creases = 10 / (n + 1)\nprint(distance_between_creases)\n```\n```output\n41\n1.6666666666666667\n```\nThe value of \\(a\\) in the geometric sequence is \\(\\boxed{41}\\).\n\nFor the creasing of the paper, if \\(n\\) equally spaced creases are made parallel to the shorter side of the paper, the distance between two consecutive creases is \\(\\frac{10}{n+1}\\) inches. For example, if \\(n = 5\\), the distance between two consecutive creases is approximately \\(1.67\\) inches.", 'A set of tiles numbered 1 through 100 is modified repeatedly by the following operation: remove all tiles numbered with a [perfect square](https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php/Perfect_square), and renumber the remaining tiles consecutively starting with 1. How many times must the operation be performed to reduce the number of tiles in the set to one? \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} 