 answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{12}\n\\]\n\nLet's verify this with Python code to ensure accuracy.\n```python\n# Define the possible pairs (A, B) that satisfy AB = 10\npossible_pairs = [(1, 10), (2, 5), (5, 2), (10, 1), (-1, -10), (-2, -5), (-5, -2), (-10, -1)]\n\n# Check which pair satisfies 3A - 8B = -1\nfor A, B in possible_pairs:\n    if 3 * A - 8 * B == -1:\n        result = A * B + B\n        break\n\nprint(result)\n```", "To find the degree of the polynomial \\( p(x) \\), we need to analyze the expression:\n\n\\[ p(x) = \\frac{(x - b)(x - c)}{(a - b)(a - c)} + \\frac{(x - a)(x - c)}{(b - a)(b - c)} + \\frac{(x - a)(x - b)}{(c - a)(c - b)}. \\]\n\nFirst, let's simplify each term individually. Notice that each term is a quadratic polynomial divided by a constant. Therefore, each term is a quadratic polynomial in \\( x \\). The degree of a polynomial is the highest power of \\( x \\) in the polynomial. Since each term is a quadratic polynomial, the degree of each term is 2. However, we need to check if there are any common factors that might reduce the degree of the overall polynomial.\n\nTo do this, let's evaluate \\( p(x) \\) at specific points \\( x = a \\), \\( x = b \\), and \\( x = c \\):\n\n1. When \\( x = a \\):\n\\[ p(a) = \\frac{(a - b)(a - c)}{(a - b)(a - c)} + \\frac{(a - a)(a - c)}{(b - a)(b - c)} + \\frac{(a - a)(a - b)}{(c - a)(c - b)} = 1 + 0 + 0 = 1. \\]\n\n2. When \\( x = b \\):\n\\[ p(b) = \\frac{(b - b)(b - c)}{(a - b)(a - c)} + \\frac{(b - a)(b - c)}{(b - a)(b - c)} + \\frac{(b - a)(b - b)}{(c - a)(c - b)} = 0 + 1 + 0 = 1. \\]\n\n3. When \\( x = c \\):\n\\[ p(c) = \\frac{(c - b)(c - c)}{(a - b)(a - c)} + \\frac{(c - a)(c - c)}{(b - a)(b - c)} + \\frac{(c - a)(c - b)}{(c - a)(c - b)} = 0 + 0 + 1 = 1. \\]\n\nSince \\( p(a) = 1 \\), \\( p(b) = 1 \\), and \\( p(c) = 1 \\), we see that \\( p(x) \\) is a polynomial that takes the value 1 at three distinct points \\( a \\), \\( b \\), and \\( c \\). The only polynomial of degree less than 3 that can take