ine the functions \\(f(x)\\) and \\(q(x)\\).\n2. Substitute \\(x = 1\\) into \\(q(x)\\) to find \\(q(1)\\).\n3. Substitute \\(q(1)\\) into \\(f(x)\\) and set it equal to \\(-3\\).\n4. Solve the resulting equation for \\(b\\).\n\nLet's break this down step-by-step and use Python to ensure accuracy.\n\n1. Define the functions:\n   \\[\n   f(x) = x - 3\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   q(x) = bx + 1\n   \\]\n\n2. Substitute \\(x = 1\\) into \\(q(x)\\):\n   \\[\n   q(1) = b \\cdot 1 + 1 = b + 1\n   \\]\n\n3. Substitute \\(q(1)\\) into \\(f(x)\\):\n   \\[\n   f(q(1)) = f(b + 1) = (b + 1) - 3 = b + 1 - 3 = b - 2\n   \\]\n\n4. Set \\(f(q(1))\\) equal to \\(-3\\) and solve for \\(b\\):\n   \\[\n   b - 2 = -3\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   b = -3 + 2\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   b = -1\n   \\]\n\nLet's verify this by implementing it in Python.\n```python\n# Define the functions f and q\ndef f(x):\n    return x - 3\n\ndef q(x, b):\n    return b * x + 1\n\n# Calculate q(1) and then f(q(1))\nb = -1\nq_1 = q(1, b)\nf_q_1 = f(q_1)\n\n# Check if f(q(1)) equals -3\nprint((f_q_1, b))\n```\n```output\n(-3, -1)\n```\nThe calculation confirms that when \\(b = -1\\), \\(f(q(1)) = -3\\). Therefore, the value of \\(b\\) is \\(\\boxed{-1}\\).", " To solve the problem, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. Substitute \\(q(1)\\) into \\(f(x)\\) and set it equal to \\(-3\\).\n2. Solve the resulting equation for \\(b\\).\n\nGiven:\n\\[ f(x) = x - 3 \\]\n\\[ q(x) = bx + 1 \\]\n\\[ f(q(1)) = -3 \\]\n\nFirst, we need to find \\(q(1)\\):\n\\[ q(1) = b \\cdot 1 + 1 = b + 1 \\]\n\nNow, substitute \\(q(1)\\) into \\(f(x)\\):\n\\[ f(q(1)) = f(b + 1) = (b + 1) - 3 \\]\n\nSet this equal to \\(-3\\):\n\\[ (b + 1) - 3 = -3 \\]\n\nSimplify and solve for \\(b\\):\n\\[ b + 1 - 3 = -3 \\]\n\\[ b - 2 = -3 \\]\n\\[ b = -1 \\]\n\nLet's verify this by substituting \\(b = -1\\) back into the equations.\n\n1. Calculate \\(q(1)\\):\n\\[ q(1) = -1 \\cdot 1 + 1 = -1 + 1 = 0 \\]\n\n2. Calculate \\(f(q(1))\\):\n\\[ f(0) = 0 - 3 = -3 \\]\n\nSince the resu