 denominator, we can use the identity:\n\\[ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{2} - 1} = \\frac{\\sqrt[3]{4} + \\sqrt[3]{2} + 1}{(\\sqrt[3]{2} - 1)(\\sqrt[3]{4} + \\sqrt[3]{2} + 1)} = \\frac{\\sqrt[3]{4} + \\sqrt[3]{2} + 1}{2 - 1} = \\sqrt[3]{4} + \\sqrt[3]{2} + 1 \\]\n\nSo we have:\n\\[ y = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{4} + \\sqrt[3]{2} + 1} \\]\n\nNow, we need to express $\\sqrt[3]{4} + \\sqrt[3]{2} + 1$ as a continued fraction. We can approximate this value and find its integer part. Let's calculate the numerical value of $\\sqrt[3]{4} + \\sqrt[3]{2} + 1$ and find its integer part.\n\nUsing Python, we can compute this value and find the continued fraction representation.\n```python\nfrom sympy import Rational, cbrt, nsimplify\n\n# Calculate the value of cbrt(4) + cbrt(2) + 1\nvalue = cbrt(4) + cbrt(2) + 1\n\n# Get the integer part of the value\ninteger_part = int(value.evalf())\n\n# Calculate the fractional part\nfractional_part = value - integer_part\n\n# The continued fraction representation starts with the integer part\na0 = integer_part\n\n# Now we need to find the continued fraction representation of the fractional part\n# We will use a numerical approach to find the next terms in the continued fraction\ndef continued_fraction(fraction, n_terms=10):\n    terms = []\n    for _ in range(n_terms):\n        integer_part = int(fraction.evalf())\n        terms.append(integer_part)\n        fraction = 1 / (fraction - integer_part)\n    return terms\n\n# Find the continued fraction representation of the fractional part\nfractional_cf = continued_fraction(fractional_part)\n\n# The first term is the integer part of the original value, and the rest are the terms of the continued fraction\nprint((a0, fractional_cf))\n```\n```output\n(4, [0, 1, 3, 1, 5, 1, 1, 4, 1, 1])\n```\nThe integer part of $\\sqrt[3]{2}$ is 1, and the continued fraction representation of the fractional part $\\sqrt[3]{4} + \\sqrt[3]{2} + 1$ starts with the terms [0, 1, 3, 1, 5, 1, 1, 4, 1, 1, ...].\n\nSo the continued frac