\\cdot n \\cdot (n+1) = 120 \\).\n   - The mean of these integers is \\( \\frac{(n-1) + n + (n+1)}{3} = n \\).\n   - The product divided by the mean is \\( \\frac{(n-1) \\cdot n \\cdot (n+1)}{n} = 120 / n = 24 \\).\n\n2. **Formulate the equation:**\n   - From the second condition, we get \\( \\frac{120}{n} = 24 \\), which simplifies to \\( n = 5 \\).\n   - We can verify that \\( (5-1) \\cdot 5 \\cdot (5+1) = 4 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 6 = 120 \\), which satisfies the first condition.\n   - The three consecutive integers are 4, 5, and 6, so the largest integer is 6.\n\nLet's implement this in Python to verify our solution.\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the variable\nn = sp.symbols('n')\n\n# Define the product of three consecutive integers\nproduct = (n-1) * n * (n+1)\n\n# Define the mean of the three consecutive integers\nmean = n\n\n# Define the equation based on the given conditions\nequation = sp.Eq(product / mean, 24)\n\n# Solve the equation\nsolution = sp.solve(equation, n)\n\n# Verify the solution\nlargest_integer = None\nfor sol in solution:\n    if sol.is_integer:\n        # Check if the product of the three consecutive integers is 120\n        if (sol-1) * sol * (sol+1) == 120:\n            largest_integer = sol + 1\n            break\n\nprint(largest_integer)\n```\n```output\n6\n```\nThe largest of the three consecutive integers whose product is 120 and whose product divided by their mean is 24 is \\(\\boxed{6}\\).", 'What is the sum of $5_6 + 14_6 + 23_6$? \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{