cos \\left( \\frac{-2 \\pi}{15} \\right) = \\cos \\left( \\frac{2 \\pi}{15} \\right)\\). So the expression simplifies to:\n\n\\[\n\\cos \\left( \\frac{2 \\pi}{15} \\right) \\cos \\left( \\frac{4 \\pi}{15} \\right) \\cos \\left( \\frac{8 \\pi}{15} \\right) \\cos \\left( \\frac{2 \\pi}{15} \\right) = \\left( \\cos \\left( \\frac{2 \\pi}{15} \\right) \\right)^2 \\cos \\left( \\frac{4 \\pi}{15} \\right) \\cos \\left( \\frac{8 \\pi}{15} \\right).\n\\]\n\nNow let's use sympy to compute the value of this expression.\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the angles\nangle1 = sp.Rational(2, 15) * sp.pi\nangle2 = sp.Rational(4, 15) * sp.pi\nangle3 = sp.Rational(8, 15) * sp.pi\n\n# Define the expression\nexpression = sp.cos(angle1) * sp.cos(angle2) * sp.cos(angle3) * sp.cos(angle1)\n\n# Simplify the expression\nsimplified_expression = sp.simplify(expression)\n\nprint(simplified_expression)\n```\n```output\nsin(pi/30)*cos(pi/15)*cos(2*pi/15)\n```\nThe output from the sympy library is \\(\\sin \\left( \\frac{\\pi}{30} \\right) \\cos \\left( \\frac{\\pi}{15} \\right) \\cos \\left( \\frac{2\\pi}{15} \\right)\\). This is another way of expressing the original product of cosines. To further simplify this, we can use trigonometric identities and properties of the roots of unity.\n\nWe can use the fact that the product of cosines of angles that are evenly spaced around the unit circle can often be simplified using known trigonometric identities. In this case, we can use the identity for the product of cosines of angles that are evenly spaced around the unit circle:\n\n\\[\n\\cos \\left( \\frac{2\\pi}{15} \\right) \\cos \\left( \\frac{4\\pi}{15} \\right) \\cos \\left( \\frac{8\\pi}{15} \\right) \\cos \\left( \\frac{16\\pi}{15} \\right) = \\frac{1}{16}.\n\\]\n\nThis identity can be derived from the properties of the roots of unity and the fact that the product of cosines of angles that are evenly spaced around the unit circle can be simplified using known trigonometric identities