es must satisfy the equation of the parabola \\(x^2 = 8y\\). So, we have \\(y_1 = \\frac{x_1^2}{8}\\) and \\(y_2 = \\frac{x_2^2}{8}\\).\n4. The distance between the origin and \\((x_1, y_1)\\) is \\(s\\), so we have:\n   \\[\n   s = \\sqrt{x_1^2 + y_1^2} = \\sqrt{x_1^2 + \\left(\\frac{x_1^2}{8}\\right)^2} = \\sqrt{x_1^2 + \\frac{x_1^4}{64}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{64x_1^2 + x_1^4}{64}} = \\frac{\\sqrt{x_1^4 + 64x_1^2}}{8} = \\frac{x_1\\sqrt{x_1^2 + 64}}{8}\n   \\]\n   Similarly, the distance between the origin and \\((x_2, y_2)\\) is also \\(s\\), so:\n   \\[\n   s = \\frac{x_2\\sqrt{x_2^2 + 64}}{8}\n   \\]\n5. The distance between \\((x_1, y_1)\\) and \\((x_2, y_2)\\) is also \\(s\\), so we have:\n   \\[\n   s = \\sqrt{(x_2 - x_1)^2 + (y_2 - y_1)^2} = \\sqrt{(x_2 - x_1)^2 + \\left(\\frac{x_2^2}{8} - \\frac{x_1^2}{8}\\right)^2} = \\sqrt{(x_2 - x_1)^2 + \\frac{(x_2^2 - x_1^2)^2}{64}}\n   \\]\n   Simplifying further, we get:\n   \\[\n   s = \\sqrt{(x_2 - x_1)^2 + \\frac{(x_2 - x_1)^2(x_2 + x_1)^2}{64}} = \\sqrt{(x_2 - x_1)^2\\left(1 + \\frac{(x_2 + x_1)^2}{64}\\right)} = |x_2 - x_1|\\sqrt{1 + \\frac{(x_2 + x_1)^2}{64}}\n   \\]\n6. Since the triangle is equilateral, the coordinates \\((x_1, y_1)\\) and \\((x_2, y_2)\\) must be symmetric about the y-axis. Therefore, \\(x_2 = -x_1\\). Substituting \\(x_2 = -x_1\\) into the equations, we get:\n   \\[\n   s = \\frac{x_1\\sqrt{x_1^2 + 64}}{8}\n   \\]\n   and\n   \\[\n   s = 2x_1\\sqrt{1 + \\frac{(x_1 - x_1)^2}{64}} = 2x_1\n   \\]\n   Equating the two expressions for \\(s\\), we get:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{x_1\\sqrt{x_1^2 + 64}}{8} = 2x_1\n   \\]\n   Simplifying, we get:\n   \\[\n   \\sqrt{x_1^2 + 64} = 16\n   \\]\n   Squaring both sides, we get:\n   \\[\n   x_1^2 + 64 = 256\n   \\]\n   So,\n   \\[\n   x_1^2 = 192\n   \\]\n   Therefore,\n   \\[\n   x_1 = \\pm 8\\sqrt{3}\n   \\]\n   Substituting \\(x_1 = 8\\sqrt{3}\\) into the expression for \\(s\\), we get:\n   \\', "To solve the problem, we need to find the side length of an equilatera