m\nb2 = 9  # longer base in cm\nh = 5   # height in cm\n\n# Calculate the area of the trapezoid\narea = 0.5 * (b1 + b2) * h\nprint(area)\n```\n```output\n35.0\n```\nThe area of the trapezoid is \\(\\boxed{35}\\) square centimeters.", "To find the area of the trapezoid, we can use the formula for the area of a trapezoid:\n\n\\[ \\text{Area} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times (b_1 + b_2) \\times h \\]\n\nwhere \\( b_1 \\) and \\( b_2 \\) are the lengths of the two parallel sides (bases), and \\( h \\) is the height of the trapezoid.\n\nFrom the diagram, we can see:\n- The two parallel sides (bases) are 5 cm and 9 cm.\n- The height of the trapezoid is 5 cm.\n\nSo, we can substitute these values into the formula:\n\n\\[ \\text{Area} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times (5 + 9) \\times 5 \\]\n\nLet's calculate this step-by-step using Python to ensure accuracy.\n```python\n# Define the bases and height of the trapezoid\nb1 = 5  # length of the first base in cm\nb2 = 9  # length of the second base in cm\nh = 5   # height of the trapezoid in cm\n\n# Calculate the area of the trapezoid\narea = 0.5 * (b1 + b2) * h\nprint(area)\n```\n```output\n35.0\n```\nThe area of the trapezoid is \\( \\boxed{35} \\) square centimeters.", "The area of a trapezoid is given by the formula:\n\n\\[ \\text{Area} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times (\\text{Base}_1 + \\text{Base}_2) \\times \\text{Height} \\]\n\nIn this trapezoid:\n- The lengths of the parallel sides (bases) are 5 cm and 9 cm.\n- The height of the trapezoid is 5 cm.\n\nLet's substitute these values into the formula to find the area:\n\n\\[ \\text{Area} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times (5 + 9) \\times 5 \\]\n\nWe can calculate this step by step. First, we add the lengths of the bases:\n\n\\[ 5 + 9 = 14 \\]\n\nThen we multiply by the height and divide by 2:\n\n\\[ \\text{Area} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 14 \\times 5 = 7 \\times 5 = 35 \\]\n\nSo the area of the trapezoid is \\(35\\) square centimeters. Let's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\n# Define the bases and height of the trapezoid\nbase1 = 5\nbase2 = 9\nheight = 5\n\n# Calculate the area of the trapezoid\narea = 0.5 * (base1 + base2) * height\nprint(area)\n```\n```output\n35.0\n```\nThe area of the trapezoid is \\(\\boxed{35}\\) square centimeters.", "To find the dot product of two vectors \\(\\mathbf{a} = \\begin{pmatrix} a_1 \\\\ a_2 \\\\ a_3 \\end{pmatrix}\\) and \\(\\mathbf{b} = \\begin{pmatrix} b_1 \\\\ b_2 \\\\ b_3 \\end{pmatrix}\\), we use the formula:\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{b} = a_1 b_1 + a_2 b_2 + a_3 b_3\n\\]\n\nGiven vectors are \\(\\mathbf{a} = \\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 7 \\end{pmatrix}\\) and \\(\\mathbf{b} = \\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -5 \\end{pmatrix}\\).\n\nLet's compute the dot product step by step using Python.\n```python\nimport numpy as np\n\n# Define the vectors\na = np.array([-2, 0, 7])\nb = np.array([3, 4, -5])\n\n# Compute the dot product\ndot_product = np.dot(a, b)\nprint(dot_product)\n```\n```output\n-41\n```\nThe dot product of \\(\\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 7 \\end{pmatrix}\\) and \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -5 \\end{pmatrix}\\) is \\(\\boxed{-41}\\).", "The dot product of two vectors $\\mathbf{a} = \\begin{pmatrix} a_1 \\\\ a_2 \\\\ a_3 \\end{pmatrix}$ and $\\mathbf{b} = \\begin{pmatrix} b_1 \\\\ b_2 \\\\ b_3 \\end{pmatrix}$ is given by:\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{b} = a_1 b_1 + a_2 b_2 + a_3 b_3\n\\]\n\nFor the vectors $\\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 7 \\end{pmatrix}$ and $\\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -5 \\end{pmatrix}$, the dot product is:\n\n\\[\n(-2) \\cdot 3 + 0 \\cdot 4 + 7 \\cdot (-5) = -6 + 0 - 35 = -41\n\\]\n\nLet's confirm this by calculating it using Python.\n```python\n# Define the vectors\nvector_a = [-2, 0, 7]\nvector_b = [3, 4, -5]\n\n# Calculate the dot product\ndot_product = sum(x*y for x, y in zip(vector_a, vector_b))\nprint(dot_product)\n```\n```output\n-41\n```\nThe dot product of the vectors $\\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 7 \\end{pmatrix}$ and $\\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -5 \\end{pmatrix}$ is \\(\\boxed{-41}\\).", "To find the dot product of two vectors, we multiply the corresponding components of the vectors and then sum those products. Given vectors $\\mathbf{a} = \\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 7 \\end{pmatrix}$ and $\\mathbf{b} = \\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -5 \\end{pmatrix}$, the dot product $\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{b}$ is calculated as follows:\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{b} = (-2) \\cdot 3 + 0 \\cdot 4 + 7 \\cdot (-5) = -6 + 0 - 35 = -41\n\\]\n\nLet's confirm this calculation using Python code.\n```python\n# Define the vectors\na = [-2, 0, 7]\nb = [3, 4, -5]\n\n# Calculate the dot product\ndot_product = sum(x*y for x, y in zip(a, b))\nprint(dot_product)\n```\n```output\n-41\n```\nThe dot product of the vectors $\\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 7 \\end{pmatrix}$ and $\\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -5 \\end{pmatrix}$ is \\(\\boxed{-41}\\).", "To find the dot product of two vectors, we multiply the corresponding components of the vectors and then sum the results. Given the vectors $\\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 7 \\end{pmatrix}$ and $\\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -5 \\end{pmatrix}$, the dot product is calculated as follows:\n\n\\[\n(-2) \\cdot 3 + 0 \\cdot 4 + 7 \\cdot (-5) = -6 + 0 - 35 = -41\n\\]\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\n# Define the vectors\nvector1 = [-2, 0, 7]\nvector2 = [3, 4, -5]\n\n# Calculate the dot product\ndot_product = sum(x*y for x, y in zip(vector1, vector2))\nprint(dot_product)\n```\n```output\n-41\n```\nThe dot product of the vectors $\\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 7 \\end{pmatrix}$ and $\\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -5 \\end{pmatrix}$ is \\(\\boxed{-41}\\).", "The dot product of two vectors $\\mathbf{a} = \\begin{pmatrix} a_1 \\\\ a_2 \\\\ a_3 \\end{pmatrix}$ and $\\mathbf{b} = \\begin{pmatrix} b_1 \\\\ b_2 \\\\ b_3 \\end{pmatrix}$ is given by:\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{b} = a_1 b_1 + a_2 b_2 + a_3 b_3\n\\]\n\nFor the vectors $\\mathbf{a} = \\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 