(-3) - 14 = 9 + 15 - 14 = 10 > 0\n   \\]\n   So, \\(x^2 - 5x - 14 > 0\\) in \\((-\\infty, -2)\\).\n\n2. For \\(x \\in (-2, 7)\\), we can test a value like \\(x = 0\\):\n   \\[\n   0^2 - 5(0) - 14 = -14 < 0\n   \\]\n   So, \\(x^2 - 5x - 14 < 0\\) in \\((-2, 7)\\).\n\n3. For \\(x \\in (7, \\infty)\\), we can test a value like \\(x = 8\\):\n   \\[\n   8^2 - 5(8) - 14 = 64 - 40 - 14 = 10 > 0\n   \\]\n   So, \\(x^2 - 5x - 14 > 0\\) in \\((7, \\infty)\\).\n\nSince the inequality \\(x^2 - 5x - 14 \\le 0\\) includes the roots \\(x = -2\\) and \\(x = 7\\), the solution to the inequality is the interval \\([-2, 7]\\).\n\nThe final answer in interval notation is \\(\\boxed{[-2, 7]}\\).\n\nLet's verify this using Python code.\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the variable and the inequality\nx = sp.symbols('x')\ninequality = x**2 - 5*x - 14 <= 0\n\n# Solve the inequality\nsolution = sp.solve_univariate_inequality(inequality, x, relational=False)\nprint(solution)\n```", "To solve the inequality \\(x^2 - 5x - 4 \\le 10\\), we first rewrite it as \\(x^2 - 5x - 14 \\le 0\\). We then find the roots of the equation \\(x^2 - 5x - 14 = 0\\). These roots will help us determine the intervals to test for the inequality.\n\nFirst, we solve the equation \\(x^2 - 5x - 14 = 0\\) using the quadratic formula \\(x = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}\\), where \\(a = 1\\), \\(b = -5\\), and \\(c = -14\\).\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{-(-5) \\pm \\sqrt{(-5)^2 - 4(1)(-14)}}{2(1)} = \\frac{5 \\pm \\sqrt{25 + 56}}{2} = \\frac{5 \\pm \\sqrt{81}}{2} = \\frac{5 \\pm 9}{2}\n\\]\n\nThis gives us the roots \\(x = \\frac{14}{2} = 7\\) and \\(x = \\frac{-4}{2} = -2\\). So, the roots are \\(x = 7\\) and \\(x = -2\\). These roots divide the number line into three intervals: \\((-\\infty, -2)\\), \\((-2, 7)\\), and \\((7, \\infty)\\).\n\nNext, we test each interval to determine where the inequality \\(x^2 - 5x - 14 \\le 0\\) holds true. We can choose a test point from each interval:\n\n1. For \\((-\\infty, -