es of \\( x \\) the function \\( f(x) \\) equals 0 and 6, respectively.\n\nThe function \\( f(x) \\) is defined piecewise:\n\\[ f(x) = \\begin{cases} \n3 - x & \\text{if } x \\leq 3, \\\\\n-x^3 + 2x^2 + 3x & \\text{if } x > 3.\n\\end{cases} \\]\n\n### Step 1: Find \\( f^{-1}(0) \\)\nWe need to solve \\( f(x) = 0 \\).\n\n1. For \\( x \\leq 3 \\):\n\\[ 3 - x = 0 \\implies x = 3. \\]\nSince \\( x = 3 \\) is within the domain \\( x \\leq 3 \\), it is a valid solution.\n\n2. For \\( x > 3 \\):\n\\[ -x^3 + 2x^2 + 3x = 0. \\]\nFactoring out \\( x \\), we get:\n\\[ x(-x^2 + 2x + 3) = 0. \\]\nSince \\( x > 3 \\), \\( x \\neq 0 \\). So we solve:\n\\[ -x^2 + 2x + 3 = 0. \\]\nThis is a quadratic equation. We can solve it using the quadratic formula \\( x = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a} \\), where \\( a = -1 \\), \\( b = 2 \\), and \\( c = 3 \\):\n\\[ x = \\frac{-2 \\pm \\sqrt{2^2 - 4(-1)(3)}}{2(-1)} = \\frac{-2 \\pm \\sqrt{4 + 12}}{-2} = \\frac{-2 \\pm \\sqrt{16}}{-2} = \\frac{-2 \\pm 4}{-2}. \\]\nThis gives us two solutions:\n\\[ x = \\frac{2}{-2} = -1 \\quad \\text{(not valid since } x > 3) \\]\n\\[ x = \\frac{-6}{-2} = 3 \\quad \\text{(not valid since } x > 3). \\]\nSo there are no valid solutions for \\( x > 3 \\).\n\nTherefore, the only solution for \\( f(x) = 0 \\) is \\( x = 3 \\). So, \\( f^{-1}(0) = 3 \\).\n\n### Step 2: Find \\( f^{-1}(6) \\)\nWe need to solve \\( f(x) = 6 \\).\n\n1. For \\( x \\leq 3 \\):\n\\[ 3 - x = 6 \\implies x = -3. \\]\nSince \\( x = -3 \\) is within the domain \\( x \\leq 3 \\), it is a valid solution.\n\n2. For \\( x > 3 \\):\n\\[ -x^3 + 2x^2 + 3x = 6. \\]\nThis is a cubic equation. We can solve it using numerical methods or by inspection. Let's check if there are any integer solutions greater than 3. We can try \\( x = 4 \\):\n\\[ -4^3 + 2(4^2) + 3(4) = -64 + 32 + 12 = -20 \\neq 6. \\]\nLet's try \\( x = 5 \\):\n\\[ -5^3 + 2(5^2) + 3(5) = -125 + 50 + 15 = -60 \\neq 6. \\]\nLet's try \\( x = 6 \\):\n\\[ -6^3 + 2(6^2) + 3(6) = -216 + 7