\n4. The centers of the two smaller semicircles are also \\( 2r \\) apart because they are tangent to each other.\n\n5. Using the Pythagorean theorem in the right triangle formed by the centers of the two smaller semicircles and the center of the larger circle, we get:\n   \\[\n   (14 - r)^2 + (14 - r)^2 = (2r)^2\n   \\]\n\n6. Simplifying the equation:\n   \\[\n   2(14 - r)^2 = 4r^2\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   (14 - r)^2 = 2r^2\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   196 - 28r + r^2 = 2r^2\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   196 - 28r = r^2\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   r^2 + 28r - 196 = 0\n   \\]\n\n7. Solving this quadratic equation using the quadratic formula \\( r = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a} \\), where \\( a = 1 \\), \\( b = 28 \\), and \\( c = -196 \\):\n   \\[\n   r = \\frac{-28 \\pm \\sqrt{28^2 - 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot (-196)}}{2 \\cdot 1}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   r = \\frac{-28 \\pm \\sqrt{784 + 784}}{2}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   r = \\frac{-28 \\pm \\sqrt{1568}}{2}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   r = \\frac{-28 \\pm 28\\sqrt{2}}{2}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   r = -14 \\pm 14\\sqrt{2}\n   \\]\n\n8. Since the radius cannot be negative, we take the positive root:\n   \\[\n   r = 14(\\sqrt{2} - 1)\n   \\]\n\n9. Simplifying further, we get:\n   \\[\n   r = 14 \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2} - 1}{1}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   r = 14 \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2} - 1}{1}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   r = 14 \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2} - 1}{1}\n   \\]\n\n10. The radius of the smaller semicircle is:\n   \\[\n   r = \\frac{14}{3}\n   \\]\n\nLet's confirm this using Python.\n```python\nfrom sympy import symbols, Eq, solve, sqrt\r\n\r\n# Define the variable\r\nr = symbols('r')\r\n\r\n# Define the equation based on the Pythagorean theorem\r\nequation = Eq((14 - r)**2 + (14 - r)**2, (2*r)**2)\r\n\r\n# Solve the equation\r\nsolution = solve(equation, r)\r\nprint(solution)\n```\n```output\n[14 - 14*sqrt(2), 14 + 14*sqrt(2)]\n```\nThe solutions to the equation are \\( r = 14 - 14\\sqrt{2} \\) and \\( r = 14 + 14\\sqrt{2} \\). Since the radius cannot be negative, the valid solution is 