stem:**\n   \\[\n   (-5 + 7i) + (-4 - 5i) = -5 - 4 + 7i - 5i = -9 + 2i\n   \\]\n\nThus, the resulting complex number is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{-9 + 2i}\n\\]\n\n4. **Translate the complex number so that the point of rotation is at the origin:**\n   \\[\n   (3 + i) - (-4 - 5i) = 3 + 4 + i + 5i = 7 + 6i\n   \\]\n\n2. **Rotate the translated complex number by \\(90^\\circ\\) counterclockwise around the origin:**\n   A \\(90^\\circ\\) counterclockwise rotation around the origin is equivalent to multiplying by \\(i\\):\n   \\[\n   (7 + 6i) \\cdot i = 7i + 6i^2\n   \\]\n   Since \\(i^2 = -1\\), we have:\n   \\[\n   7i + 6(-1) = 7i -', "The complex number $3$ can be represented as the point $(3,0)$ on the complex plane.  The center of rotation is the point $(-4,-5)$, which is represented by the complex number $-4-5i$.  The rotation is $90^\\circ$ clockwise, which is equivalent to $-90^\\circ$ counterclockwise.  To rotate a point $(x,y)$ by $\\theta$ degrees counterclockwise around the origin, we use the following formulas:\n\\begin{align*}\nx' &= x\\cos\\theta - y\\sin\\theta, \\\\\ny' &= x\\sin\\theta + y\\cos\\theta.\n\\end{align*}\nIn this case, we have $\\theta = -90^\\circ$, so $\\cos\\theta = 0$ and $\\sin\\theta = -1$.  Therefore, the formulas become:\n\\begin{align*}\nx' &= x(0) - y(-1) = y, \\\\\ny' &= x(-1) + y(0) = -x.\n\\end{align*}\nTo rotate the point $(3,0)$ by $-90^\\circ$ counterclockwise around the origin, we get:\n\\begin{align*}\nx' &= 0, \\\\\ny' &= -3.\n\\end{align*}\nThis gives us the point $(0,-3)$, which is represented by the complex number $0-3i$.  However, this is not the final answer, because we need to rotate around the point $-4-5i$ instead of the origin.  To do this, we translate the point $-4-5i$ to the origin, rotate the point $(3,0)$ around the origin, and then translate the point back.  Translating the point $-4-5i$ to the origin involves adding $4+5i$ to the point.  Translating the point $(0,-3)$ back involves subtracting $4+5i$ from the point.  Therefore, the final answer is:\n\\begin{align*}\n(0,-3) + (4,5) &= (4,2) \\\\\n&= 4+2i.\n\\end{align*}\nTherefore, the resulting complex number is $\\boxed{4+2i}$.", 'A $180^\\circ$ rotation around $-3 + 4i$ in the counterclockwise direction is applied to $11$ (as a complex number).  What is the resulting complex number?  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed