boxes contain both pens and pencils)\n\nUsing the principle of inclusion and exclusion, the number of boxes that contain either pens or pencils or both is:\n\\[ |A \\cup B| = |A| + |B| - |A \\cap B| = 5 + 4 - 2 = 7 \\]\n\nSince there are ten boxes in total, the number of boxes that contain neither pens nor pencils is:\n\\[ 10 - |A \\cup B| = 10 - 7 = 3 \\]\n\nSo, the number of boxes that contain neither pens nor pencils is \\( \\boxed{3} \\).\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\n# Given values\ntotal_boxes = 10\nboxes_with_pencils = 5\nboxes_with_pens = 4\nboxes_with_both = 2\n\n# Using the principle of inclusion and exclusion\nboxes_with_either_or_both = boxes_with_pencils + boxes_with_pens - boxes_with_both\n\n# Calculating the number of boxes with neither pens nor pencils\nboxes_with_neither = total_boxes - boxes_with_either_or_both\nprint(boxes_with_neither)\n```", 'Roslyn has ten boxes. Five of the boxes contain pencils, four of the boxes contain pens, and two of the boxes contain both pens and pencils. How many boxes contain only pencils? \\boxed{3}\n\\end{enumerate}\n\\end{document}\n```\n\nThis LaTeX code will produce a formatted exam with the specified content. You can customize the exam further by adding more questions, changing the layout, or using different packages for specific formatting needs.', "To solve this problem, we need to use the principle of inclusion and exclusion. Let's break it down step by step:\n\n1. Let \\( A \\) be the set of boxes that contain pencils.\n2. Let \\( B \\) be the set of boxes that contain pens.\n3. We know that \\( |A| = 5 \\) (five boxes contain pencils).\n4. We know that \\( |B| = 4 \\) (four boxes contain pens).\n5. We know that \\( |A \\cap B| = 2 \\) (two boxes contain both pens and pencils).\n6. The number of boxes that contain either pens or pencils (or both) is given by the principle of inclusion and exclusion: \\( |A \\cup B| = |A| + |B| - |A \\cap B| = 5 + 4 - 2 = 7 \\).\n7. Since there are ten b