 The number of favorable outcomes (no one sharing an office) is the same as the number of ways to assign 3 people to 3 different offices out of 5, which is \\(P(5, 3) = 60\\).\n   - The probability is the ratio of the number of favorable outcomes to the total number of outcomes, which is \\(\\frac{60}{125} = \\frac{12}{25}\\).\n\nLet\'s write the Python code to confirm these calculations:\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Part 1: Number of ways to assign 3 people to 5 different offices\nnum_ways = sp.perm(5, 3)\n\n# Part 2: Probability that no one shares an office\ntotal_ways = 5**3\nfavorable_ways = sp.perm(5, 3)\nprobability = favorable_ways / total_ways\n\nprint(f"Number of ways to assign 3 people to 5 different offices: {num_ways}")\nprint(f"Probability that no one shares an office: {probability}")\n```\n```output\nAttributeError: module \'sympy\' has no attribute \'perm\'\n```\nIt seems there was an error because the `sympy` module does not have a `perm` function. Instead, we can use the factorial function to calculate permutations. The permutation \\(P(n, k)\\) is given by \\( \\frac{n!}{(n-k)!} \\).\n\nLet\'s correct the code and re-run it:\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Part 1: Number of ways to assign 3 people to 5 different offices\nnum_ways = sp.factorial(5) / sp.factorial(5 - 3)\n\n# Part 2: Probability that no one shares an office\ntotal_ways = 5**3\nfavorable_ways = num_ways\nprobability = favorable_ways / total_ways\n\nprint(f"Number of ways to assign 3 people to 5 different offices: {num_ways}")\nprint(f"Probability that no one shares an office: {probability}")\n```\n```output\nNumber of ways to assign 3 people to 5 different offices: 60\nProbability that no one shares an office: 12/25\n```\nThe calculations confirm our reasoning. The number of ways to assign 3 people to 5 different offices is \\(60\\), and the probability that no one shares an office is \\(\\frac{12}{25}\\).\n\nSo, the final answers are:\n1. The number of ways to assign 3