the image of the vector \\(\\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}\\) under this reflection.\n\nA reflection matrix \\(R\\) in 2D can be written in the form:\n\\[ R = \\begin{pmatrix} \\cos(2\\theta) & \\sin(2\\theta) \\\\ \\sin(2\\theta) & -\\cos(2\\theta) \\end{pmatrix} \\]\nwhere \\(\\theta\\) is the angle between the line of reflection and the x-axis.\n\nGiven that \\(R \\begin{pmatrix} 5 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}\\), we can set up the following equation:\n\\[ \\begin{pmatrix} \\cos(2\\theta) & \\sin(2\\theta) \\\\ \\sin(2\\theta) & -\\cos(2\\theta) \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} 5 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix} \\]\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[ \\begin{pmatrix} 5\\cos(2\\theta) \\\\ 5\\sin(2\\theta) \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix} \\]\nFrom this, we get:\n\\[ 5\\cos(2\\theta) = 4 \\]\n\\[ 5\\sin(2\\theta) = 3 \\]\nSolving these equations, we get:\n\\[ \\cos(2\\theta) = \\frac{4}{5} \\]\n\\[ \\sin(2\\theta) = \\frac{3}{5} \\]\nSo the reflection matrix \\(R\\) is:\n\\[ R = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} & -\\frac{4}{5} \\end{pmatrix} \\]\nNow we can use this matrix to find the image of the vector \\(\\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}\\):\n\\[ R \\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} & -\\frac{4}{5} \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{4}{5}(-2) + \\frac{3}{5}(3) \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}(-2) + \\left(-\\frac{4}{5}\\right)(3) \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{-8 + 9}{5} \\\\ \\frac{-6 - 12}{5} \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{1}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{18}{5} \\end{pmatrix} \\]\nSo the reflection takes \\(\\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}\\) to \\(\\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{1}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{18}{5} \\end{pmatrix}\\).\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\nimport numpy as np\n\n# Define the reflection matrix R\nR = np.array([[4/5, 3/5], [3/5, -4/5]])\n\n# Define the vector to be reflected\nv = np.array([-2, 3])\n\n# Compute the reflection of the vector\nreflected_v = R @ v\nprint(reflected_v)\n```\n```output\n[ 0.2 -3.6]\n```\nThe reflection takes the vector \\(\\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}\\) to \\(\\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{1}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{18}{5} \\end{pmatrix}\\), which is equivalent to \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 0.2 \\\\ -3.6 \\end{pmatrix}\\).\n\nSo the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{1}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{18}{5} \\end{pmatrix}}\n", "l The reflection takes $\\begin{pmatrix} 5 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix}$ to $\\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}.$  We can use this information to find the line of reflection.  The line of reflection is the perpendicular bisector of the segment connecting $\\begin{pmatrix} 5 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix}$ and $\\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}.$  The midpoint of this segment is $\\begin{pmatrix} 4.5 \\\\ 1.5 \\end{pmatrix},$ and the slope of the segment is $\\frac{3-0}{4-5}=-3.$  Therefore, the slope of the line of reflection is $\\frac{1}{3}.$  The equation of the line of reflection is thus $y=\\frac{1}{3}x+1.$\n\nNow, we can use the equation of the line of reflection to find the reflection of $\\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}.$  The reflection of a point $(x,y)$ across the line $y=mx+b$ is $(x',y')$ where $x'=x-2\\frac{mx+b-y}{m^2+1}$ and $y'=y+2\\frac{mx+b-y}{m^2+1}.$  Substituting the values of $m,b,x,$ and $y,$ we get $x'=-2-2\\frac{\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot(-2)+1-3}{\\frac{1}{9}+1}=-2-2\\frac{-\\frac{5}{3}}{10}=-2+\\frac{1}{3}=-\\frac{5}{3}$ and $y'=3+2\\frac{\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot(-2)+1-3}{\\frac{1}{9}+1}=3+2\\frac{-\\frac{5}{3}}{10}=3-\\frac{1}{3}=\\frac{8}{3}.$  Therefore, the reflection of $\\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}$ is $\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} -\\frac{5}{3} \\\\ \\frac{8}{3} \\end{pmatrix}}.$", "To solve this problem, we need to find the reflection matrix that transforms the vector \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 5 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix}\\) to \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}\\). Once we have the reflection matrix, we can use it to find the image of the vector \\(\\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}\\).\n\nLet's break down the problem into manageable steps:\n\n1. **Find the reflection matrix:**\n   A reflection matrix \\(R\\) in 2D can be written in the form:\n   \\[\n   R = \\begin{pmatrix} a & b \\\\ b & -a \\end{pmatrix}\n   \\]\n   where \\(a^2 + b^2 = 1\\).\n\n2. **Determine the values of \\(a\\) and \\(b\\):**\n   We know that:\n   \\[\n   R \\begin{pmatrix} 5 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}\n   \\]\n   This gives us the equation:\n   \\[\n   \\begin{pmatrix} a & b \\\\ b & -a \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} 5 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 5a \\\\ 5b \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}\n   \\]\n   So, we have:\n   \\[\n   5a = 4 \\implies a = \\frac{4}{5}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   5b = 3 \\implies b = \\frac{3}{5}\n   \\]\n   We can verify that \\(a^2 + b^2 = \\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)^2 = 1\\), so the values are correct.\n\n3. **Form the reflection matrix:**\n   The reflection matrix is:\n   \\[\n   R = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} & -\\frac{4}{5} \\end{pmatrix}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Find the image of the vector \\(\\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}\\):**\n   We need to compute:\n   \\[\n   R \\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} & -\\frac{4}{5} \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{4}{5} \\cdot (-2) + \\frac{3}{5} \\cdot 3 \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} \\cdot (-2) + \\left(-\\frac{4}{5}\\right) \\cdot 3 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -\\frac{8}{5} + \\frac{9}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{6}{5} - \\frac{12}{5} \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{1}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{18}{5} \\end{pmatrix}\n   \\]\n\nLet's implement this in Python using sympy to verify our solution.\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the reflection matrix\na = sp.Rational(4, 5)\nb = sp.Rational(3, 5)\nR = sp.Matrix([[a, b], [b, -a]])\n\n# Define the vector to be reflected\nv = sp.Matrix([-2, 3])\n\n# Compute the reflection of the vector\nreflected_v = R * v\nprint(reflected_v)\n```\n```output\nMatrix([[1/5], [-18/5]])\n```\nThe reflection matrix that takes \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 5 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix}\\) to \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}\\) is:\n\\[\nR = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} & -\\frac{4}{5} \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\nUsing this reflection matrix, the vector \\(\\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}\\) is transformed to:\n", 'A rotation takes $\\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix}$ to $\\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix}.$  Which vector does the rotation take $\\begin{pmatrix} 5 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}$ to?  \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\b