egin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{4}{5}(-2) + \\frac{3}{5}(3) \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}(-2) + \\left(-\\frac{4}{5}\\right)(3) \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -\\frac{8}{5} + \\frac{9}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{6}{5} - \\frac{12}{5} \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{1}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{18}{5} \\end{pmatrix} \\]\n\nThus, the reflection takes the vector \\(\\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}\\) to \\(\\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{1}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{18}{5} \\end{pmatrix}\\).\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\nimport numpy as np\n\n# Define the reflection matrix\nR = np.array([[4/5, 3/5], [3/5, -4/5]])\n\n# Define the vector to be reflected\nv = np.array([-2, 3])\n\n# Compute the reflection of the vector\nreflected_v = R @ v\nprint(reflected_v)\n```\n```output\n[ 0.2 -3.6]\n```\nThe reflection takes the vector \\(\\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}\\) to \\(\\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{1}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{18}{5} \\end{pmatrix}\\), which is equivalent to \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 0.2 \\\\ -3.6 \\end{pmatrix}\\).\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{1}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{18}{5} \\end{pmatrix}}\n\\]", "Let's solve this problem step-by-step.\n\n1. **Understand the Reflection Transformation:**\n   A reflection over a line in the plane can be represented by a matrix. If a reflection takes the vector $\\begin{pmatrix} 5 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix}$ to $\\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}$, we can use this information to determine the reflection matrix.\n\n2. **Determine the Reflection Matrix:**\n   A reflection matrix $R$ can be written as $R = \\begin{pmatrix} a & b \\\\ b & -a \\end{pmatrix}$, where $a^2 + b^2 = 1$. We need to find $a$ and $b$ such that:\n   \\[\n   R \\begin{pmatrix} 5 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}\n   \\]\n   This gives us the equation:\n   \\[\n   \\begin{pmatrix} a & b \\\\ b & -a \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} 5 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 5a \\\\ 5b \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}\n   \\]\n   From this, we get the system of equations:\n   \\[\n   5a = 4 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 5b = 3\n   \\]\n   Solving these, we get:\n   \\[\n   a = \\frac{4}{5} \\quad \\text{and} \\quad b = \\frac{3}{5}\n   \\]\n   So the reflection matrix is:\n   \\[\n   R = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} & -\\frac{4}{5} \\end{pmatrix}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Apply the Reflection Matrix to the Vector $\\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}$:**\n   Now we need to find the image of the vector $\\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}$ under this reflection matrix. We compute:\n   \\[\n   R \\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} & -\\frac{4}{5} \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{4}{5}(-2) + \\frac{3}{5}(3) \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}(-2) + \\left(-\\frac{4}{5}\\right)(3) \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -\\frac{8}{5} + \\frac{9}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{6}{5} - \\frac{12}{5} \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{1}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{18}{5} \\end{pmatrix}\n   \\]\n\nSo the reflection takes the vector $\\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}$ to $\\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{1}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{18}{5} \\end{pmatrix}$.\n\nLet's verify this with Python code.\n```python\nimport numpy as np\n\n# Define the reflection matrix\nR = np.array([[4/5, 3/5], [3/5, -4/5]])\n\n# Define the vector to be reflected\nv = np.array([-2, 3])\n\n# Compute the reflected vector\nreflected_v = R @ v\nprint(reflected_v)\n```\n```output\n[ 0.2 -3.6]\n```\nThe reflection takes the vector $\\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}$ to $\\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{1}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{18}{5} \\end{pmatrix}$, which is equivalent to $\\begin{pmatrix} 0.2 \\\\ -3.6 \\end{pmatrix}$.\n\nSo the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{1}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{18}{5} \\end{pmatrix}}\n\\]", 'A reflection takes $\\begin{pmatrix} 5 