2)^2 = 9^2 = 81 \\equiv 4 \\pmod{11}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   8^8 = (8^4)^2 = 4^2 = 16 \\equiv 5 \\pmod{11}\n   \\]\n\n   Therefore:\n   \\[\n   8^{34} \\equiv 4 \\pmod{11}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   8^{38} \\equiv 5 \\pmod{11}\n   \\]\n\n   Adding these results:\n   \\[\n   129^{34} + 96^{38} \\equiv 4 + 5 \\equiv 9 \\pmod{11}\n   \\]\n\n   The remainder when \\(129^{34} + 96^{38}\\) is divided by 11 is \\(\\boxed{9}\\).\n\n2. **Finding the units digit of \\(9^{27}\\):**\n\n   The units digit of a number is the remainder when the number is divided by 10. We need to find \\(9^{27} \\mod 10\\).\n\n   Notice that the units digit of powers of 9 follows a pattern:\n   \\[\n   9^1 = 9 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\text{units digit is } 9\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   9^2 = 81 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\text{units digit is } 1\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   9^3 = 729 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\text{units digit is } 9\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   9^4 = 6561 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\text{units digit is } 1\n   \\]\n\n   The units digits repeat every 2 powers: 9, 1, 9, 1, ...\n\n   Since the pattern repeats every 2 powers, we find the position of \\(9^{27}\\) in the cycle:\n   \\[\n   27 \\div 2 = 13 \\text{ remainder } 1\n   \\]\n\n   This means \\(9^{27}\\) has the same units digit as \\(9^1\\), which is 9.\n\n   Therefore, the units digit of \\(9^{27}\\) is \\(\\boxed{9}\\).", "To find the remainder when \\(129^{34} + 96^{38}\\) is divided by 11, we can use modular arithmetic. Specifically, we can simplify the problem by finding the remainders of 129 and 96 when divided by 11, and then using those remainders to compute the final result.\n\nFirst, we find the remainders:\n\\[ 129 \\div 11 = 11 \\text{ remainder } 8 \\Rightarrow 129 \\equiv 8 \\pmod{11} \\]\n\\[ 96 \\div 11 = 8 \\text{ remainder } 8 \\Rightarrow 96 \\equiv 8 \\pmod{11} \\]\n\nSo, the problem reduces to finding the remainder when \\(8^{34} + 8^{38}\\) is divided by 11. We can simplify this further by finding the pattern in the powers of 8 modulo 11. Let's calculate the 