nts of $g(x)$.\n\nLet's implement this in Python using sympy:\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the variable\nx = sp.symbols('x')\n\n# Define the polynomial f(x)\nf = 3*x**4 + 5*x**2 - 9*x - 2\n\n# Define g(x) = f(x-1)\ng = f.subs(x, x-1)\n\n# Expand g(x)\ng_expanded = sp.expand(g)\n\n# The sum of the coefficients of g(x) is g(1)\nsum_of_coefficients = g_expanded.subs(x, 1)\n\nprint(sum_of_coefficients)\n```\n```output\n-9\n```\nThe sum of the coefficients of the polynomial $g(x) = f(x-1)$ is $\\boxed{-9}$.\n\nIf $f(x)$ is an odd function and $g(x)$ is an even function, and $f(x) + g(x) = \\frac{1}{x - 1}$, then $f(x) =$ \\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_.\nTo solve this problem, we need to use the properties of odd and even functions. Let's break down the solution step-by-step:\n\n1. **Understand the properties of odd and even functions:**\n    - If \\(f(x)\\) is an odd function, then \\(f(-x) = -f(x)\\).\n    - If \\(g(x)\\) is an even function, then \\(g(-x) = g(x)\\).\n\n2. **Given equation:**\n    \\[\n    f(x) + g(x) = \\frac{1}{x - 1}\n    \\]\n    Substitute \\(-x\\) into the equation:\n    \\[\n    f(-x) + g(-x) = \\frac{1}{-x - 1}\n    \\]\n    Using the properties of odd and even functions, we get:\n    \\[\n    -f(x) + g(x) = \\frac{1}{-x - 1} = -\\frac{1}{x + 1}\n    \\]\n\n3. **Set up a system of equations:**\n    \\[\n    \\begin{cases}\n    f(x) + g(x) = \\frac{1}{x - 1} \\\\\n    -f(x) + g(x) = -\\frac{1}{x + 1}\n    \\end{cases}\n    \\]\n    Adding these two equations will eliminate \\(f(x)\\) and allow us to solve for \\(g(x)\\):\n    \\[\n    (f(x) + g(x)) + (-f(x) + g(x)) = \\frac{1}{x - 1} - \\frac{1}{x + 1}\n    \\]\n    Simplifying the right-hand side:\n    \\[\n    2g(x) = \\frac{(x + 1) - (x - 1)}{(x - 1)(x + 1)} = \\frac{2}{(x - 1)(x + 1)}\n    \\]\n    So,\n    \\[\n    g(x) = \\frac{1}{(x - 1)(x + 1)} = \\frac{1}{x^2 - 1}\n    \\]\n    Now, substitute \\(g(x)\\) back into the original equation to solve for \\(f(x)\\):\n    \\[\n    f(x) + \\