v_2 \\\\ -5v_1 - 1v_3 \\\\ 1v_2 - 2v_1 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2v_3 + 5v_2 \\\\ -5v_1 - v_3 \\\\ v_2 - 2v_1 \\end{pmatrix}\n   \\]\n   Setting this equal to the given vector:\n   \\[\n   \\begin{pmatrix} 2v_3 + 5v_2 \\\\ -5v_1 - v_3 \\\\ v_2 - 2v_1 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 90 \\\\ 30 \\\\ 30 \\end{pmatrix}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Set Up the System of Equations:**\n   This gives us the following system of linear equations:\n   \\[\n   \\begin{cases}\n   2v_3 + 5v_2 = 90 \\\\\n   -5v_1 - v_3 = 30 \\\\\n   v_2 - 2v_1 = 30\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Solve the System of Equations:**\n   We can solve this system to find the values of \\(v_1\\), \\(v_2\\), and \\(v_3\\).\n\n5. **Find the Vector with the Smallest Magnitude:**\n   The magnitude of a vector \\(\\mathbf{v} = \\begin{pmatrix} v_1 \\\\ v_2 \\\\ v_3 \\end{pmatrix}\\) is given by:\n   \\[\n   \\|\\mathbf{v}\\| = \\sqrt{v_1^2 + v_2^2 + v_3^2}\n   \\]\n   We need to find the vector that minimizes this magnitude.\n\nLet\'s solve this using Python and SymPy.\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the variables\nv1, v2, v3 = sp.symbols(\'v1 v2 v3\')\n\n# Define the system of equations\neq1 = sp.Eq(2*v3 + 5*v2, 90)\neq2 = sp.Eq(-5*v1 - v3, 30)\neq3 = sp.Eq(v2 - 2*v1, 30)\n\n# Solve the system of equations\nsolution = sp.solve((eq1, eq2, eq3), (v1, v2, v3))\n\n# Extract the solution\nv1_sol = solution[v1]\nv2_sol = solution[v2]\nv3_sol = solution[v3]\n\n# Define the magnitude of the vector\nmagnitude = sp.sqrt(v1_sol**2 + v2_sol**2 + v3_sol**2)\n\n# Print the solution and the magnitude\nprint(f"v1 = {v1_sol}, v2 = {v2_sol}, v3 = {v3_sol}")\nprint(f"Magnitude = {magnitude}")\n```\n```output\nv1 = 0, v2 = ', "To find the vector $\\mathbf{v}$ that satisfies the equation\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -5 \\end{pmatrix} \\times \\mathbf{v} = \\begin{pmatrix} 90 \\\\ 30 \\\\ 30 \\end{pmatrix},\n\\]\nwe can use the properties of the cross product. Let's denote $\\mathbf{v} = \\begin{pmatrix} v_1 \\\