_1 - (-30 - 5v_1) = 30 \\implies -5v_1 + 30 + 5v_1 = 30 \\implies 30 = 30\n\\]\n\nThis equation is always true, so it doesn't give us new information. Instead, we can use the expressions we have for $v_2$ and $v_3$ to find a specific solution. Let's choose $v_1 = -6$, then:\n\\[\nv_2 = 30 + 2(-6) = 30 - 12 = 18\n\\]\n\\[\nv_3 = -30 - 5(-6) = -30 + 30 = 0\n\\]\n\nSo, one solution is $\\mathbf{v} = \\begin{pmatrix} -6 \\\\ 18 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix}$. The magnitude of this vector is:\n\\[\n\\|\\mathbf{v}\\| = \\sqrt{(-6)^2 + 18^2 + 0^2} = \\sqrt{36 + 324} = \\sqrt{360} = 6\\sqrt{10}\n\\]\n\nTo verify that this is the vector with the smallest magnitude, we can check the general solution. The system of equations is underdetermined, so we can express the solution in terms of a parameter. Let's use Python to verify and find the smallest magnitude vector.\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the variables\nv1, v2, v3 = sp.symbols('v1 v2 v3')\n\n# Define the equations\neq1 = sp.Eq(2*v3 + 5*v2, 90)\neq2 = sp.Eq(-5*v1 - v3, 30)\neq3 = sp.Eq(v2 - 2*v1, 30)\n\n# Solve the system of equations\nsolution = sp.solve((eq1, eq2, eq3), (v1, v2, v3))\nprint(solution)\n\n# Calculate the magnitude of the solution\nv", 'To find the vector $\\mathbf{v}$ such that\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -5 \\end{pmatrix} \\times \\mathbf{v} = \\begin{pmatrix} 90 \\\\ 30 \\\\ 30 \\end{pmatrix},\n\\]\nwe can use the properties of the cross product. Let $\\mathbf{v} = \\begin{pmatrix} v_1 \\\\ v_2 \\\\ v_3 \\end{pmatrix}$. The cross product can be computed as follows:\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -5 \\end{pmatrix} \\times \\begin{pmatrix} v_1 \\\\ v_2 \\\\ v_3 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2v_3 - (-5)v_2 \\\\ -5v_1 - 1v_3 \\\\ 1v_2 - 2v_1 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2v_3 + 5v_2 \\\\ -5v_1 - v_3 \\\\ v_2 - 2v_1 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nSetting this equal to $\\begin{pmatrix} 90 \\\\ 30 \\\\ 30 \\end{pmatrix}$, we get the following system of equations:\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\n2v_