dular inverse of 12 modulo 13. The modular inverse \\( c \\) of 12 modulo 13 satisfies \\( 12c \\equiv 1 \\pmod{13} \\). By checking, we find that \\( 12 \\times 12 \\equiv 144 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{13} \\), so the inverse is 12. Thus:\n\\[ b \\equiv 2 \\times 12 \\pmod{13} \\]\n\\[ b \\equiv 24 \\pmod{13} \\]\n\\[ b \\equiv 11 \\pmod{13} \\]\nSo we can write:\n\\[ b = 13d + 11 \\]\nfor some integer \\( d \\). Substituting this back into the expression for \\( n \\):\n\\[ n = 77(13d + 11) + 54 \\]\n\\[ n = 1001d + 881 \\]\nThe largest such number less than 2010 is obtained by setting \\( d = 1 \\):\n\\[ n = 1001 \\times 1 + 881 = 1882 \\]\nLet's verify that \\( 1882 \\) satisfies all the conditions:\n1. \\( 1882 \\div 7 = 268 \\) remainder 6 (which is 5 modulo 7)\n2. \\( 1882 \\div 11 = 171 \\) remainder 1 (which is 10 modulo 11)\n3. \\( 1882 \\div 13 = 144 \\) remainder 1", "To solve this problem, we need to find the largest integer less than 2010 that satisfies the following system of congruences:\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\nx \\equiv 5 \\pmod{7} \\\\\nx \\equiv 10 \\pmod{11} \\\\\nx \\equiv 10 \\pmod{13}\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nThis can be solved using the Chinese Remainder Theorem (CRT). Let's break it down step-by-step.\n\n1. First, we note that the second and third congruences can be combined since they both have the same remainder:\n\\[\nx \\equiv 10 \\pmod{143}\n\\]\nwhere 143 is the least common multiple (LCM) of 11 and 13.\n\n2. Now we have the following system of congruences:\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\nx \\equiv 5 \\pmod{7} \\\\\nx \\equiv 10 \\pmod{143}\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nWe can use the Chinese Remainder Theorem to solve this system. Let's find a solution to these congruences.\n\n3. We need to find integers \\(m\\) and \\(n\\) such that:\n\\[\nx = 10 + 143k = 5 + 7m\n\\]\nfor some integer \\(k\\). This simplifies to:\n\\[\n143k = 7m - 5\n\\]\nor equivalently:\n\\[\n143k \\equiv -5 \\pmod{7}\n\\]\nSince \\(143 \\equiv 6 \\pmod{7}\\), we have:\n\\[\n6k \\equiv -5 \\pmod{7}\n