ial condition \\( f(1) = f(1986) \\).\n\nFirst, we will compute the values of \\( f(n) \\) for the first few terms to identify any patterns. We will then use these patterns to find the sum \\( f(1) + f(2) + f(3) + \\dots + f(1985) \\).\n\n1. For \\( n = 1 \\):\n\\[ f(2) = (-1)^2 \\cdot 1 - 2f(1) = 1 - 2f(1) \\]\n\n2. For \\( n = 2 \\):\n\\[ f(3) = (-1)^3 \\cdot 2 - 2f(2) = -2 - 2(1 - 2f(1)) = -2 - 2 + 4f(1) = -4 + 4f(1) \\]\n\n3. For \\( n = 3 \\):\n\\[ f(4) = (-1)^4 \\cdot 3 - 2f(3) = 3 - 2(-4 + 4f(1)) = 3 + 8 - 8f(1) = 11 - 8f(1) \\]\n\n4. For \\( n = 4 \\):\n\\[ f(5) = (-1)^5 \\cdot 4 - 2f(4) = -4 - 2(11 - 8f(1)) = -4 - 22 + 16f(1) = -26 + 16f(1) \\]\n\nWe can observe that the function alternates between even and odd indices, and the coefficients of \\( f(1) \\) seem to follow a pattern. Let's generalize this pattern.\n\nFor even \\( n = 2k \\):\n\\[ f(2k) = (2k-1) - 2f(2k-1) \\]\n\nFor odd \\( n = 2k+1 \\):\n\\[ f(2k+1) = -(2k) - 2f(2k) \\]\n\nBy substituting the recursive formula, we can find a general form for \\( f(n) \\). Let's use Python to compute the values of \\( f(n) \\) for the first 1986 terms and then sum the values from \\( f(1) \\) to \\( f(1985) \\).\n\nWe will use the initial condition \\( f(1) = f(1986) \\) to find the value of \\( f(1) \\).\n\nLet's write the Python code to compute the values and the sum.\n```python\n# Define the function f(n) based on the recursive relation\r\ndef f(n, memo={1: 0}):\r\n    if n in memo:\r\n        return memo[n]\r\n    if n % 2 == 1:  # n is odd\r\n        memo[n] = -(n-1) - 2 * f(n-1, memo)\r\n    else:  # n is even\r\n        memo[n] = (n-1) - 2 * f(n-1, memo)\r\n    return memo[n]\r\n\r\n# Find the value of f(1) that satisfies f(1) = f(1986)\r\nf1 = 0\r\nfor f1 in range(-1000, 1000):  # search in a reasonable range\r\n    if f(1986, {1: f1}) == f1:\r\n        break\r\n\r\n# Now we know f(1), we can compute the sum f(1) + f(2) + ... + f(1985)\r\ntotal_sum = sum(f(n, {1: f1}) for n in range(1, 1986))\r\n\r\np