f the common tangents to the circle \\(x^2 + y^2 = 2\\) and the parabola \\(y^2 = 8x\\), and then determine the area of the quadrilateral formed by these points.\n\n### Step-by-Step Solution:\n\n1. **Find the equation of the tangent to the circle \\(x^2 + y^2 = 2\\)**:\n   The general equation of the tangent to the circle \\(x^2 + y^2 = r^2\\) at the point \\((x_1, y_1)\\) is \\(xx_1 + yy_1 = r^2\\). For our circle, \\(r^2 = 2\\), so the tangent is \\(xx_1 + yy_1 = 2\\).\n\n2. **Find the equation of the tangent to the parabola \\(y^2 = 8x\\)**:\n   The general equation of the tangent to the parabola \\(y^2 = 4ax\\) at the point \\((x_1, y_1)\\) is \\(yy_1 = 2a(x + x_1)\\). For our parabola, \\(4a = 8\\) so \\(a = 2\\), and the tangent is \\(yy_1 = 4(x + x_1)\\).\n\n3. **Find the points of tangency**:\n   The common tangents will have the same form for both the circle and the parabola. So, we equate the two forms of the tangent equations:\n   \\[\n   xx_1 + yy_1 = 2 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad yy_1 = 4(x + x_1).\n   \\]\n   From the second equation, we can express \\(x\\) in terms of \\(y\\):\n   \\[\n   x = \\frac{yy_1}{4} - x_1.\n   \\]\n   Substitute this into the first equation:\n   \\[\n   \\left(\\frac{yy_1}{4} - x_1\\right)x_1 + yy_1 = 2.\n   \\]\n   Simplifying, we get:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{y_1^2}{4}x_1 + yy_1 - x_1^2 = 2.\n   \\]\n   Since \\(y_1^2 = 8x_1\\) (from the equation of the parabola), we substitute \\(y_1^2\\) with \\(8x_1\\):\n   \\[\n   2x_1^2 + yy_1 - x_1^2 = 2 \\implies x_1^2 + yy_1 = 2.\n   \\]\n   This gives us the points of tangency. Solving for \\(x_1\\) and \\(y_1\\), we find the points of tangency.\n\n4. **Find the coordinates of the points of tangency**:\n   By solving the equations, we find the points of tangency to be \\((2, 4)\\), \\((2, -4)\\), \\((-1, 1)\\), and \\((-1, -1)\\).\n\n5. **Calculate the area of the quadrilateral**:\n   The quadrilateral formed by these points is a kite. The area of a kite can be calculated using the formula