= 13\\)\n2. \\((10a + b) - (10b + a) = 27\\)\n\nSimplify the second equation:\n\\[\n10a + b - 10b - a = 27\n\\]\n\\[\n9a - 9b = 27\n\\]\n\\[\na - b = 3\n\\]\n\nNow we have a system of linear equations:\n\\[\na + b = 13\n\\]\n\\[\na - b = 3\n\\]\n\nWe can solve this system by adding the two equations:\n\\[\n(a + b) + (a - b) = 13 + 3\n\\]\n\\[\n2a = 16\n\\]\n\\[\na = 8\n\\]\n\nSubstitute \\(a = 8\\) back into the first equation:\n\\[\n8 + b = 13\n\\]\n\\[\nb = 5\n\\]\n\nSo, the original number is \\(10a + b = 10 \\cdot 8 + 5 = 85\\). The number with its digits reversed is \\(10b + a = 10 \\cdot 5 + 8 = 58\\).\n\nThe sum of the original number and the number with its digits reversed is:\n\\[\n85 + 58 = 143\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{143}\n\\]', "To solve this problem, we will first define the two-digit number and its properties. Let the two-digit number be \\(10a + b\\), where \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are the digits of the number. Here, \\(a\\) is the tens digit and \\(b\\) is the units digit.\n\nWe are given two conditions:\n1. The sum of the digits is 13.\n2. The difference between the number and the number with its digits reversed is 27.\n\nLet's translate these conditions into equations:\n1. \\(a + b = 13\\)\n2. \\((10a + b) - (10b + a) = 27\\)\n\nWe can simplify the second equation:\n\\[\n(10a + b) - (10b + a) = 9a - 9b = 27\n\\]\n\\[\n9a - 9b = 27\n\\]\n\\[\na - b = 3\n\\]\n\nNow we have a system of linear equations:\n1. \\(a + b = 13\\)\n2. \\(a - b = 3\\)\n\nWe can solve this system of equations to find the values of \\(a\\) and \\(b\\).\n\nLet's solve these equations using Python.\n```python\nfrom sympy import symbols, Eq, solve\n\n# Define the variables\na, b = symbols('a b')\n\n# Define the equations\neq1 = Eq(a + b, 13)\neq2 = Eq(a - b, 3)\n\n# Solve the system of equations\nsolution = solve((eq1, eq2), (a, b))\nprint(solution)\n```\n```output\n{a: 8, b: 5}\n```\nThe solution to the system of equations is \\(a = 8\\) and \\(b = 5\\). 