r and the reversed number\nsum_of_numbers = original_number + reversed_number\n\nprint(sum_of_numbers)\n```", "To solve this problem, we will set up a system of equations based on the given conditions. Let's denote the two-digit number as \\(10a + b\\), where \\(a\\) is the tens digit and \\(b\\) is the units digit.\n\n1. The sum of the digits is 13:\n\\[ a + b = 13 \\]\n\n2. The difference between the number and the number with its digits reversed is 27:\n\\[ (10a + b) - (10b + a) = 27 \\]\n\\[ 10a + b - 10b - a = 27 \\]\n\\[ 9a - 9b = 27 \\]\n\\[ a - b = 3 \\]\n\nNow we have a system of linear equations:\n\\[ a + b = 13 \\]\n\\[ a - b = 3 \\]\n\nWe can solve this system by adding the two equations to eliminate \\(b\\):\n\\[ (a + b) + (a - b) = 13 + 3 \\]\n\\[ 2a = 16 \\]\n\\[ a = 8 \\]\n\nSubstituting \\(a = 8\\) back into the first equation:\n\\[ 8 + b = 13 \\]\n\\[ b = 5 \\]\n\nSo the original number is \\(10a + b = 10(8) + 5 = 85\\). The number with its digits reversed is \\(10b + a = 10(5) + 8 = 58\\). The sum of these two numbers is:\n\\[ 85 + 58 = 143 \\]\n\nLet's verify our solution using Python code to ensure accuracy.\n```python\n# Define the equations\nfrom sympy import symbols, Eq, solve\n\n# Define the variables\na, b = symbols('a b')\n\n# Define the equations\neq1 = Eq(a + b, 13)\neq2 = Eq(a - b, 3)\n\n# Solve the equations\nsolution = solve((eq1, eq2), (a, b))\na_val = solution[a]\nb_val = solution[b]\n\n# Calculate the original number and the reversed number\noriginal_number = 10 * a_val + b_val\nreversed_number = 10 * b_val + a_val\n\n# Calculate the sum of the original number and the reversed number\nsum_of_numbers = original_number + reversed_number\nprint(sum_of_numbers)\n```\n```output\n143\n```\nThe sum of the original number and the number with its digits reversed is \\(\\boxed{143}\\).", "Let's denote the two-digit number as \\(10a + b\\), where \\(a\\) is the tens digit and \\(b\\) is the units digit. The number with its digits reversed is 