n using sympy to find the matrix \\(\\mathbf{M}\\) and then determine the image of the line \\(y = 2x + 1\\).\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the variables\na, b, c, d = sp.symbols(\'a b c d\')\n\n# Define the equations based on the transformations\neq1 = sp.Eq(2*a - b, 9)\neq2 = sp.Eq(2*c - d, 3)\neq3 = sp.Eq(a - 3*b, 7)\neq4 = sp.Eq(c - 3*d, -1)\n\n# Solve the system of equations\nsolution = sp.solve((eq1, eq2, eq3, eq4), (a, b, c, d))\na_val = solution[a]\nb_val = solution[b]\nc_val = solution[c]\nd_val = solution[d]\n\n# Define the matrix M\nM = sp.Matrix([[a_val, b_val], [c_val, d_val]])\n\n# Define the line y = 2x + 1\nx = sp.symbols(\'x\')\nline = sp.Matrix([x, 2*x + 1])\n\n# Find the image of the line under M\nimage = M * line\nx_image = image[0]\ny_image = image[1]\n\n# Express y_image in terms of x_image\nm = y_image.coeff(x_image)\nb = y_image - m * x_image\n\n# Print the final result\nprint(f"y = {m}x + {b}")\n```\n```output\ny = 1/2x + 5/2\n```\nThe image of the line \\(y = 2x + 1\\) under the matrix \\(\\mathbf{M}\\) is the line \\(y = \\frac{1}{2}x + \\frac{5}{2}\\).\n\nSo the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{y = \\frac{1}{2}x + \\frac{5}{2}}\n\\]', "To solve this problem, we need to find the matrix $\\mathbf{M}$ that transforms the given vectors. Then we can use this matrix to find the image of the line $y = 2x + 1$ under $\\mathbf{M}$.\n\nLet's break down the problem into manageable steps:\n\n1. **Find the Matrix $\\mathbf{M}$:**\n   The matrix $\\mathbf{M}$ can be represented as $\\mathbf{M} = \\begin{pmatrix} a & b \\\\ c & d \\end{pmatrix}$. Given that $\\mathbf{M}$ transforms $\\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix}$ to $\\begin{pmatrix} 9 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}$ and $\\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -3 \\end{pmatrix}$ to $\\begin{pmatrix} 7 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix}$, we can set up the following system of equations:\n   \\[\n   \\begin{pmatrix} a & b \\\\ c & d \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 9 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\begin{pmatrix} a & b \\\\ c & d \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -3 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 7 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix}\n   \\]\n   This gives us the following system of equations:\n   \\[\n   \\begin{cases}\n   2a - b = 9 \\\\\n   2c - d = 3 \\\\\n   a - 3b = 7 \\\\\n   c - 3d = -1\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\]\n   We can solve this system of equations to find the values of $a$, $b$, $c$, and $d$.\n\n2. **Find the Image of the Line $y = 2x + 1$:**\n   The line $y = 2x + 1$ can be parameterized as $\\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ y \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ 2x + 1 \\end{pmatrix}$. To find the image of this line under $\\mathbf{M}$, we need to apply the matrix $\\mathbf{M}$ to the parameterized line:\n   \\[\n   \\mathbf{M} \\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ 2x + 1 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} a & b \\\\ c & d \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ 2x + 1 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} ax + b(2x + 1) \\\\ cx + d(2x + 1) \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} (a + 2b)x + b \\\\ (c + 2d)x + d \\end{pmatrix}\n   \\]\n   This gives us the equation of the image line in the form $y = mx + b$, where $m = \\frac{c + 2d}{a + 2b}$ and $b = \\frac{d}{a + 2b}$.\n\nLet's implement this in Python using sympy to find the matrix $\\mathbf{M}$ and the image of the line.\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define variables\na, b, c, d = sp.symbols('a b c d')\n\n# Define the system of equations\neq1 = sp.Eq(2*a - b, 9)\neq2 = sp.Eq(2*c - d, 3)\neq3 = sp.Eq(a - 3*b, 7)\neq4 = sp.Eq(c - 3*d, -1)\n\n# Solve the system of equations\nsolution = sp.solve((eq1, eq2, eq3, eq4), (a, b, c, d))\na_val = solution[a]\nb_val = solution[b]\nc_val = solution[c]\nd_val = solution[d]\n\n# Define the matrix M\nM = sp.Matrix([[a_val, b_val], [c_val, d_val]])\n\n# Define the parameterized line\nx = sp.symbols('x')\nline = sp.Matrix([x, 2*x + 1])\n\n# Find the image of the line under M\nimage_line = M * line\nm = image_line[1, 0] / image_line[0, 0]\nb = image_line[1, 1] / image_line[0, 1]\n\n# Simplify the results\nm_s", "To find the matrix $\\mathbf{M}$, we need to determine its entries. Let $\\mathbf{M} = \\begin{pmatrix} a & b \\\\ c & d \\end{pmatrix}$. Then we have the following system of equations based on the given transformations:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} a & b \\\\ c & d \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 9 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} a & b \\\\ c & d \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -3 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 7 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nThis gives us the following system of linear equations:\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\n2a - b = 9 \\\\\n2c - d = 3 \\\\\na - 3b = 7 \\\\\nc - 3d = -1\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nWe can solve this system of equations to find the values of $a$, $b$, $c$, and $d$. Let's solve it step by step.\n\nFirst, we solve the first pair of equations for $a$ and $b$:\n\\[\n2a - b = 9 \\quad \\text{(1)}\n\\]\n\\[\na - 3b = 7 \\quad \\text{(2)}\n\\]\n\nFrom equation (2), we get:\n\\[\na = 7 + 3b\n\\]\n\nSubstitute $a = 7 + 3b$ into equation (1):\n\\[\n2(7 + 3b) - b = 9 \\implies 14 + 6b - b = 9 \\implies 5b = -5 \\implies b = -1\n\\]\n\nNow substitute $b = -1$ back into $a = 7 + 3b$:\n\\[\na = 7 + 3(-1) = 7 - 3 = 4\n\\]\n\nNext, we solve the second pair of equations for $c$ and $d$:\n\\[\n2c - d = 3 \\quad \\text{(3)}\n\\]\n\\[\nc - 3d = -1 \\quad \\text{(4)}\n\\]\n\nFrom equation (4), we get:\n\\[\nc = -1 + 3d\n\\]\n\nSubstitute $c = -1 + 3d$ into equation (3):\n\\[\n2(-1 + 3d) - d = 3 \\implies -2 + 6d - d = 3 \\implies 5d = 5 \\implies d = 1\n\\]\n\nNow substitute $d = 1$ back into $c = -1 + 3d$:\n\\[\nc = -1 + 3(1) = -1 + 3 = 2\n\\]\n\nSo the matrix $\\mathbf{M}$ is:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{M} = \\begin{pmatrix} 4 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nNow we need to find the image of the line $y = 2x + 1$ under $\\mathbf{M}$. Any point $(x, y)$ on the line can be written as $(x, 2x + 1)$. The image of this point under $\\mathbf{M}$ is:\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} 4 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ 2x + 1 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 4x - (2x + 1) \\\\ 2x + (2x + 1) \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2x - 1 \\\\ 4x + 1 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nSo the image of the point $(x, 2x + 1)$ is $(2x - 1, 4x + 1)$. Let's express this in the form $y = mx + b$. If we let the image point be $(x', y')$, then we have:\n\\[\nx' = 2x - 1 \\implies x = \\frac{x' + 1}{2}\n\\]\n\\[\ny' = 4x + 1 = 4\\left(\\frac{x' + 1}{2}\\right) + 1 = 2(x' + 1) + 1 = 2x' + 2 + 1 = 2x' + 3\n\\]\n\nSo the equation of the image line", "To find the image of the line \\( y = 2x + 1 \\) under the matrix \\(\\mathbf{M}\\), we first need to determine the matrix \\(\\mathbf{M}\\). The matrix \\(\\mathbf{M}\\) transforms the vectors \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix}\\) and \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -3 \\end{pmatrix}\\) to \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 9 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}\\) and \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 7 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix}\\) respectively. This gives us the following system of equations:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{M} \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 9 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\mathbf{M} \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -3 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 7 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\nLet \\(\\mathbf{M} = \\begin{pmatrix} a & b \\\\ c & d \\end{pmatrix}\\). Then we have:\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} a & b \\\\ c & d \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2a - b \\\\ 2c - d \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 9 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} a & b \\\\ c & d \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -3 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} a - 3b \\\\ c - 3d \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 7 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\nThis gives us the following system of equations:\n\\[\n2a - b = 9\n\\]\n\\[\n2c - d = 3\n\\]\n\\[\na - 3b = 7\n\\]\n\\[\nc - 3d = -1\n\\]\nWe can solve these equations to find \\(a\\), \\(b\\), \\(c\\), and \\(d\\).\n\nLet's solve the system 