begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix}\\) to \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 9 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}\\) and \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -3 \\end{pmatrix}\\) to \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 7 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix}\\).\n   - Let \\(\\mathbf{M} = \\begin{pmatrix} a & b \\\\ c & d \\end{pmatrix}\\).\n   - We can set up the following system of equations:\n     \\[\n     \\begin{cases}\n     2a - b = 9 \\\\\n     2c - d = 3 \\\\\n     a - 3b = 7 \\\\\n     c - 3d = -1\n     \\end{cases}\n     \\]\n   - Solve this system to find \\(a\\), \\(b\\), \\(c\\), and \\(d\\).\n\n2. **Transform the line \\(y = 2x + 1\\):**\n   - Any point on the line \\(y = 2x + 1\\) can be written as \\(\\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ 2x + 1 \\end{pmatrix}\\).\n   - Apply the matrix \\(\\mathbf{M}\\) to this point to find the transformed coordinates.\n   - Express the transformed coordinates in the form \\(y = mx + b\\).\n\nLet\'s implement this in Python using sympy:\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the variables\na, b, c, d = sp.symbols(\'a b c d\')\n\n# Define the equations based on the given transformations\neq1 = sp.Eq(2*a - b, 9)\neq2 = sp.Eq(2*c - d, 3)\neq3 = sp.Eq(a - 3*b, 7)\neq4 = sp.Eq(c - 3*d, -1)\n\n# Solve the system of equations\nsolution = sp.solve((eq1, eq2, eq3, eq4), (a, b, c, d))\na_val = solution[a]\nb_val = solution[b]\nc_val = solution[c]\nd_val = solution[d]\n\n# Define the matrix M\nM = sp.Matrix([[a_val, b_val], [c_val, d_val]])\n\n# Define the line y = 2x + 1\nx = sp.symbols(\'x\')\nline = sp.Matrix([x, 2*x + 1])\n\n# Transform the line using matrix M\ntransformed_line = M * line\n\n# Simplify the transformed line to the form y = mx + b\ntransformed_line_simplified = sp.simplify(transformed_line)\ny_expr = transformed_line_simplified[1] / transformed_line_simplified[0]\n\n# Extract m and b from the simplified expression\nm = sp.simplify(y_expr.coeff(x))\nb = sp.simplify(transformed_line_simplified[1].subs(x, 0) / transformed_line_simplified[0].subs(x, 0))\n\n# Print the final result\np