r than 1 (i.e., \\( n \\) is a prime number), and \\( n + 2 \\) is also not divisible by any integer greater than 1 (i.e., \\( n + 2 \\) is also a prime number). This means that both \\( n \\) and \\( n + 2 \\) must be prime numbers, and such pairs of prime numbers are known as twin primes.\n\nLet\'s write a Python code to find such a number \\( n \\) between 30 and 50.\n```python\ndef is_prime(num):\n    """Check if a number is prime."""\n    if num <= 1:\n        return False\n    if num <= 3:\n        return True\n    if num % 2 == 0 or num % 3 == 0:\n        return False\n    i = 5\n    while i * i <= num:\n        if num % i == 0 or num % (i + 2) == 0:\n            return False\n        i += 6\n    return True\n\n# Find the number of students between 30 and 50 such that both n and n+2 are prime\nfor n in range(30, 51):\n    if is_prime(n) and is_prime(n + 2):\n        result = n\n        break\n\nprint(result)\n```\n```output\n41\n```\nThe number of students that showed up to the archery class this week is \\(\\boxed{41}\\).', "To solve this problem, we need to find a number of students \\( n \\) between 30 and 50 such that \\( n \\) is not divisible by any number other than 1 and itself (i.e., \\( n \\) is a prime number), and \\( n + 2 \\) is also not divisible by any number other than 1 and itself (i.e., \\( n + 2 \\) is also a prime number). In other words, we are looking for a pair of twin primes between 30 and 50.\n\nLet's write a Python code to find such a number.\n```python\ndef is_prime(num):\n    if num <= 1:\n        return False\n    if num == 2:\n        return True\n    if num % 2 == 0:\n        return False\n    for i in range(3, int(num**0.5) + 1, 2):\n        if num % i == 0:\n            return False\n    return True\n\n# Find the prime numbers between 30 and 50\nprime_numbers = [num for num in range(30, 51) if is_prime(num)]\n\n# Check for twin primes\ntwin_primes = [(n, n+2) for n in prime_numbers if is_prime(n+2)]\n\n# The number of student