e steps in Python code to ensure the result is accurate.\n```python\n# Given angles\nangle_P = 40\nangle_Q = 35\n\n# Since ST is parallel to QR, angle STR is equal to angle Q (corresponding angles)\nangle_STR = angle_Q\n\nprint(angle_STR)\n```\n```output\n35\n```\nThe measure of $\\angle STR$ is \\(\\boxed{35}\\) degrees.", 'Since $\\overline{ST}\\parallel\\overline{QR}$, we know that $\\angle STR$ and $\\angle Q$ are alternate interior angles, so they are equal. Therefore, $\\angle STR = \\angle Q = 35^\\circ$.\n####\nThe answer is $\\angle STR = \\angle Q = \\boxed{35^\\circ}$.', 'Since $\\overline{ST}\\parallel\\overline{QR}$, we know that $\\angle STR$ and $\\angle Q$ are alternate interior angles. Therefore, $\\angle STR = \\angle Q = \\boxed{35^\\circ}$.', "To solve the problem, we need to find the measure of angle $\\angle STR$. Given that $\\overline{ST} \\parallel \\overline{QR}$, we can use the properties of parallel lines and transversals. Here's the step-by-step reasoning:\n\n1. Since $\\overline{ST} \\parallel \\overline{QR}$, angle $\\angle STR$ is equal to angle $\\angle PQR$ because they are corresponding angles.\n2. We know that the sum of the angles in a triangle is $180^\\circ$. Therefore, in triangle $PQR$, we can find $\\angle PQR$ as follows:\n   \\[\n   \\angle PQR = 180^\\circ - \\angle P - \\angle Q = 180^\\circ - 40^\\circ - 35^\\circ = 105^\\circ\n   \\]\n3. Since $\\angle STR = \\angle PQR$, we have:\n   \\[\n   \\angle STR = 105^\\circ\n   \\]\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code to ensure accuracy.\n```python\n# Given angles\nangle_P = 40\nangle_Q = 35\n\n# Calculate angle PQR\nangle_PQR = 180 - angle_P - angle_Q\n\n# Since ST is parallel to QR, angle STR is equal to angle PQR\nangle_STR = angle_PQR\nprint(angle_STR)\n```\n```output\n105\n```\nThe measure of $\\angle STR$ is \\(\\boxed{105}\\) degrees.", "Given the function \\( f(n) \\) defined as:\n\\[\nf(n) = \\left\\{\n\\begin{array}{cl}\n\\lfloor n \\rfloor & \\text{ if }n \\geq 4, \\\\\n\\lceil n \\rceil & \\text{ if } n < 4.\n\\end{array}\n\\right.\n\\]\n\nWe need to find \\( f\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right) + f(\\sqrt{45}) + f(8^{2/3}) \\).\n\nLet's evaluate each term step-by-step:\n\n1. Evaluate \\( f\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right) \\):\n   - Since \\( \\frac{\\pi}{3} \\approx 1.047 \\) and \\( 1.047 < 4 \\), we use \\( f(n) = \\lceil n \\rceil \\).\n   - Therefore, \\( f\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right) = \\lceil 1.047 \\rceil = 2 \\).\n\n2. Evaluate \\( f(\\sqrt{45}) \\):\n   - Since \\( \\sqrt{45} \\approx 6.708 \\) and \\( 6.708 \\geq 4 \\), we use \\( f(n) = \\lfloor n \\rfloor \\).\n   - Therefore, \\( f(\\sqrt{45}) = \\lfloor 6.708 \\rfloor = 6 \\).\n\n3. Evaluate \\( f(8^{2/3}) \\):\n   - Since \\( 8^{2/3} = 4 \\) and \\( 4 \\geq 4 \\), we use \\( f(n) = \\lfloor n \\rfloor \\).\n   - Therefore, \\( f(8^{2/3}) = \\lfloor 4 \\rfloor = 4 \\).\n\nNow, we sum these values:\n\\[\nf\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right) + f(\\sqrt{45}) + f(8^{2/3}) = 2 + 6 + 4 = 12\n\\]\n\nLet's verify this with Python code to ensure accuracy.\n```python\nimport math\r\n\r\n# Define the function f(n)\r\ndef f(n):\r\n    if n >= 4:\r\n        return math.floor(n)\r\n    else:\r\n        return math.ceil(n)\r\n\r\n# Values to evaluate\r\npi_over_3 = math.pi / 3\r\nsqrt_45 = math.sqrt(45)\r\neight_two_thirds = 8 ** (2 / 3)\r\n\r\n# Calculate f for each value\r\nf_pi_over_3 = f(pi_over_3)\r\nf_sqrt_45 = f(sqrt_45)\r\nf_eight_two_thirds = f(eight_two_thirds)\r\n\r\n# Sum the results\r\nresult = f_pi_over_3 + f_sqrt_45 + f_eight_two_thirds\r\nprint(result)\n```\n```output\n12\n```\nThe final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{12}\n\\]", "Let \\[f(n) = \\left\\{\n\\begin{array}{cl}\n\\lfloor n \\rfloor & \\text{ if }n \\geq 4, \\\\\n\\lceil n \\rceil & \\text{ if } n < 4.\n\\end{array}\n\\right.\\]Find $f\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right) + f(\\sqrt{45}) + f(8^{2/3})$. 1. Evaluate \\( f\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right) \\):\n   - Since \\(\\frac{\\pi}{3} \\approx 1.047\\), and \\(\\frac{\