e_k + 1) \\). We need \\( d(n) = 15 \\).\n\nThe possible factorizations of 15 are:\n- \\( 15 = 15 \\times 1 \\)\n- \\( 15 = 5 \\times 3 \\)\n- \\( 15 = 3 \\times 5 \\)\n\nThis means \\( n \\) can have one of the following forms:\n- \\( n = p_1^{14} \\)\n- \\( n = p_1^4 p_2^2 \\)\n\nSince \\( n \\) must be divisible by 15, the smallest possible \\( n \\) is \\( 3^4 \\times 5^2 = 2025 \\). Let's check if this is divisible by 15 and has exactly 15 divisors.\n\n### Problem 2: Calculating the total amount of money in cents after pooling the money from the girl scouts and converting as many coins into bills as possible.\n\nLinda earned \\$27.47, Sherry earned \\$35.23, June earned \\$37.37, and Connie earned \\$26.52. The total amount of money is:\n\\[ 27.47 + 35.23 + 37.37 + 26.52 = 126.59 \\]\n\nConverting this to cents gives \\( 12659 \\) cents. The largest number of dollars that can be formed from 12659 cents is \\( \\left\\lfloor \\frac{12659}{100} \\right\\rfloor = 126 \\) dollars. The remaining cents are \\( 12659 - 126 \\times 100 = 59 \\) cents.\n\n### Problem 3: Calculating the value of a rare stamp in the year 2000, given that it was worth \\$250 in 1989 and increased by 10% each year.\n\nThe value of the stamp in 2000 (11 years later) can be calculated using the formula for compound interest:\n\\[ V = P \\times (1 + r)^t \\]\nwhere \\( P = 250 \\), \\( r = 0.10 \\), and \\( t = 11 \\).\n\nLet's implement this in Python to get the final answers.\n```python\n# Problem 1: Finding the sum of the digits of the smallest positive integer n such that n is divisible by 15 and n has exactly 15 positive integral divisors.\nimport math\n\n# Function to calculate the number of divisors of a number\ndef count_divisors(n):\n    count = 0\n    for i in range(1, int(math.sqrt(n)) + 1):\n        if n % i == 0:\n            if i * i == n:\n                count += 1\n            else:\n                count += 2\n    return count\n\n# Find the smallest n that is divisible by 15 and has exactly 15 divisors\nn = 15\nwhile True:\n    if n % 15 == 0 and count_divisors(n) == 15:\n        break\n    n += 15\n\n# Sum of the digits of n\nsum_of_digits = sum(int(digit) for digit in str(n))\nprint((n, sum_of_digits))\n\n# Problem 2: Calculating the total amount of money in cents after pooling the money from the girl scouts and converting as many coins into bills as possible.\nlinda_earned = 27.47\nsherry_earned = ", 'Linda, Sherry, June, and Connie walked around their neighborhoods selling girl scout cookies. Linda earned $\\$27.47$, Sherry earned $\\$35.23$, June earned $\\$37.37$, and Connie earned $\\$26.52$. After the sale, they pooled their money together and went to the bank to convert the coins into dollars. How much money, in cents, was left after they converted as many of the coins into bills as they could? initWithNibName\nLinda, Sherry, June, and Connie walked around their neighborhoods selling girl scout cookies. Linda earned $\\$27.47$, Sherry earned $\\$35.23$, June earned $\\$37.37$, and Connie earned $\\$26.52$. After the sale, they pooled their money together and went to the bank to convert the coins into dollars. How much money, in cents, was left after they converted as many of the coins into bills as they could?l\nLinda, Sherry, June, and Connie walked around their neighborhoods selling girl scout cookies. Linda earned $\\$27.47$, Sherry earned $\\$35.23$, June earned $\\$37.37$, and Connie earned $\\$26.52$. After the sale, they pooled their money together and went to the bank to convert the coins into dollars. How much money, in cents, was left after they converted as many of the coins into bills as they could?l\nLinda, Sherry, June, and Connie walked around their neighborhoods selling girl scout cookies. Linda earned $\\$27.47$, Sherry earned $\\$35.23$, June earned $\\$37.37$, and Connie earned $\\$26.52$. After the sale, they pooled their money together and went to the bank to convert the coins into dollars. How much money, 