phi) = -\\cos(3x + \\varphi) \\]\n\nUsing the property of the cosine function, $\\cos(-\\theta) = \\cos(\\theta)$, we can rewrite the left-hand side of the equation:\n\\[ \\cos(-3x + \\varphi) = \\cos(3x - \\varphi) \\]\n\nSo the equation becomes:\n\\[ \\cos(3x - \\varphi) = -\\cos(3x + \\varphi) \\]\n\nThe cosine function has the property that $\\cos(\\theta) = -\\cos(\\theta + \\pi)$. Therefore, we can set:\n\\[ 3x - \\varphi = 3x + \\varphi + \\pi + 2k\\pi \\]\nfor some integer $k$. Simplifying this, we get:\n\\[ -\\varphi = \\varphi + \\pi + 2k\\pi \\]\n\\[ -2\\varphi = \\pi + 2k\\pi \\]\n\\[ \\varphi = -\\frac{\\pi}{2} - k\\pi \\]\n\nSince $0 \\leq \\varphi \\leq \\pi$, the only valid value for $k$ is $k = -1$, which gives:\n\\[ \\varphi = -\\frac{\\pi}{2} + \\pi = \\frac{\\pi}{2} \\]\n\nSo the value of $\\varphi$ is $\\frac{\\pi}{2}$. Let's confirm this using Python and SymPy.\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the variable and the function\nx, phi = sp.symbols('x phi')\nf = sp.cos(3*x + phi)\n\n# The condition for the function to be odd: f(-x) = -f(x)\ncondition = sp.Eq(sp.cos(-3*x + phi), -sp.cos(3*x + phi))\n\n# Solve for phi in the interval [0, pi]\nsolution = sp.solveset(condition, phi, domain=sp.Interval(0, sp.pi))\nprint(solution)\n```\n```output\n{pi/2}\n```\nThe value of $\\varphi$ for which the function $f(x) = \\cos(3x + \\varphi)$ is an odd function is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\).\n\nGiven the function $f(x)=2\\sin (ωx+φ)+1(ω > 0,|φ|≤\\dfrac{π}{2})$, the distance between two adjacent intersection points of its graph and the line $y=-1$ is $π$. If $f(x) > 1$ holds true for any $x∈(-\\dfrac{π}{12}, \\dfrac{π}{3})$, then the range of values for $φ$ is ( ).\nA: $[\\dfrac{π}{12}, \\dfrac{π}{6}]$\nB: $[\\dfrac{π}{6}, \\dfrac{π}{2}]$\nC: $[\\dfrac{π}{12}, \\dfrac{π}{3}]$\nD: $[\\dfrac{π}{6}, \\dfrac{π}{3}]$ To solve this problem, let's break it down step-by-step:\n\n1. **Understand the Function and Intersection Points:**\n   The function give