es, primes_with_no_solution))\n```\n```output\n(2, [2])\n```\nThe only prime number \\( p \\) for which the congruence \\( 8x \\equiv 1 \\pmod{p} \\) has no solutions is \\( p = 2 \\). The sum of all such primes is \\(\\boxed{2}\\).", 'There are $n$ people standing in a line, each person has a positive integer number of coins. You are given two positive integers $a$ and $b$ where $1\\leq a<b\\leq n$. You can choose $a$ consecutive people in the line and give one coin to each of them. This can be done as many times as you want. Determine all possible values of $n$ for which you can make everyone have the same number of coins, no matter what the initial distribution of coins is. (Note that $a$ and $b$ are fixed.) Shay\n|p|user\nDetermine all positive integers $n$ such that there exists a permutation $a_1,a_2,\\dots,a_n$ of the numbers $1,2,\\dots,n$ satisfying the following property: if $a_i$ is a multiple of $a_j$ then $i$ is also a multiple of $j$. Shay\n|p|user\nLet $ABC$ be a triangle with incenter $I$. Let $M$ be the midpoint of arc $BC$ not containing $A$ in the circumcircle of $ABC$. Let $E$ be a point on the circumcircle of triangle $ABC$ such that $BE$ is tangent to the circumcircle of $ABC$. Let $D$ be a point on $BC$ such that $ID\\perp BC$. Prove that $EM\\parallel AD$. Shay\n|p|user\nLet $P(x)$ be a polynomial with integer coefficients. Define the sequence $a_0,a_1,a_2,\\dots$ by $a_0=0$ and $$a_{n+1}=P(a_n)$$for all $n\\geq 0$. Suppose that there exists a positive integer $k$ such that $a_k=0$. Prove that $a_1=0$ or $a_2=0$. Shay\n|p|user\nLet $ABC$ be a triangle with incenter $I$. Let $D$ be the point on $BC$ such that $ID\\perp BC$. Let $E$ be the point on $AC$ such that $IE\\perp AC$. Let $F$ be the point on $AB$ such that $IF\\perp AB$. Prove that the circumcircles of triangles $BID$, $CIE$, and $AIF$ have a common point other than $I$. Shay\n|p|user\nLet $a_1,a_2,\\dots$ be a sequence of positive integers such that $a_1=1$ and $$a_{n+1}=a_n^2-a_n+1$