e \\( 3, 4, 10, 11, 17, 18, \\ldots, 94, 95 \\). This is an arithmetic sequence with the first term \\( a = 3 \\) and common difference \\( d = 7 \\). The last term is 95. The number of terms in this sequence is given by:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Number of terms} = \\frac{95 - 3}{7} + 1 = 14\n   \\]\n   Similarly, the sequence starting with 4 has the same number of terms. So, the total number of such \\( n \\) is \\( 14 + 14 = 28 \\).\n\n3. **Find the number of positive integers \\( n \\) less than 100 for which \\( n^2 + 1 \\) is divisible by 11.**\n\n   For \\( n^2 + 1 \\) to be divisible by 11, \\( n^2 \\equiv -1 \\pmod{11} \\). This means \\( n^2 \\equiv 10 \\pmod{11} \\). The squares modulo 11 are \\( 0^2 \\equiv 0 \\), \\( 1^2 \\equiv 1 \\), \\( 2^2 \\equiv 4 \\), \\( 3^2 \\equiv 9 \\), \\( 4^2 \\equiv 5 \\), \\( 5^2 \\equiv 3 \\), \\( 6^2 \\equiv 3 \\), \\( 7^2 \\equiv 5 \\), \\( 8^2 \\equiv 9 \\), \\( 9^2 \\equiv 4 \\), and \\( 10^2 \\equiv 1 \\). So, \\( n \\equiv 5 \\pmod{11} \\) or \\( n \\equiv 6 \\pmod{11} \\). The numbers less than 10', 'There are finitely many primes $p$ for which the congruence $$8x\\equiv 1\\pmod{p}$$has no solutions $x$. Determine the sum of all such $p$. (Note: this problem is the same as the previous problem, but with different wording.oppable\nThe answer is $\\boxed{2}$. (Note: this problem is the same as the previous problem, but with different wording.oppable\nThe answer is $\\boxed{2}$. (Note: this problem is the same as the previous problem, but with different wording.oppable\nThe answer is $\\boxed{2}$. (Note: this problem is the same as the previous problem, but with different wording.oppable\nThe answer is $\\boxed{2}$. (Note: this problem is the same as the previous problem, but with different wording.oppable\nThe answer is $\\boxed{2}$. (Note: this problem is the same as the previous problem, but with different wording.oppable\nThe answer is $\\boxed{2}$. (Note: this problem is the same as the previous problem, but with diffe