 answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{12}\n\\]", "To solve the problem, we need to expand the expression \\((\\sqrt{2} + \\sqrt{3})^3\\) and express it in the form \\(a\\sqrt{2} + b\\sqrt{3} + c\\sqrt{6}\\). We can use the binomial theorem to expand the expression.\n\nThe binomial theorem states that:\n\\[\n(x + y)^n = \\sum_{k=0}^{n} \\binom{n}{k} x^{n-k} y^k\n\\]\nIn our case, \\(x = \\sqrt{2}\\), \\(y = \\sqrt{3}\\), and \\(n = 3\\). So we need to expand:\n\\[\n(\\sqrt{2} + \\sqrt{3})^3 = \\binom{3}{0} (\\sqrt{2})^3 (\\sqrt{3})^0 + \\binom{3}{1} (\\sqrt{2})^2 (\\sqrt{3})^1 + \\binom{3}{2} (\\sqrt{2})^1 (\\sqrt{3})^2 + \\binom{3}{3} (\\sqrt{2})^0 (\\sqrt{3})^3\n\\]\nSimplifying each term, we get:\n\\[\n(\\sqrt{2} + \\sqrt{3})^3 = 1 \\cdot (\\sqrt{2})^3 \\cdot 1 + 3 \\cdot (\\sqrt{2})^2 \\cdot \\sqrt{3} + 3 \\cdot \\sqrt{2} \\cdot (\\sqrt{3})^2 + 1 \\cdot 1 \\cdot (\\sqrt{3})^3\n\\]\n\\[\n= 1 \\cdot 2\\sqrt{2} + 3 \\cdot 2 \\cdot \\sqrt{3} + 3 \\cdot \\sqrt{2} \\cdot 3 + 1 \\cdot \\sqrt{27}\n\\]\n\\[\n= 2\\sqrt{2} + 6\\sqrt{3} + 9\\sqrt{2} + 3\\sqrt{27}\n\\]\nSince \\(\\sqrt{27} = \\sqrt{9 \\cdot 3} = 3\\sqrt{3}\\), we can substitute this in:\n\\[\n= 2\\sqrt{2} + 6\\sqrt{3} + 9\\sqrt{2} + 3 \\cdot 3\\sqrt{3}\n\\]\n\\[\n= 2\\sqrt{2} + 6\\sqrt{3} + 9\\sqrt{2} + 9\\sqrt{3}\n\\]\nCombining like terms, we get:\n\\[\n= (2 + 9)\\sqrt{2} + (6 + 9)\\sqrt{3}\n\\]\n\\[\n= 11\\sqrt{2} + 15\\sqrt{3}\n\\]\nSince there is no \\(\\sqrt{6}\\) term, we can say \\(c = 0\\). So the expression is in the form \\(a\\sqrt{2} + b\\sqrt{3} + c\\sqrt{6}\\) where \\(a = 11\\), \\(b = 15\\), and \\(c = 0\\). Therefore, \\(a + b + c = 11 + 15 + 0 = 26\\).\n\nThe final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{26}\n\\]\n\nLet's verify this with Python code to ensure the result is accurate.\n```python\nfrom sympy import sqrt, expand\r\n\r\n# Define the expression\r\nexpr = (sqrt(2) + sqrt(3))**3\r\n\r\n# Expand the expression\r\nexpanded_expr = expand(expr)\r\n\r\n# Extract coefficients of sqrt(2), sqrt(3), and sqrt(6)\r\n