ch means \\( x > 2 \\).\n2. The expression \\( 2 - \\log(x-2) \\) must be positive for the outer logarithm to be defined. So, \\( 2 - \\log(x-2) > 0 \\), which means \\( \\log(x-2) < 2 \\). Since the base of the logarithm is 10, this translates to \\( x-2 < 10^2 \\), or \\( x-2 < 100 \\), which means \\( x < 102 \\).\n3. The denominator \\( \\log(2 - \\log(x-2)) \\) must not be zero. So, \\( 2 - \\log(x-2) \\neq 1 \\), which means \\( \\log(x-2) \\neq 1 \\). Since the base of the logarithm is 10, this translates to \\( x-2 \\neq 10^1 \\), or \\( x-2 \\neq 10 \\), which means \\( x \\neq 12 \\).\n\nCombining these conditions, we get \\( 2 < x < 102 \\) and \\( x \\neq 12 \\). Therefore, the domain of the function is \\( (2, 12) \\cup (12, 102) \\).\n\nLet's confirm this using Python code.\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the variable and the function\nx = sp.symbols('x')\nf = (2 - x) / sp.log(2 - sp.log(x - 2), 10)\n\n# Find the domain of the function\ndomain = sp.calculus.util.continuous_domain(f, x, sp.S.Reals)\nprint(domain)\n```\n```output\nUnion(Interval.open(2, 12), Interval.open(12, 102))\n```\nThe domain of the function \\( f(x) = \\frac{2-x}{\\log(2-\\log(x-2))} \\) is \\(\\boxed{(2, 12) \\cup (12, 102)}\\).", "To determine the domain of the function \\( f(x) = \\frac{2-x}{\\log(2-\\log(x-2))} \\), we need to identify the values of \\( x \\) for which the function is defined. This involves ensuring that the expression in the denominator is defined and non-zero.\n\n1. The expression \\( \\log(x-2) \\) is defined when \\( x-2 > 0 \\), i.e., \\( x > 2 \\).\n2. The expression \\( 2 - \\log(x-2) \\) must be positive for \\( \\log(2 - \\log(x-2)) \\) to be defined, i.e., \\( 2 - \\log(x-2) > 0 \\). This simplifies to \\( \\log(x-2) < 2 \\), which means \\( x-2 < 10^2 = 100 \\), so \\( x < 102 \\).\n3. The expression \\( \\log(2 - \\log(x-2)) \\) must not be equal to zero to avoid division by zero. So, \\( 2 - \\log(x-2) \\neq 1 \\), which means \\( \\log