stinct values of \\( b \\) that make the "mathematical creature" stable, we need to analyze the quadratic function \\( f(x) = x^2 + bx - 1 \\) and find the conditions under which it intersects the x-axis at exactly two distinct points. This occurs when the quadratic equation \\( x^2 + bx - 1 = 0 \\) has two distinct real roots. The condition for a quadratic equation \\( ax^2 + bx + c = 0 \\) to have two distinct real roots is that its discriminant must be positive. The discriminant \\(\\Delta\\) of the quadratic equation \\( x^2 + bx - 1 = 0 \\) is given by:\n\n\\[\n\\Delta = b^2 - 4ac\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\( a = 1 \\) and \\( c = -1 \\) into the discriminant formula, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\Delta = b^2 - 4(1)(-1) = b^2 + 4\n\\]\n\nFor the quadratic equation to have two distinct real roots, the discriminant must be positive:\n\n\\[\nb^2 + 4 > 0\n\\]\n\nSince \\( b^2 \\) is always non-negative for any real number \\( b \\), \\( b^2 + 4 \\) is always positive. Therefore, the inequality \\( b^2 + 4 > 0 \\) is satisfied for all real numbers \\( b \\). This means that the quadratic equation \\( x^2 + bx - 1 = 0 \\) will always have two distinct real roots for any integer value of \\( b \\).\n\nSince \\( b \\) can be any integer, there are infinitely many distinct integer values for \\( b \\). Therefore, the number of distinct values of \\( b \\) that make the "mathematical creature" stable is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\infty}\n\\]', 'In the diagram, $ABCD$ is a square with side length $1$, and $\\overline{AE}$ is perpendicular to $\\overline{ED}$. If $\\overline{AE}$ and $\\overline{ED}$ are of equal length, what is the area of quadrilateral $AEDB$? (Note: The diagram is not provided, but assume $A$ and $D$ are adjacent vertices of the square, and $E$ is outside the square.) \\boxed{?}\n\\end{enumerate}\n\n\\section*{Problem 1}\nTo find the area of the octagon, we can divide it into simpler shapes. The octagon can be divided into a square and four right-angled triangles. The square has sides of length $1$, so its area is $1^2 = 1$. Each triangle has a base of $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ and a height of $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$, so the area of each triangle is $\\frac{1}{2} \\times \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\times \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} = \\frac{1}{4}$. Since there are four such triangles, their total area is $4 \\times \\frac{1}{4} = 1$. Therefore, the total area of the octagon is the sum of the area of the square and the four triangles, which is $1 + 1 = \\boxed{2}$.\n\n\\section*{Problem 2}\nSince $ABCD$ is a square with side length $1$, the diagonal $AC$ has length $\\sqrt{2}$. Since $\\overline{AE}$ and $\\overline{ED}$ are of equal length and $\\overline{AE}$ is perpendicular to $\\overline{ED}$, $\\triangle AED$ is an isosceles right triangle. The length of $\\overline{AE}$ and $\\overline{ED}$ can be found using the Pythagorean theorem: $AE^2 + ED^2 = AD^2$. Since $AD = 1$, we have $2AE^2 = 1$, so $AE = ED = \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$. The area of $\\triangle AED$ is $\\frac{1}{2} \\times \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\times \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} = \\frac{1}{4}$. The area of the square $ABCD$ is $1$, so the area of quadrilateral $AEDB$ is $1 - \\frac{1}{4} = \\boxed{\\frac{3}{4}}$.', ' An equiangular octagon has four sides of length $1$ and four sides of length $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$, arranged so that no two consecutive sides have the same length. What is the area of the octagon? \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\box