ed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\boxed\n \\', "Let $p$ be a prime number and let $a_1, a_2, a_3, \\ldots, a_{p-1}$ be integers. Prove that if the sum $a_1^p + a_2^p + a_3^p + \\ldots + a_{p-1}^p$ is divisible by $p$, then the sum $a_1 + a_2 + a_3 + \\ldots + a_{p-1}$ is also divisible by $p$. \\boxed{Assistant}\nLet's solve each problem step by step.\n\n### Problem 1: Trigonometry Problem\n\nGiven:\n\\[\n\\sin x = \\frac{3}{5}, \\quad \\cos y = \\frac{4}{5}, \\quad 0 < x, y < \\frac{\\pi}{2}\n\\]\n\nWe need to find \\(\\sin(x + y)\\).\n\nFirst, we find \\(\\cos x\\) and \\(\\sin y\\) using the Pythagorean identity:\n\\[\n\\sin^2 x + \\cos^2 x = 1 \\implies \\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)^2 + \\cos^2 x = 1 \\implies \\frac{9}{25} + \\cos^2 x = 1 \\implies \\cos^2 x = \\frac{16}{25} \\implies \\cos x = \\frac{4}{5}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\sin^2 y + \\cos^2 y = 1 \\implies \\sin^2 y + \\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)^2 = 1 \\implies \\sin^2 y + \\frac{16}{25} = 1 \\implies \\sin^2 y = \\frac{9}{25} \\implies \\sin y = \\frac{3}{5}\n\\]\n\nNow, we use the angle addition formula for sine:\n\\[\n\\sin(x + y) = \\sin x \\cos y + \\cos x \\sin y\n\\]\nSubstitute the known values:\n\\[\n\\sin(x + y) = \\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right) \\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right) + \\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right) \\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right) = \\frac{12}{25} + \\frac{12}{25} = \\frac{24}{25}\n\\]\n\nThus, the answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{24}{25}}\n\\]\n\n### Problem 2: Geometry Problem\n\nGiven:\n- Equiangular octagon with four sides of length \\(1\\) and four sides of length \\(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\).\n- No two consecutive sides have the same length.\n\nWe need to find the area of the octagon.\n\nFirst, note that an equiangular octagon can be divided into a square and four right triangles. The side length of the square is the same as the length of the longer sides of the octagon, which is \\(1\\).\n\nThe right triangles are isosceles with legs of length \\(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\). The hypotenuse of each triangle is the side of the square, which is \\(1\\).\n\nThe area of the square is:\n\\[\n1^2 = 1\n\\]\n\nThe area of one right triangle is:\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{2} \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)^2 = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{2}{4} = \\frac{1}{4}\n\\]\n\nSince there are four such triangles, their total area is:\n\\[\n4 \\cdot \\frac{1}{4} = 1\n\\]\n\nThus, the total area of the octagon is:\n\\[\n1 + 1 = 2\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{2}\n\\]\n\n### Problem 3: Number Theory Problem\n\nGiven:\n- \\(p\\) is a prime number.\n- \\(a_1, a_2, a_3, \\ldots, a_{p-1}\\) are integers.\n- The sum \\(a_1^p + a_2^p + a_3^p + \\ldots + a_{p-1}^p\\) is divisible by \\(p\\).\n\nWe need to prove that the sum \\(a_1 + a_2 + a_3 + \\ldots + a_{p-1}\\) is also divisible by \\(p\\).\n\nBy Fermat's Little Theorem, for any integer \\(a\\) and a prime \\(p\\):\n\\[\na^p \\equiv a \\pmod{p}\n\\]\n\nApplying this theorem to", 'Let $A$ and $B$ be two different, randomly chosen roots of $z^{2018} - 1 = 0$. Find the probability that $\\sqrt{1 + \\sqrt{2}\\text{Re}(AB)}$ is a real number. (Here $\\text{Re}(z)$ denotes the real part of $z$.) \\boxed{}.:mathematician\nA fair coin is flipped $10$ times. What is the probability that at least $5$ consecutive flips come up heads? \\boxed{}.:mathlete\nA box contains $3$ red, $13$ black, and $11$ blue chips. A player draws chips from the box one at a time 